var e={notice:`The data here is/may be copyrighted and NOT included in the GPLv3 license. Converted from MegaMek's unit files (https://github.com/MegaMek/megamek): MegaMek Data (C) 2025-2026 by The MegaMek Team, licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/).`,shared:[{manufacturer:`Field Refit`,primaryFactory:`Various`,communications:`Dalban StarLink Classic`,targeting:`Garret E2b`},{engine:`160 ICE`,armor:`ProTech 4`},`The Kestrel VTOL is a light special operations transport built by the Blackwell Corporation on Outreach for Wolf's Dragoons' Seventh Kommando. Commissioned in 3007 as the third combat vehicle in Blackwell's catalog, the design reflects the Dragoons' unusual access to advanced manufacturing expertise unavailable to most Inner Sphere firms. At a glance the Kestrel appears to be an ordinary combat helicopter, but its true purpose becomes clear only when understood in context: it is not a fighting vehicle but a precision delivery platform, sized for small teams operating far behind enemy lines.

Despite modest armor and minimal armament, the Kestrel became the benchmark for special operations VTOLs in the Inner Sphere. Its reputation rests not on firepower but on subtlety, survivability through stealth, and the near-silent operation of its distinctive five-blade rotor system, which allows it to move through combat zones largely undetected.`,`The Kestrel weighs 25 tons and is powered by a 160-rated internal combustion engine, achieving a cruising speed of 129 kilometers per hour and a flank speed of 194 kilometers per hour. The airframe is almost entirely free of sharp edges, with surfaces curved and flared to reduce radar return. The five-blade main rotor produces only a high-pitched whisper rather than the distinctive chop of conventional helicopters, greatly reducing acoustic detection range.

Navigational capabilities far exceed what one would expect for a vehicle of its class. The Kestrel carries thermal imaging, laser terrain-avoidance systems, and a tru-sync uplink to navigational satellite networks. These systems support nap-of-earth flight in all weather conditions, allowing the craft to thread beneath most aircraft-detection grids with reliable precision. The Dalban StarLink Classic communications system and Garret E2b tracking suite round out a capable electronics package for a unit this size.

The vehicle's most significant weakness is its armament. The two Blackwell B75 machine guns function primarily as crew-defense weapons rather than tools of meaningful combat power. Its ProTech 4 armor is barely adequate, with the heaviest facing able to absorb just a single large laser hit before giving way. The troop compartment presents an additional trade-off: too small for a full platoon but too large for a single squad, limiting flexible use in insertion missions. In the wrong hands the Kestrel is little more than a target. In the right ones, it is exceptional.`,`The Kestrel emerged from the relationship between Wolf's Dragoons and the Blackwell Corporation of Outreach. When the Dragoons arrived in the Inner Sphere, Blackwell was a minor component supplier with little recognition beyond its home system. Their exclusive contract with the mercenary command transformed the company. Working alongside Dragoon technical advisors who brought manufacturing knowledge unavailable to most Inner Sphere firms, Blackwell developed the Kestrel in 3007 as a purpose-built tool for the Seventh Kommando's deep-insertion work. It was the third combat vehicle the company had produced, and from the beginning it represented a departure from the standard combat priorities of the Succession Wars era.

For years the Dragoons maintained a tight grip on the vehicle. Kestrels saw action in virtually every theater of the Inner Sphere, though rarely in any way visible to outside observers. Their role in covert extractions, hostage rescues, and behind-the-lines reconnaissance left almost no documentary trail. The only authenticated ground-level images from the Star League expedition crash site on Celano were reportedly taken by MI5 'Stealthy Fox' operatives using Kestrels to move undetected across Taurian territory.

As combat losses accumulated over decades, a small number of Kestrels fell into other hands. The Dragoons also sold a limited number to allied commands, including MI6's Rabid Foxes special forces, who became known users of the design. By the late Succession Wars period, Kestrels had been sighted in service with a range of factions, though almost always in very small numbers.

The relationship between Blackwell and General Motors deepened over this period. GM assisted in upgrading the Outreach maintenance and production infrastructure and established a secondary production run on New Valencia. That arrangement made the design marginally more available before larger events intervened.

Word of Blake forces struck the Blackwell facilities on Outreach in 3067 at the start of the Jihad, destroying most of the company's manufacturing capacity. That attack ended the Kestrel's production history. Surviving vehicles continued to serve as long as parts and skilled technicians could sustain them. Outreach itself changed hands multiple times during the Jihad and the early Republic of the Sphere era before falling under Capellan influence during the ilClan period. Blackwell never recovered as a producing entity, and General Motors publicly stated it would not restart Kestrel production.`,[{tag:`cargo`,amount:.5,doors:1}],{front:8,left:5,right:5,rear:4,turret:0,rotor:2,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[{tag:`medium-laser`,location:`front`,uuid:`#0`}],`Dalban StarLink Classic`,[{tag:`quirk-difficult-to-maintain`}],[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`,`halfTon`,`feedsWeaponTag`],[`communications-equipment`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`#3`,`front`,null,null],`remote-sensor-dispenser`,`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`feedsWeaponTag`],$v:[[`srm-2`,`front`,`#0`,null],[`ammo-srm-standard`,null,`#1`,`srm-2`]]},{manufacturer:`Aldis Industries`,primaryFactory:`Terra`,communications:`Basix 220`,targeting:`Scope 2000`},`Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/1`,`The Kinnol MBT is a frontline tracked combat vehicle developed for the Republic of the Sphere's Republic Armed Forces. Designed by Aldis Industries and approved for production at their Terra facility in 3083, the tank represents the Republic's effort to field a fast, well-protected standard armor unit for its newly formed military. Named for General Nicolas Kinnol, the third commanding officer of the Star League Defense Force, the design reflects an institutional nod to the Star League legacy that shaped the Republic's founding ideals. Its expensive extralight fusion engine sets it apart from comparable designs, providing the mobility the RAF considered essential for modern combined-arms warfare.`,`The Kinnol's GM 350 extralight fusion engine delivers flanking speeds exceeding 86 km/h, exceptional for a 70-ton tracked vehicle. This speed advantage is the design's most defining tactical asset, allowing Kinnol crews to reposition quickly, exploit flanks, and disengage from unfavorable engagements. The cost is significant, as the XL powerplant divides the vehicle's internal structure and reduces its ability to absorb battle damage without catastrophic loss. A single penetrating hit to the engine section can disable the vehicle entirely.

Twelve tons of StarSlab/1 ferro-fibrous armor, reinforced at the front, provide strong protection against direct fire threats. A CASE system safeguards the crew from ammunition explosions, an important provision given the tank's three-ton missile magazine. The primary weapon is a turret-mounted Mydron Excel LB 10-X autocannon, capable of alternating between solid and cluster rounds to engage both armored and light targets. A coaxial Diverse Optics extended-range medium laser extends effective reach. The hull-mounted pair of Doombud MML-5s adds substantial flexibility, with crews able to load long-range, short-range, semi-guided, or Inferno rounds depending on the tactical situation.

The design's principal weaknesses are its vulnerability to internal damage from the XL engine, its high acquisition cost limiting fielding numbers, and its reliance on ammunition for its best firepower options.`,`Before the Kinnol reached production approval, the Republic conducted a series of covert field trials to validate the design under combat conditions. A lance of pre-production vehicles was dispatched on anti-piracy operations to Astrokaszy and Tortuga. The tanks performed decisively in these engagements, using their speed to flank mixed pirate formations and disable heavier units with concentrated missile and autocannon fire. One vehicle was lost in the fighting, but the strategic results exceeded Republic expectations. Production approval followed in 3083, and Aldis Industries opened the line at their Terra facility that same year.

The Republic distributed the Kinnol broadly among RAF militia formations, prioritizing units expected to see frontline action. The tank's cost prompted the RAF to subsidize sales to mercenary commands under Republic contract as well, keeping allied units equipped while managing production output. As surplus vehicles reached the open market, the Kinnol spread beyond Republic borders, appearing in the armored forces of multiple successor states. Even the Capellan Confederation acquired examples, though political friction between governments frequently delayed contract approvals.

By the Dark Age, the Kinnol's production base had expanded beyond Terra. Avanti Assemblies on Skye, operating under license originally granted by Kressly Warworks, produced the base model for distribution across the former Lyran corridor. Kressly Warworks on Epsilon Eridani also maintained a production line, assumed to have run alongside the Avanti license arrangement. These secondary production sites ensured continued availability of the design even as Terra's political situation became increasingly volatile. The (RAC) variant introduced in 3122 reflected lessons learned from Dark Age combat, addressing demands for higher sustained fire output against the lighter, faster opponents common in that era's conflicts.`,[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`,`feedsWeaponTag`],[`mml-5`,`#2`,`front`,null],[`machine-gun`,`#4`,`front`,null],[`medium-laser`,`#7`,`turret`,null],[`machine-gun`,`#8`,`turret`,null],[`rotary-ac-5`,`#3`,`turret`,null],[`ammo-is-rotary-ac-5-standard`,`#6`,null,`rotary-ac-5`],[`ammo-is-rotary-ac-5-standard`,`#7`,null,`rotary-ac-5`],`ammo-is-rotary-ac-5-standard`,{manufacturer:`Trellshire Heavy Industries|Manufacturing Plant SFF-NG1|Manufacturing Plant SFF-SH2`,primaryFactory:`Twycross|Ingersoll|Shimonita`,communications:`Neil 9000 with Angel ECM Suite`,targeting:`RCA Econotrac with Light TAG and Active Probe`},`Forging AM15 Ferro-Fibrous`,`The Kite Reconnaissance Vehicle is a Clan Sea Fox design introduced in 3105 as a purpose-built hover scout platform marketed to both Inner Sphere militaries and the broader Clan community. Developed to replace aging Odin scout vehicles in the Sea Fox inventory, the Kite was designed for intelligence gathering, target acquisition, and electronic warfare rather than direct combat. The Sea Foxes established production at three geographically dispersed factories spanning Clan and Inner Sphere territory, distributing the vehicle through their merchant networks across multiple theaters. Its combination of speed, sensor capability, and sophisticated electronic countermeasures made it a commercial success, and the Kite became a fixture in reconnaissance elements across numerous militaries throughout the Dark Age and ilClan eras.`,`The Kite's performance centers on its Neil 9000 communications system paired with an Angel ECM suite, giving it exceptional electronic countermeasure capability in contested environments. The RCA Econotrac targeting system, equipped with Light TAG and an Active Probe, enables the crew to mark targets for indirect fire while simultaneously detecting concealed units. Sea Fox testing data indicates the Kite locates hidden units approximately twenty percent faster than comparable Sea Fox scout platforms, and it can track more than a full Trinary of independent contacts with full telemetry for up to five priority targets simultaneously.

The hover drive provides a cruising speed of 97 kph and a maximum of 151 kph, sufficient to outpace most pursuit forces. Two Pattern Alpha SRM 2 launchers and a pair of Tau-II anti-personnel Gauss rifles give the crew a self-defense capability adequate against infantry and light vehicles, though the Kite is not intended for sustained firefights. The Forging AM15 ferro-fibrous armor offers reasonable protection given the vehicle's mass, but the design relies on speed and electronics rather than endurance under fire.

The central design trade-off is the fuel cell powerplant. While quieter and cheaper to maintain than a fusion engine, the Fuel Cell 140 limits operational flexibility in environments where resupply is difficult. The (Fusion) variant addresses this limitation at the cost of greater expense and maintenance complexity, but the standard fuel cell model remains the dominant production version.`,`The Kite emerged from a formal development program approved by the Sea Fox Khan in the late 3090s, driven by growing concern that the Odin scout tank was falling behind Inner Sphere reconnaissance technology. Sea Fox merchants had acquired detailed intelligence on Inner Sphere ECM and sensor developments through commercial transactions during the Republic era, and that intelligence informed a specification calling for a scout vehicle capable of effective operations in both Clan and Inner Sphere theaters.

Production lines were established at three facilities: Trellshire Heavy Industries on Twycross, Manufacturing Plant SFF-NG1 on Ingersoll, and Manufacturing Plant SFF-SH2 on Shimonita. The selection of Twycross was commercially strategic, as the Clan Diamond Sharks had controlled the Trellshire facility for decades and the planet provided an established logistics base for Sea Fox operations in the former Lyran space. The Ingersoll and Shimonita plants extended production into Capellan and Draconis manufacturing networks, giving the Sea Foxes redundancy across multiple political spheres and direct access to major military markets.

The vehicle's combat debut came during the Second Combine-Dominion War in 3110, when Sea Fox forces employed Kite units alongside Arrow IV artillery at the Battle of Mualang. The performance there, with Kite crews maintaining telemetry across simultaneous engagements while disrupting enemy command communications, validated the design's core concept and the Sea Foxes incorporated those after-action accounts into their subsequent marketing campaigns. The battle demonstrated that the Kite could synchronize target acquisition effectively across a mixed-arms force in a way that older scout platforms could not.

In 3144, three Points of Kites featured prominently in the Jade Falcon conquest of Alphecca. Operating against the Hastati Sentinels, the vehicles located defensive positions, disrupted communications, guided Arrow IV barrages, and supported hunter-killer units throughout the campaign. The Falcons drove the Sentinels off-world in under two weeks, and subsequent after-action reporting credited the Kites' force projection contribution as a decisive factor. The engagement reinforced the Kite's reputation across multiple Clan factions and drove additional procurement contracts through Sea Fox merchant channels.

Production of the standard fuel cell variant continued at all three factories through the Dark Age. The (Fusion) variant entered production in 3147 at the same facilities, offering a higher-performance option for commands willing to accept increased maintenance demands. Both variants remained in active production into the ilClan era.`,[{tag:`quirk-improved-sensors`}],`Kite Reconnaissance Vehicle`,{front:25,left:20,right:20,rear:20,turret:20,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`clan-active-probe`,`#0`,null,null],`ammo-clan-ap-gauss-rifle-standard`,[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`],`clan_medium-pulse-laser`,{manufacturer:`Albion Armor Works Beta|Trellshire Heavy Industries`,primaryFactory:`Albion (Junction City)|Twycross`,communications:`Build 1685/3 Tacicom`,targeting:`Build 2 JRD TTS`},`Arcadia Compound Delta VII Ferro-Fibrous`,`The Kokou Defense Tank is a seventy-ton tracked vehicle developed by Clan Star Adder during the vehicular arms race of the 2840s. Albion Armor Works Beta on Albion built the design from salvaged Royal Burke hulls discovered in the Kerensky Cluster, following orders from Khan Clancy Truscott to create an inexpensive garrison vehicle from available Star League legacy materials. Despite a chaotic first production run that left its initial combat performance below expectations, the Kokou recovered to become one of Clan Star Adder's most popular combat vehicles. Its pulse laser armament and ferro-fibrous protection made it a reliable defensive platform, and the design achieved near-ubiquitous distribution across the Star Adder touman before gradually ceding ground to more maneuverable successors. A few Kokous can be found in nearly every Clan touman, traded and dispersed through decades of inter-Clan transfers.`,`Powered by a 140-rated fusion engine, the Kokou inherits its ancestor's emphasis on firepower and protection over speed. Three Kolibri Delta Series Large Pulse Lasers provide accurate, high-rate fire well suited to the static and hull-down defense roles at which the vehicle excels. A Mk. 46 Type II LRM-10 launcher complements the pulse laser battery with area suppression and ranged engagement capability against lighter targets. Arcadia Compound Delta VII ferro-fibrous armor gives the Kokou robust protection appropriate to its role as a garrison support vehicle.

The Build 1685/3 Tacicom communications system and Build 2 JRD TTS targeting and tracking system provide reliable electronic capability for its defensive operations. Like its Burke ancestor, the Kokou performs best in fixed or improved positions where its poor maneuverability is not a liability, and it is frequently imperiled by faster opponents in open maneuvering engagements. Tacticians consistently recommend flanking support and enfilade fire positions when deploying the Kokou, using its excellent rate of fire to punish opponents who commit to crossing its engagement zone rather than exposing it to the kind of fast-moving assaults that quickly overcome its limited agility.`,`The Kokou traces its origin to the Star Adder discovery of Royal Burke vehicle caches in the years following the Pentagon Civil War. Khan Clancy Truscott directed Albion Armor Works Beta to develop a production vehicle from these Star League hulls, seeing in the Royal Burke's armament and protection the foundation of a capable and inexpensive garrison design. The resulting initial production run, designated Block A, was so rushed and error-prone that mechanics and engineers considered it remarkable that the first tank reached operational status at all.

Early combat results did little to vindicate the program. A pair of Clan Mongoose 'Mechs easily defeated four points of Kokous in open engagement, and word spread quickly that the new Adder vehicle lacked credibility as a combat threat. Despite this criticism, the Star Adders continued deploying the Kokou for resource security tasks, and six months later the design's record began to improve dramatically. Kokous accumulated a string of Trial victories against Clan Mongoose, eventually reaching nineteen victories without a single loss in that campaign, decisively reversing the design's reputation within Star Adder sibkos and command circles.

The Hell's Horses later challenged the Star Adders for the Kokou production schematics, bidding a full Star to compete against four Adder points in an open terrain engagement. The Star Commander leading the Hell's Horses force made expert use of the open field to disable opposing transports and pick apart the slower Adder vehicles one at a time, emerging without casualties and securing the schematics for Horse production. The resulting Block B production vehicles extended the Kokou's reach across a second major Clan touman.

The design gradually faded from frontline use as more maneuverable vehicles became available, but it remained a staple of garrison forces across the Clan homeworlds. By the Dark Age, a few Kokous could be found in nearly every Clan touman, reflecting decades of inter-Clan transfers and trading. Rumors in the ilClan era suggested that new production had begun on Twycross, indicating continued demand for the venerable design.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`feedsWeaponTag`],$v:[[`clan-lrm-10`,`front`,`#0`,null],[`clan_large-pulse-laser`,`turret`,`#1`,null],[`clan_large-pulse-laser`,`turret`,`#2`,null],[`clan_large-pulse-laser`,`turret`,`#3`,null],[`ammo-lrm-standard`,null,`#4`,`clan-lrm-10`]]},{manufacturer:`Avalon Arms Company`,primaryFactory:`New Avalon`,communications:`Broadstar Micro-Pulse`,targeting:`Broadstar Pintel-Star`},`The Kruger Combat Car is a ten-ton wheeled scout vehicle with origins stretching back to the Star League era. First produced by the Avalon Arms Company of New Avalon in 2620, the Kruger served as a fast reconnaissance and patrol asset in Federated Suns militia forces throughout the Star League period and into the First Succession War. The design was abandoned when the collapse of high-technology manufacturing made fusion engine production untenable across much of the Inner Sphere, and surviving vehicles passed into museums and private collections over the following decades. Nearly three centuries later, Avalon Arms revived the design in 3091, updating it with modern weaponry and armor while preserving the fundamental qualities that made the original effective. The reintroduced Kruger found ready buyers among militia and planetary defense commands seeking a low-cost, high-speed scout capable of operating effectively in urban environments.`,`The Kruger Combat Car entered production at Avalon Arms Company's New Avalon facility in 2620, serving Federated Suns militia forces during the closing years of the Star League. Its light weight and high speed made it a natural choice for planetary defense commands seeking affordable reconnaissance assets, and the design spread to numerous garrison units along the Federated Suns' rimward and spinward borders. Krugers served alongside heavier armor through the Star League period and remained in production into the opening years of the First Succession War.

The collapse of the Star League shattered the industrial base that sustained fusion engine production. As manufacturers closed and supply chains failed, the Kruger was among the many fusion-powered designs that became impossible to maintain in numbers. By the mid-twenty-eighth century, production had ceased entirely, and surviving vehicles were lost to attrition and battle damage over the following generations. By the height of the Succession Wars, most Krugers had passed into military museums and private collections, remembered as relics of technical sophistication the Inner Sphere had once taken for granted.

Avalon Arms revived the design in 3091, to the considerable surprise of Federated Suns militia procurement officers who had known Krugers only as museum pieces. The reintroduced vehicle incorporated modern technology, replacing the original's standard protection and weaponry with ferro-fibrous armor and extended-range energy weapons while retaining the fundamental wheeled scout configuration. Timing coincided with growing demand for inexpensive garrison assets in the post-Jihad recovery period, and the Kruger found ready buyers among militia commands needing something faster and better-armed than standard patrol vehicles. Avalon Arms also continued offering a simplified version using standard armor and conventional lasers, catering to buyers who lacked the logistical infrastructure to maintain advanced components.

The modern Kruger demonstrated its value in several Dark Age engagements. During the assault on New Demeter City in 3144, Kruger scouts operating for the local militia provided critical situational awareness against Warrior House Ijori forces pushing into the urban center, using their speed and communications range to maintain contact even under heavy electronic jamming. On Dahar IV in 3145, Kruger crews covering for the Dahar Draconis March Militia tracked a Combine reconnaissance force for more than sixty hours in the Harsefield Desert, gathering intelligence that proved decisive to the defense. The vehicle's independent drive wheels allowed sustained operations in terrain that would have stranded conventional wheeled designs.

The fall of New Avalon to Draconis Combine forces in the early ilClan period transferred ownership of the Avalon Arms facility to DC administration, though production of the Kruger line has continued under the new management, supplying both Combine garrison formations and the remaining Federated Suns customers who had previously relied on the design.`,{$k:[`tag`],$v:[[`quirk-power-reverse`],[`quirk-improved-communications`]]},[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`feedsWeaponTag`],`ammo-clan-streak-srm-standard`,`ammo-clan-ultra-ac-10-standard`,[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`feedsWeaponTag`,`halfTon`],[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#2`,null,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,null,`#4`,`machine-gun`,!0],`FireScan with IndirecTrack`,{engine:`InterComBust 180 ICE`,armor:`Simple Armor Plate Standard`},{front:12,left:12,right:12,rear:12,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},{engine:`InterComBust 140 ICE`,armor:`Standard`},{overview:`The Light SRM Carrier and its derivative, the Light Thunderbolt Carrier, are Periphery-designed wheeled combat vehicles built to provide mobile short-range fire support at low cost. Developed by Pinard Protectorates Limited on Perdition for the Taurian Concordat in 3058, the Light SRM Carrier addressed the standard SRM Carrier's limited range and speed by adopting a smaller, faster wheeled chassis with turret-mounted weapons. Protector Jeffrey Calderon opened sales to all Periphery powers, making the design one of the most widely exported Taurian military products.`,capabilities:`The Light SRM Carrier mounts five turret-mounted Shannon Six-Shooter SRM-6 launchers, providing wide-angle engagement capability unavailable to the standard fixed-mount SRM Carrier. An InterComBust 140 internal combustion engine on a forty-ton wheeled chassis provides a top speed of sixty-five kilometers per hour, a significant improvement over the standard carrier's fifty-four. A Communicator system and FireScan with IndirecTrack targeting handle coordination.

The wheeled chassis restricts the vehicle to relatively smooth terrain but provides the speed needed to approach targets, deliver volleys, and retreat before effective counterbattery fire can respond. Seventy-two points of standard armor provide substantially better protection than the standard SRM Carrier's forty-eight. Increased ammunition storage gives each launcher additional volleys. The design uses commonly available parts, making service and repair cheap and easy.`,deployment:`Every major Periphery realm and most lesser Periphery states purchased the Light SRM Carrier in significant quantities after its introduction in 3058. Pinard Protectorates Limited on Perdition and Majesty Metals on Dunianshire served as the primary production sites. The design's cheap parts and easy maintenance made it a perfect support vehicle for planetary militias with limited technical resources.`,battleHistory:`While the Inner Sphere focused on incorporating advanced technology into new vehicle designs, the Periphery states pursued a different strategy: upgrading proven platforms to address known deficiencies. Pinard Protectorates Limited, already the main vehicle supplier for the Taurian Concordat, designed the Light SRM Carrier to fix the standard SRM Carrier's chief weaknesses of limited speed, restricted firing arcs, and paper-thin armor. The new wheeled chassis provided a thirty-three percent speed increase, turret-mounted launchers eliminated the fixed forward-arc limitation, and additional armor improved crew survivability.

The Light SRM Carrier began production in 3058, paired with the Canopian Heavy LRM Carrier in a joint Periphery sales campaign. Protector Calderon's willingness to sell to any interested Periphery realm demonstrated the Taurian commitment to collective Periphery defense. Majesty Metals and Manufacturing on Dunianshire also produced the Light SRM Carrier. Every major Periphery realm acquired the design in significant quantities.

The Light Thunderbolt Carrier emerged in 3072 as a Jihad-era variant, replacing the SRM launchers with five Thunderbolt 5 missile launchers. Quikscell Company on Broken Wheel produced the variant, which was initially developed by the Filtvelt Coalition as a field refit before entering dedicated production. The fusion engine replaced the original ICE powerplant and ferro-fibrous armor was fitted, representing a significant technology upgrade over the base design.`},[`srm-6`,`turret`,`#0`,null],[`srm-6`,`turret`,`#1`,null],[`srm-6`,`turret`,`#2`,null],[`srm-6`,`turret`,`#4`,null],[`ammo-srm-standard`,null,`#5`,`srm-6`],[`ammo-srm-standard`,null,`#6`,`srm-6`],[`ammo-srm-standard`,null,`#7`,`srm-6`],`ammo-is-thunderbolt-5-standard`,{chassis:`Curtiss Industries`,engine:`General Motors GM 210`,armor:`Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/3 Ferro Fibrous`},`The Lightning Attack Hovercraft was commissioned in 2696 by the Star League Defense Force as a light strike vehicle designed to exploit the flanks and rear of enemy formations. Produced by Curtiss Industries on New Stevens, it was conceived as a fast, expendable platform capable of disrupting slower BattleMechs and vehicles through coordinated hit-and-run attacks. Multiple Lightnings were often grouped into temporary compact units tasked with punching through hostile lines, using their exceptional speed to break up formations before heavier assets could engage. The design featured a distinctive low-mounted rear wing that improved stability at high speeds, giving crews greater control during the aggressive maneuvering the vehicle's mission profile demanded. Broadly distributed throughout the SLDF's conventional forces, the Lightning earned a reputation as an effective, if specialized, harassment platform that rewarded bold crews willing to close with much larger adversaries.`,`The Lightning's defining attribute is raw speed. Its GM 210 fusion engine pushes the thirty-five-ton hovercraft to a flank speed exceeding 180 kilometers per hour, making it one of the faster combat vehicles in its weight class. Rear-mounted wings with small thrusters and elevators provide remarkable high-speed stability, and testing has demonstrated the craft can clear obstacles up to two meters high at full throttle. This is an extremely dangerous maneuver, however, and numerous Lightnings have been destroyed along with their crews during failed attempts.

The vehicle's armament is built around a pass-and-fire concept. Two Raker-IV medium pulse lasers in the front allow the crew to engage targets on approach, while a Maxima One Shot SRM-4 rack on each flank delivers a missile volley as the Lightning passes its target. A return pass fires the second rack from the opposite side. This tactic can inflict surprising damage on heavier opponents, particularly when multiple Lightnings coordinate their attack runs. StarSlab/3 ferro-fibrous armor provides reasonable protection for the vehicle's mass, but the one-shot missile launchers represent a significant limitation. Once both racks are expended, the Lightning's offensive capability drops to its pulse lasers alone, and crews must choose their missile targets carefully.`,`Curtiss Industries received the Star League Defense Force contract in the mid-twenty-seventh century to develop a light, fast vehicle capable of breaking up enemy formations ahead of the main battle line. The resulting Lightning entered service in 2696 and was quickly distributed throughout the SLDF's conventional armor regiments. Its high speed and advanced fire-control systems impressed commanders, and the design saw broad deployment across Hegemony and member state garrisons. In 2720, an upgraded Royal variant entered limited production for SLDF Royal units, replacing the expendable one-shot missile systems with more sustainable launchers and adding a target acquisition designator to improve coordination with supporting artillery.

Production at the Curtiss Industries facility on New Stevens continued through the Star League era and into the Early Succession Wars. New Stevens passed from Terran Hegemony control to a brief period of independence before absorption by the Capellan Confederation, but the factory ceased active production before the Late Succession Wars began. The Lightning largely disappeared from battlefield inventories as existing stocks were destroyed or cannibalized for parts during the long technological decline.

The design's revival came under unexpected circumstances. Kressly Warworks on Epsilon Eridani began mass production of the Lightning in August 3067, mere months before the dissolution of the Second Star League. Under Word of Blake patronage, Kressly produced both the original design and a series of new variants tailored to Blakist tactical requirements. The Lightning-RL was conceived by Precentor Robert Havasy specifically to counter Capellan Confederation Shadow Lances operating on Tikonov. Havasy deployed teams of rocket-pod-equipped Lightnings several kilometers from Blakist factory complexes, waiting for stealth-armored BattleMechs to begin their raids before springing coordinated ambushes. In one notable engagement, his crews intercepted a Shadow Lance strike force, crippling a Pillager and a Warhammer before the remaining BattleMechs could withdraw. The surviving raiders attempted to disengage, but the Lightnings reloaded and made a second pass, destroying the rest of the formation.

Following the Jihad, Epsilon Eridani passed to the Republic of the Sphere, and Kressly continued Lightning production under Republic oversight. The factory remained active through the Dark Age, eventually falling under Capellan Confederation control during the ilClan era. ComStar also experimented with the design during the Jihad, producing a limited number of heavily modified CX-3 prototypes with vehicular stealth armor and sponson-mounted rocket launchers, though this variant saw only limited field evaluation.`,`Lightning Attack Hovercraft`,{front:20,left:16,right:16,rear:10,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`medium-pulse-laser`,`front`,`#0`],[`medium-pulse-laser`,`front`,`#1`],{manufacturer:`Kressly Warworks`,primaryFactory:`Epsilon Eridani`,communications:`Century Model 770`,targeting:`TGI 2331C/TGI F-190`},{chassis:`Kressly Warworks`,engine:`General Motors GM 210`,armor:`Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/3 Ferro Fibrous`},[`rocket-launcher-15`,`front`,`#0`],[`rocket-launcher-15`,`front`,`#1`],[`rocket-launcher-15`,`front`,`#2`],[`rocket-launcher-15`,`front`,`#3`],[`rocket-launcher-15`,`front`,`#4`],[`rocket-launcher-15`,`front`,`#5`],[`rocket-launcher-15`,`front`,`#6`],`ammo-is-streak-srm-standard`,[`quirk-narrow-low-profile`],[`ecm-suite`,`#0`,null,null],`Joint Equipment Systems|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|King Dumount Defense Facility|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Aldis Industries|Amaris Armored Vehicles`,`Alshain|Ares|Broken Wheel|Castile|Kalidasa|Layover|Maxwell|Oliver|Pandora|Terra|Timbuktu`,`The LRM Carrier is a sixty-ton tracked combat vehicle designed to deliver massed long-range missile fire at minimal cost. Developed by the Quikscell Company as a modernized successor to primitive Age of War designs dating back to the 2440s, the current production model entered service around 2470 and has remained in continuous use ever since. Manufactured by nearly every major military supplier across the Inner Sphere, the LRM Carrier fills the role of cheap, expendable indirect fire support in armies that cannot afford dedicated artillery assets. Its reputation as a disposable weapons platform belies its effectiveness when properly employed in massed formations, where concentrated volleys of missiles can devastate even heavily armored targets.`,[`ammo-lrm-standard`,null,`#4`,`lrm-20`],[`ammo-lrm-standard`,null,`#5`,`lrm-20`],[`ammo-lrm-standard`,null,`#6`,`lrm-20`],`Lexington Ltd. High Grade`,{front:14,left:14,right:14,rear:11,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`c3-computer-slave`,`#0`,null,null],[`lrm-20-artemis-iv`,`#1`,`front`,null],[`lrm-20-artemis-iv`,`#2`,`front`,null],[`lrm-20-artemis-iv`,`#3`,`front`,null],[`ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv`,`#4`,null,`lrm-20-artemis-iv`],[`ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv`,`#5`,null,`lrm-20-artemis-iv`],[`ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv`,`#6`,null,`lrm-20-artemis-iv`],[`ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv`,`#7`,null,`lrm-20-artemis-iv`],[`ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv`,`#8`,null,`lrm-20-artemis-iv`],[`ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv`,`#9`,null,`lrm-20-artemis-iv`],`quirk-improved-communications`,`ammo-is-magshot-gauss-rifle-standard`,`Killosh Xtra-Weave Ferro-Fibrous`,`The Magi Infantry Support Vehicle was designed by Killosh Industries to fulfill a Star League Defense Force requirement for a dedicated infantry support vehicle. Growing civil unrest in the Periphery territories during the twenty-eighth century demanded a heavier, more capable platform than existing designs could provide. Competition for the contract was fierce, with many expecting the smaller Leopard Armor Prowler to win. The selection of the seventy-ton Magi came as a surprise to military observers, and a subsequent procurement scandal temporarily cast doubt on the vehicle's legitimacy. Despite this rocky start, the Magi proved itself a capable infantry support platform, earning respect for its combination of heavy protection, respectable speed, and reliable performance in counterinsurgency operations.`,`The Magi's most distinctive feature is its turretless design. The low profile makes it a harder target and allows it to navigate urban environments more easily than taller vehicles. Despite weighing seventy tons, the Magna 350 fusion engine propels it to a respectable 86 kilometers per hour, giving it surprising agility for its weight class.

The vehicle carries three Randall Hellbitch medium lasers, with one mounted forward and one on each flank. While the lack of a turret prevents concentrating all weapons on a single target, the advanced Lynx RM targeting system allows a single gunner to engage three separate opponents simultaneously. Two front-mounted Ramsey-65 machine guns provide effective anti-infantry firepower, with their ammunition protected by CASE.

Nearly ten tons of Killosh Xtra-Weave ferro-fibrous armor provides excellent protection, though the armor has a documented flaw. Abrupt maneuvers can cause hairline cracks to form between the plates, potentially increasing damage from subsequent weapon hits. Most crews consider this an acceptable tradeoff given the overall level of protection. The light weaponry relative to its tonnage remains the Magi's most significant shortcoming, limiting its effectiveness against heavier opponents.`,`Killosh Industries began development of the Magi in response to an SLDF solicitation issued in the mid-2720s. The contract drew significant interest, with Leopard Armor's twenty-five-ton Prowler considered the frontrunner. When General Aaron Clavy of the Quartermaster Command announced that Killosh had won the contract, suspicions arose almost immediately. Six months later, investigators discovered that Clavy and Killosh CEO Tempest Halloran had been close friends for years. The resulting scandal halted all testing and triggered an eighteen-month inquiry. When the investigation found no evidence of wrongdoing, testing resumed, and the Magi entered full-scale production in 2727.

The SLDF assigned the Magi to Mechanized Infantry Divisions operating in the Periphery theater. Under attack from increasingly well-equipped and organized insurgents, League troops found the heavily armored Magi to be a valuable asset. The vehicle fared less well during the campaign to liberate the Terran Hegemony from Rim Worlds occupiers. Adequate firepower for battling paramilitary forces in the Taurian Concordat proved woefully inadequate against the entrenched forces defending Terra. Despite its capable performance in the Periphery, the Magi suffered from poor sales, and Killosh Industries filed for bankruptcy prior to the fall of the first Star League.

The Magi faded into obscurity during the Succession Wars as surviving examples were lost or scrapped. Centuries later, ComStar's Com Guards recovered and restored a number of these vehicles, incorporating them into combined-arms formations. The Magi proved well-suited to the Guards' doctrine, providing effective support to infantry facing Clan Elementals despite being unable to match Clan OmniMechs directly. Following the ComStar Schism, many of the vehicles passed into Word of Blake hands. The Blakists deployed updated versions across their Protectorate worlds, where the Magi became a common sight in Blake Protectorate Militia units tasked with urban peacekeeping.`,`Magi Infantry Support Vehicle`,{front:44,left:43,right:43,rear:40,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`machine-gun`,`#2`,`front`,null],[`case`,`#0`,null,null,null],{manufacturer:`Brooks Incorporated|Brooks Incorporated`,primaryFactory:`Irian|Kendall`,communications:`Garret T21-C`,targeting:`Corean B-Tech`},{engine:`GM 120 Fusion`,armor:`Jolassa 320`},`Brooks Incorporated designed the Main Gauche as a fire support companion to their popular Galleon light tank, intended to address the Free Worlds League Military's need for an affordable vehicle with genuine long-range striking power. Development proved protracted, with the design not entering trials until 3062, but the League General Armaments Office recognized the tank's potential and authorized a limited production run for field evaluation. After two years of testing and refinement with frontline units, the LGAO certified the Main Gauche for full-scale production in 3064. Though modest in speed and protection compared to heavier designs, the tank earned a reputation as an effective standoff platform capable of contributing meaningful firepower from positions well behind the main line of engagement.`,`The Main Gauche mounts an Imperator Type-II Light Gauss Rifle as its primary armament, an Atreus-produced weapon capable of delivering accurate fire out to 750 meters. The rifle gives the tank genuine long-range punch disproportionate to its thirty-ton weight class, though its ammunition supply is limited to a single ton. A pair of Voelkers 200 machine guns provide token close-in defense against infantry and soft targets, but offer no credible deterrent against armored opponents.

The GM 120 fusion power plant drives the tracked chassis to a top speed of 65 kilometers per hour, a figure that critics have consistently called inadequate for a vehicle operating in the fire support role. The small engine does, however, leave room for the oversized main gun. Five tons of Jolassa 320 crystal steel armor provides reasonable protection for the tank's weight class, though the absence of a turret forces crews to orient the entire hull to engage targets. This fixed-forward configuration, combined with thin side and rear plating, makes the Main Gauche vulnerable to flanking attacks and forces it to rely on companion vehicles for close protection.`,`The Main Gauche emerged from the Free Worlds League Military's search for a successor or complement to the aging Galleon light tank during the late 3040s. Sales of the Galleon had declined sharply following the Clan Invasion, as its short-range weapon loadout proved increasingly inadequate against opponents fielding advanced technology. In the wake of Operation Bulldog, the FWLM formally solicited proposals for a new light vehicle with extended-range firepower. Brooks Incorporated, developers of the original Galleon, submitted the Main Gauche design.

Development proceeded slowly. The prototype did not reach competitive trials until 3062, and the League General Armaments Office initially authorized only a limited production run for field evaluation with several frontline formations. Two years of operational testing identified minor issues that Brooks addressed before receiving certification for full-scale manufacturing in 3064. Production was established at Brooks facilities on both Irian and Kendall.

The FWLM initially restricted exports of the Main Gauche, keeping the design exclusively for League service. The Free Worlds Guard and Free Worlds Legionnaires received the largest initial allocations, though the tank also filtered into various provincial militias across the League. The Word of Blake obtained a small number of vehicles, reportedly several dozen, which deployed with the Second Division. Within three years of entering service, Brooks had already developed multiple variant configurations to expand the design's tactical flexibility, reflecting strong institutional demand for the platform despite its acknowledged limitations in speed and close-quarters survivability.`,`Main Gauche Light Support Tank`,{front:29,left:19,right:19,rear:13,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},`ammo-is-light-gauss-rifle-standard`,[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`halfTon`,`feedsWeaponTag`],`ammo-is-light-machine-gun-standard`,`ammo-is-plasma-rifle-standard`,[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`,`feedsWeaponTag`,`halfTon`],{manufacturer:`StarCorps Industries|StarCorps Industries|StarCorps Industries|Arcturan Arms`,primaryFactory:`Crofton|Loburg|Son Hoa|Buena`,communications:`O/P COMSET 3`,targeting:`O/P 2550`},{engine:`Magna 350 XL`,armor:`Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/3 Ferro-Fibrous`},`StarCorps Industries introduced the Manteuffel Attack Tank in 3062 in response to an Armed Forces of the Federated Commonwealth requirement for OmniVehicle designs. Named after a distinguished line of Lyran armor generals, the Manteuffel was StarCorps' first foray into military vehicle production, as the company had previously built only BattleMechs. Despite competing against multiple submissions, the design won the contract decisively, fulfilling all official military requirements. The seventy-ton tracked OmniVehicle combined heavy firepower with enough speed to keep pace with frontline combined-arms formations, earning it a reputation as one of the most capable armored vehicles fielded by the successor states of the former Federated Commonwealth. Its high unit cost and advanced technology base restricted distribution to elite formations, making the Manteuffel a prestige assignment for crews fortunate enough to operate one.`,`The Manteuffel's Magna 350 XL fusion engine drives the seventy-ton vehicle to a top speed of 86 km/h, fast enough to keep pace with most medium BattleMech formations. StarSlab/3 ferro-fibrous armor provides eleven and a half tons of protection, enhanced by CASE to contain ammunition explosions. The vehicle dedicates 23.5 tons of pod space to mission-configurable weaponry, with fifteen tons available in the turret and the remainder distributed across hull mounting points.

The Prime configuration pairs a Rotary AC/5 with three extended-range medium lasers, delivering devastating burst damage at medium range. A Guardian ECM suite disrupts enemy electronic warfare systems, while a C3 slave module allows the Manteuffel to share targeting data across a networked lance. This combination makes the Prime a potent force multiplier in coordinated engagements. The XL engine stands as a significant vulnerability, as penetrating hits to either side can disable the vehicle entirely. The reliance on ammunition-fed weapons in most configurations also limits sustained endurance without resupply, and crews must manage their stores carefully during extended operations.`,`When the AFFC issued its requirement for OmniVehicle designs in the early 3060s, procurement officials were simultaneously rejecting nearly every OmniMech submission while actively courting manufacturers to produce OmniVehicles instead. The reasoning was financial. Omni-technology equipped vehicles cost approximately the same to produce as their BattleMech equivalents, but the budget allocation for new military vehicles came from a different account than that for BattleMechs. StarCorps Industries, sensing an opportunity, assembled a dedicated military vehicle design team despite having no prior experience in vehicle production. The company produced a working prototype in record time. While the Manteuffel officially won the competition on its merits, persistent rumors suggest that StarCorps leveraged its extensive political connections within the AFFC procurement hierarchy. More than a dozen former AFFC and LAAF officers involved in the selection decision subsequently accepted directorships within StarCorps.

Production began at StarCorps facilities on Crofton, Loburg, and Son Hoa, with all three plants manufacturing the Manteuffel for distribution across both the Lyran Alliance and Federated Suns. The Word of Blake's assault on Son Hoa in April 3074 devastated that facility. Chemical weapons killed the StarCorps Board of Directors during a meeting at the complex, and repeated aerospace and DropShip strikes left the plant inoperable. The loss of Son Hoa reduced Manteuffel production capacity significantly during the Jihad, though the Crofton and Loburg plants continued operations throughout the conflict. Arcturan Arms on Buena joined the production network during the Civil War era, providing additional Lyran Alliance output. The Manteuffel remained in continuous production through the Dark Age and into the ilClan era, with StarCorps and Arcturan Arms maintaining active lines to supply both Lyran and Davion forces.`,{front:56,left:40,right:40,rear:25,turret:45,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`tag`,`uuid`,`pod`,`location`,`feedsWeaponTag`],[`case`,`#1`,null,null,null],[`medium-laser`,`#3`,!0,`front`,null],`ammo-is-ultra-ac-20-standard`,`The Manteuffel's Magna 350 XL fusion engine drives the seventy-ton vehicle to a top speed of 86 km/h, fast enough to keep pace with most medium BattleMech formations. StarSlab/3 ferro-fibrous armor provides eleven and a half tons of protection, enhanced by CASE to contain ammunition explosions. The vehicle dedicates 23.5 tons of pod space to mission-configurable weaponry, with fifteen tons available in the turret and the remainder distributed across hull mounting points.

The Prime configuration pairs a Rotary AC/5 with three extended-range medium lasers, delivering devastating burst damage at medium range. A Guardian ECM suite disrupts enemy electronic warfare systems, while a C3 slave module allows the Manteuffel to share targeting data across a networked lance. This combination makes the Prime a potent force multiplier in coordinated engagements. The XL engine serves as a significant vulnerability, as penetrating hits to either side can disable the vehicle entirely. The reliance on ammunition-fed weapons in most configurations also limits sustained endurance without resupply, and crews must manage their stores carefully during extended operations.`,`ammo-is-gauss-rifle-standard`,[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`,`pod`,`feedsWeaponTag`],[`ecm-suite`,`#0`,!0,null,null],`ammo-is-lb-10x-standard`,[`ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster`,`#8`,null,null,`autocannon-lbx-10`],`ammo-is-mech-taser-standard`,`The Manticore is one of the most widely produced and respected heavy tanks in the Inner Sphere, originally designed by TechniCorp as an infantry support vehicle capable of engaging BattleMechs on favorable terms. Introduced during the Reunification War in 2575, the sixty-ton tracked vehicle was built to fill the gap between lighter screening vehicles and the massive assault tanks that dominated battlefield formations. Its combination of long-range fire support, close-range punch, and respectable armor protection made it an attractive option for military planners across the Star League. After TechniCorp's destruction during the Amaris Coup, the design's production licenses spread to manufacturers across the Inner Sphere and beyond. The Manticore remains in frontline service with nearly every major military power, a remarkable achievement for a design approaching five centuries of continuous production.`,`The Manticore's primary weapon is the Parti-Kill Heavy Cannon particle projection cannon, which uses magnetic collection bottles to charge plasma that loses cohesion and disintegrates targets at ranges approaching 540 meters. A FarFire LRM-10 rack mounted on independent swivel mounts above the turret provides supplementary long-range fire with a full 120-degree arc independent of the main turret. At closer ranges, the SureShot Mk VI SRM-6 launcher and hull-mounted OMI HighBurn medium laser round out the weapon array, giving the Manticore credible firepower at every engagement distance.

The Pitban 240 fusion engine gives the tank a respectable cruise speed of 43 km/h and a top speed of 65 km/h, a significant advantage over vehicles reliant on internal combustion engines during the Succession Wars when fuel supplies were unreliable. Eleven tons of ArcShield Maxi II armor provide solid protection for a vehicle of its tonnage. The Manticore's chief weakness is that it lacks the concentrated firepower of heavier vehicles such as the Behemoth and Demolisher, making it a poor choice for head-on engagements with assault-class opponents. Manticore crews are trained to avoid such confrontations, leveraging their mobility and range to control engagements on their own terms.`,`TechniCorp developed the Manticore during the Reunification War for the Star League member states, creating an infantry support weapon that could hold its own against BattleMechs in the right circumstances. The design proved so successful that production continued uninterrupted through the Star League era, with TechniCorp's Terra-based headquarters overseeing a network of licensed manufacturers across the Inner Sphere.

The Amaris Coup proved catastrophic for TechniCorp. When Stefan Amaris seized Terra, TechniCorp's head offices were destroyed along with their primary production facility. Rather than ending the Manticore's story, this event scattered the design across the Inner Sphere as surviving subsidiary manufacturers absorbed the production technology. Defiance Industries on Hesperus II, Jalastar Aerospace on Panpour, Quikscell Company on Ares, and several other firms continued building the tank independently.

The Succession Wars tested the Manticore's design philosophy. While most combat vehicles relied on internal combustion engines that were cheaper and easier to maintain, the Manticore's fusion engine demanded more resources. This made each tank expensive to produce and difficult to repair, but the fusion engine's reliability and the elimination of fuel logistics concerns proved invaluable during prolonged campaigns. Commanders who could afford Manticores found them worth every C-bill, and the tank's popularity endured even as lighter and cheaper alternatives flooded the battlefield.

Licensing the vehicle to companies across multiple states spread the Manticore's reputation to the farthest corners of the Inner Sphere and parts of the Periphery. Defiance Industries drew criticism when it started Manticore production on Proserpina through its relationship with Bulldog Enterprises, effectively arming the Draconis Combine. Defiance defended the decision by arguing that the Combine's weapons manufacturer, Bulldog Enterprises, was already attempting to clone the vehicle for House Kurita. It was better for the Commonwealth to control the licensing and profit from the arrangement.

The technological renaissance of the 3050s brought significant changes to the Manticore family. Defiance Industries developed the 3055 Upgrade, replacing the original particle cannon with a large pulse laser and adding ferro-fibrous armor while retaining the basic chassis and engine to allow easy field conversion of existing vehicles. The upgrade's focus on improved accuracy through fire-control systems rather than raw firepower reflected the changing nature of combined-arms warfare. Both Allied and Loyalist forces fielded the upgraded design during the FedCom Civil War, and many upgrade kits were imported into the Federated Commonwealth.

Bulldog Industries on Proserpina introduced C3-equipped variants in 3063, integrating the Combine's networking technology into the Manticore chassis. The New Earth Trading Company developed the LB-X variant using unproduced Defiance plans, mounting an autocannon effective against battle armor during the Civil War. Jalastar Aerospace followed with an experimental rotary autocannon variant in 3069, and Bulldog developed the Heavy PPC variant during the Jihad in 3074, selling the schematics to Quikscell and Jalastar to broaden production.

The Manticore's enduring popularity led Defiance Industries to develop the Manticore II in 3112, a heavier seventy-ton design optimized for amphibious operations. Drawing on that design's principles, Defiance then licensed the Manticore XL back to the original production network in 3117. This lighter variant incorporated an extra-light engine and a targeting computer, updating the classic chassis for modern warfare while maintaining the broad manufacturing base that had sustained the Manticore for centuries. In the Magistracy of Canopus, Majesty Metals and Manufacturing maintained continuous Manticore production from the Star League through the ilClan period, an unbroken run that few other factories could claim.`,[`standard-ppc`,`turret`,`#3`,null],`TharHes Targitrack with Artemis`,{engine:`Pitban 240 Fusion`,armor:`Durallex Super Ferro-Fibrous`},{front:43,left:34,right:34,rear:26,turret:42,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`streak-srm-2`,`front`,`#0`,null],{manufacturer:`Bulldog Enterprises`,primaryFactory:`Proserpina`,communications:`TharHes Muse 54-58K`,targeting:`TharHes Targitrack with Artemis`},{front:44,left:38,right:38,rear:26,turret:42,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`c3-computer-master`,`#0`,null,null],[`standard-ppc`,`#2`,`turret`,null],[`ammo-is-streak-srm-standard`,null,`#7`,`streak-srm-2`],`Durallex Super Ferro-Fibrous`,[`targeting-computer`,`#0`,null,null],[`is-ams`,`#3`,`turret`,null],[`ammo-is-ams-standard`,`#6`,null,`is-ams`],[`autocannon-lbx-10`,`turret`,`#1`,null],[`ammo-is-lb-10x-standard`,null,`#4`,`autocannon-lbx-10`],[`ammo-is-rotary-ac-5-standard`,`#5`,null,`rotary-ac-5`],`ammo-is-enhanced-lrm-standard`,[`ammo-is-streak-srm-standard`,`#7`,null,`streak-srm-6`],`ammo-clan-improved-lrm-standard`,{manufacturer:`Michaelson Heavy Industries`,primaryFactory:`Ruchbah`,communications:`Garret Supremesound`,targeting:`Garret D2j`},`Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/3`,`The Mantis is a light attack VTOL produced by Michaelson Heavy Industries, one of the Inner Sphere's most prolific rotary-wing combat vehicle manufacturers. Designed at their Ruchbah facility, the Mantis was developed to fill a fast-attack cavalry role, combining exceptional speed with concentrated laser firepower. The design emerged from Michaelson's growing trade relationships with Free Worlds League weapons manufacturers, allowing the Federated Suns company to equip the helicopter with imported advanced laser technology. Building on the company's strong reputation for producing effective VTOL designs, the Mantis represented a new direction for Michaelson, moving beyond traditional ballistic and missile armament into energy-weapon-focused platforms. Entering production in 3061 after an extended prototype phase, the Mantis quickly proved its value as a high-speed strike helicopter capable of punching well above its modest fifteen-ton weight class.`,`The Mantis mounts five Diverse Optics Sunbeam extended-range small lasers, providing devastating firepower for a vehicle of its size. This battery delivers nearly twice the energy output of most helicopters twice its weight, and the ammunition-independent nature of the laser array allows the Mantis to maintain sustained operations without resupply concerns. A Galas 70 micro-fusion engine propels the helicopter to a maximum speed of 184 kilometers per hour, enabling it to outrun most ground vehicles and even many BattleMechs on the battlefield.

The Mantis also carries a Beagle Active Probe and Guardian ECM Suite, giving it significant electronic warfare and reconnaissance capability beyond its primary strike role. Two and a half tons of StarSlab/3 armor provides impressive protection for a fifteen-ton VTOL, and Michaelson engineers deliberately reinforced the nose section to absorb direct hits from even Clan extended-range particle projector cannons. The primary trade-off is the helicopter's vulnerability to sustained fire, a limitation common to all VTOLs that relies on speed and agility rather than raw durability for survival.`,`Mantis Light Attack VTOL`,[`c3-computer-slave`,`#0`,null],`The Marksman Artillery Vehicle is a 65-ton tracked self-propelled artillery platform developed by Grumman Industries in 2702 as a companion design to the Thor Artillery Vehicle. Intended to provide heavy indirect fire support at the battalion and regimental level, the Marksman drew on Grumman's experience with tracked chassis to deliver a more stable, better-protected artillery platform than the wheeled Thor at the cost of some strategic mobility. The design paired its primary Sniper artillery piece with a direct-fire laser, a combination that earned mixed reviews from commanders who received it. Despite persistent criticism of its thermal management and its role ambiguity, Grumman produced the vehicle in substantial numbers during the Succession Wars, distributing it widely across Lyran Commonwealth artillery formations. The merger that created GrummanCRI later brought the design back to active development, and a modernized variant entered Lyran Alliance service during the Jihad era.`,`The Marksman's primary weapon is a hull-mounted Sniper artillery piece, providing the vehicle with indirect fire capability at extended ranges. A turret-mounted large laser supplements the artillery, giving the crew a direct-fire option intended for point defense when enemy units close to engagement range. The dual-system configuration reflects an unusual design philosophy for an artillery platform, and it introduced significant engineering complications. The GRD series of tactical computers that manage both weapons lack adequate thermal dissipation, forcing crews to reduce processor speeds during sustained fire missions. This limitation restricts the number of valid targeting solutions the system can generate and prevents the crew from exploiting both weapons simultaneously at full effectiveness.

The Marksman's ferro-fibrous armor provides reasonable protection for an artillery vehicle operating from defilade positions, but the design was never intended for direct combat, and crews who allow enemy forces to close freely accept serious risk. The tracked suspension delivers better cross-country stability than the wheeled Thor, reducing dispersion during fire missions, though the vehicle's modest road speed limits how quickly it can displace between positions. The large laser's point defense role is undermined by the same cooling problems that affect the targeting computer, and experienced Marksman crews treat the vehicle primarily as an indirect fire platform, using the laser only as a last resort in self-defense.`,`Grumman Industries introduced the Marksman in 2702, drawing from their experience with the Thor Artillery Vehicle to develop what they positioned as a more capable fire support alternative. Where the Thor relied on a wheeled suspension for strategic mobility, Grumman shifted the Marksman to tracked running gear, sacrificing speed for improved stability and additional armor capacity. The tracked chassis reduced dispersion on rough terrain and allowed the design to carry heavier armor protection, addressing a persistent criticism of the Thor in environments where enemy counterbattery fire was active. Grumman established production at facilities on New Earth and Terra under Terran Hegemony patronage, with output distributed to Star League Defense Force formations and Lyran Commonwealth clients.

The Marksman's operational record during the Star League era was adequate, though artillery commanders consistently noted the laser's cooling problems and questioned whether its weight could have been better invested in ammunition capacity. Production at the New Earth facility continued into the early Succession Wars under independent and then Lyran ownership before the facility was presumably lost to the grinding attrition of the conflict. The Terra line likewise fell silent as the First Succession War restructured Inner Sphere industry. During the Second and Third Succession Wars, some production continued at Lyran-controlled facilities, and the Marksman became a familiar second-echelon vehicle in Commonwealth artillery batteries. Most examples spent the conflict supporting infantry advances from concealed positions, a role in which the laser's cooling problems mattered less than in the intensive fire-support operations for which the vehicle had originally been designed.

The merger of Grumman Industries with CRI Mechanical to form GrummanCRI gave the Marksman an unexpected second life. Engineers at the Melissia facility identified the GRD computer's thermal management as the root cause of the vehicle's battlefield limitations and began a comprehensive revision of the platform. The resulting modernization, introduced in 3068, eliminated the outdated tracking lock system along with the original chassis suspension and replaced the standard engine and armor with lighter equivalents. The freed tonnage supported a new turret configuration and resolved the cooling problems that had plagued the original. The timing proved significant, as Lyran Alliance forces facing Clan Jade Falcon in the Melissia Theater gained a more capable fire support vehicle at a moment when reliable artillery was in short supply.`,`Marksman Artillery Vehicle`,[`sniper-artillery`,`front`,`#0`,null],`prototype-rocket-launcher-10`,`prototype-rocket-launcher-15`,[`ammo-ac-20-standard`,null,`#7`,`autocannon-standard-d`],[`ammo-ac-20-standard`,null,`#8`,`autocannon-standard-d`],`ammo-is-heavy-machine-gun-standard`,{manufacturer:`Pandora 'Mech Works`,primaryFactory:`Terra`,communications:`COMTEC 400E`,targeting:`GroundTracker EE-4`},`The Marksman Main Battle Tank emerged from a competitive procurement process launched by the Republic of the Sphere Armed Forces in the early thirty-second century. Pandora 'Mech Works of Terra entered the bidding not in response to an explicit contract solicitation, but by submitting an assault tank design that was already undergoing internal development and trials. The RAF had previously selected the Kinnol as its light main battle tank, passing over Pandora's lighter Winston Combat Vehicle. Rather than withdraw from the market, Pandora moved quickly and placed its heavier M-series design before the RAF's Quartermaster office before a formal open bid had even been announced. The RAF designated the winning design the Marksman, a name coincidentally shared by an earlier Star League artillery vehicle. Pandora subsequently released a cost-reduced variant for the broader market, extending the chassis beyond its original Protectorate role.`,`The Marksman M1A rides on a ninety-five-ton tracked chassis powered by an Edasich Motors 285 Light Fusion engine, reaching a top speed of fifty-four kilometers per hour. The light engine's reduced weight footprint frees up mass for combat systems, though it carries a substantial cost premium over conventional powerplants. Protection is provided by fifteen and a half tons of AmberStar Weave heavy ferro-fibrous armor backed by a chassis-mounted CASE system, offering strong protection against conventional fire while containing ammunition cook-off risks.

The primary weapon is an M-7 Gauss Rifle, capable of delivering precision strikes at long range with minimal heat buildup. Two Holly Multi-Missile Launcher nines extend the tank's engagement envelope across ranges, providing flexibility against armored and unarmored targets alike. Four Krieger C1 Series medium lasers, adapted from the Cyclops Eye design used on the Drillson Hover Tank, supply reliable close-range backup firepower. Four MainFire Miniguns round out the weapon suite for anti-infantry and light vehicle work.

The Marksman M1A's principal weaknesses are cost and ground speed. The combination of a light fusion engine, heavy ferro-fibrous armor, and a high-tier weapons package makes this vehicle expensive to procure and maintain. At fifty-four kilometers per hour, the Marksman is slower than many lighter competitors, reducing its ability to reposition under fire or exploit breakthroughs in fluid engagements.`,`The Marksman's origins lie in Pandora 'Mech Works' broader vehicle development efforts on Terra. The company had invested in a lighter tracked design, the Winston Combat Vehicle, as a contender for the RAF's light main battle tank contract. When the Kinnol secured that award, Pandora was left with a mature development program and no buyer. Rather than shelving the work, the company pivoted to a heavier design and presented it to the RAF before a formal open bid for an assault main battle tank had been announced.

The RAF reviewed proposals from multiple manufacturers but selected the Marksman. Pandora's head start, with operational prototypes already undergoing internal testing, gave the company a meaningful advantage in both timeline and demonstrated readiness. Initial field trials were conducted at the X-5 Test Ranges in the Outback of Terra's Australian continent. In simulated combined-arms engagements, Marksman M1A tanks supported by infantry and lighter vehicles demonstrated an ability to engage and destroy heavy BattleMechs and armored vehicles. Early suspension concerns were noted, but full Protectorate deployment was projected for 3088, with planners targeting at least one lance of Marksmans on each Protectorate world by 3095.

Concurrently with M1A development, Pandora released the M1 to the general market. The M1 reduced acquisition cost by substituting a standard engine for the light fusion powerplant and ferro-fibrous armor for the heavier ferro system. The laser battery was removed, and the multi-missile launchers were replaced with a short-range missile package. The CASE system was also omitted, with designers noting that the ammunition would likely be depleted before the armor was fully compromised. First deliveries of the M1 were projected for late 3088, aimed primarily at planetary militias and independent operators with limited procurement budgets.

In 3128, Jake's Discount Equipment Warehouse introduced the M1J, a refit of the M1 built around an engine swap that freed tonnage for the installation of larger MRM launchers. JDEW operates as an independent wholesaler, and the M1J spread through third-party distribution channels, reaching operators outside the primary RAF and Protectorate supply networks. The M1J demonstrated the underlying flexibility of the Marksman chassis, which proved capable of accommodating significant propulsion changes without compromising the vehicle's structural integrity.`,[`standard-gauss-rifle`,`turret`,`#0`,null,null],[`srm-6`,`turret`,`#3`,null,null],[`srm-6`,`turret`,`#4`,null,null],[`streak-srm-4`,`turret`,`#5`,null,null],[`streak-srm-4`,`turret`,`#6`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#7`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#8`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#9`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#10`,null,null],[`medium-laser`,`#4`,`turret`,null],[`ammo-is-gauss-rifle-standard`,`#12`,null,`standard-gauss-rifle`],[`ammo-is-gauss-rifle-standard`,`#13`,null,`standard-gauss-rifle`],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,`#18`,null,`machine-gun`],`Series XXXII Multitrack IWS`,{engine:`200`,armor:`Compound Zeta Ferro-Fibrous`},`The Mars Assault Vehicle is one of the most formidable combat vehicles the Clans field. Developed in the 2840s as part of ilKhan Jerome Winson's pro-vehicle initiative, it entered service in 2871 and quickly became a fixture in Clan garrison clusters across the Homeworlds. Named for the Roman god of war, the Mars is a hundred-ton tracked assault vehicle designed to fill the same battlefield role as the heaviest BattleMechs without the extensive training demands of a MechWarrior crew. Clan Hell's Horses, whose enthusiasm for conventional forces is well documented, maintained the most consistent production record and pioneered several variant configurations. The vehicle's combination of heavy armor, a potent ECM suite, and a large-bore weapons array has kept it relevant across multiple eras of Clan warfare.`,`The Mars mounts eleven and a half tons of Compound Zeta ferro-fibrous armor, enough to absorb punishment from all but the heaviest weapons. An ECM Suite degrades enemy electronics within range, complicating targeting solutions for opposing units and partially compensating for the vehicle's limited mobility. These defensive advantages come at a price. A Fusion 200 power plant limits the Mars to a top speed of 32 km/h, making it dependent on terrain and friendly screening forces to avoid being outflanked by faster opponents.

The weapons array is designed for long-range engagement. A CRG Gauss Rifle and Series 7N Extended Range Large Laser occupy the turret, providing accurate fire at distances where most opponents cannot effectively retaliate. A Type KOV LB-10X Autocannon adds versatility, particularly against aircraft and lightly armored vehicles using cluster munitions. Three Type XV Crossbow LRM-15 launchers, each slaved to an Artemis IV fire control system, deliver sustained indirect fire with improved hit probability. Two Pattern J6c Streak SRM-6 launchers protect the flanks against close-range threats, ensuring that opponents cannot simply maneuver around the main weapons to exploit weaknesses. A pair of Series IX machine guns handles unarmored infantry. The weapons array consumes more than half the vehicle's mass, leaving crew survivability dependent on armor and ECM rather than the ability to disengage.`,`The Mars traces its origins to the 2840s, when ilKhan Jerome Winson championed a deliberate expansion of Clan conventional forces. Winson argued that the Clans' reliance on BattleMechs left them vulnerable in the garrison and defensive roles that consumed much of their military capacity between Trials and border skirmishes. The Mars was his answer, a vehicle that could match a heavy BattleMech in firepower and absorb similar punishment without requiring the years of sibko training and scarce MechWarrior talent that front-line combat units demanded.

Entering service in 2871, the design was adopted by nearly every Clan with the manufacturing base to support it. Multiple Homeworld factories began producing the Mars for their respective toumans, and the type spread across the Clan Homeworlds over the following decades. Clan Hell's Horses, whose doctrine treated conventional forces as genuine frontline assets rather than second-tier support, became the most committed operator. The Horses maintained the highest production rates and pushed the design's development further than any other Clan.

The vehicle's slow speed drew persistent criticism from warriors who viewed vehicles as inherently inferior to BattleMechs, but the Mars consistently performed in the roles it was designed for. It anchored defensive lines, supported Trials of Possession, and on more than a few occasions proved an unpleasant surprise for MechWarriors who underestimated its firepower. Its ECM suite gave it a tactical edge that few conventional vehicles of comparable era could claim.

During the Wars of Reaving, production at several Homeworld facilities was disrupted or destroyed outright. Clan Hell's Horses, having relocated significant industrial capacity to the Inner Sphere, restarted Mars production at Kirchbach in 3072. The Horses made the design available commercially, selling to any buyer. The Rasalhague Dominion secured its own supply through the Orestes facility, where Odin Manufacturing produced the HAG variant beginning in 3075. These Inner Sphere footholds ensured the Mars remained available well into the Dark Age, long after the Homeworld production network had fragmented beyond recovery.`,{front:50,left:43,right:43,rear:37,turret:47,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`clan-ecm-system`,`#0`,null,null,null],`ammo-clan-gauss-rifle-standard`,`ammo-clan-lrm-artemis-iv`,`ammo-clan-lb-10x-cluster`,`ammo-clan-lb-10x-standard`,{manufacturer:`Niles Industriplex Alpha|Swedenborg Heavy Industries`,primaryFactory:`Niles|Kirchbach`,communications:`Q2 Block 9`,targeting:`Series XXXII Multitrack IWS`},[`clan-ecm-system`,`#0`,null,null],{engine:`200 XL`,armor:`Compound Zeta Ferro-Fibrous`},[`clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv`,`#2`,`front`,null],[`clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv`,`#3`,`front`,null],[`clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv`,`#4`,`front`,null],[`ammo-clan-lrm-artemis-iv`,`#13`,null,`clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv`],`ammo-clan-hag-30-standard`,{manufacturer:`Cal-Boeing of Dorwinion`,primaryFactory:`Belladonna`,communications:`Achernar Standard`,targeting:`IsBM Lantirn`},{engine:`Tyron 100 ICE`,armor:`ProtecTech Light`},`The Marten Scout VTOL entered service in 3021 as a product of Cal-Boeing of Dorwinion's Belladonna production line, built to meet demand for a reliable, affordable combat helicopter suited to scouting and special forces insertion. Though production never reached the volumes Cal-Boeing's planners envisioned, the design spread steadily through secondary markets to militia formations and small mercenary units across the Inner Sphere and Periphery over the decades following its introduction.

The Marten earned a reputation as dependable, if unloved. It offered journeyman service in roles other units often neglected, and its simplicity and low cost kept it operational long after more capable platforms became available. Commanders who understood its limitations found it a useful tool. Those who did not often discovered the Marten's boundaries through painful operational experience.`,`The Marten is a 15-ton VTOL driven by a Tyron 100 internal combustion engine. The ICE powerplant keeps the design simple and inexpensive to maintain, an important consideration for the militia and mercenary formations that form the Marten's primary customer base. That simplicity comes at a cost: the ICE provides no fusion-derived endurance advantages, and the design's bulky muffling system limits its ability to approach targets quietly, which is a significant liability for a scout platform.

Speed is the Marten's strongest asset. The design reaches a cruising speed of 140 kilometers per hour and a maximum of 216, placing it among the faster light combat helicopters available to Inner Sphere forces. That speed allows it to cross contested airspace quickly and withdraw before enemy fire concentrates.

Armament consists of a single Federated SuperStreak SRM 2 launcher. The streak guidance system improves hit probability against maneuvering targets, giving the Marten reliable close-in firepower for a platform of its size. ProtecTech Light armor protects the airframe, though coverage is thin and offers little resilience against sustained fire. The Marten carries just enough firepower to make its presence felt and just enough armor to survive a brief engagement, but it exceeds neither threshold by any meaningful margin. Commanders who deploy it in attack or sustained interdiction roles typically find it wanting.`,`Production of the Marten Scout VTOL began at Cal-Boeing of Dorwinion's Belladonna plant in 3021, with the infantry transport variant following three years later. Distribution was never robust. The type filtered through secondary markets and reached militia formations and small mercenary companies across the Inner Sphere and Periphery over the following decades. Its reliability and low acquisition cost kept it in service long after more capable designs became available, though it never achieved the institutional prestige that would have driven significant reorder contracts.

The Marten participated in a broad range of actions during the Dark Age. One of the more notable engagements took place on Diefenbaker in 3137, when a company of Taurian mercenaries raided across the border. The local Islamabad Periphery March Militia, reinforced by a PMM BattleMech lance, used a flight of Martens to strike the mercenaries' grounded DropShip while the 'Mech lance engaged the opposing BattleMech forces. Crossing the landing zone under fire, the Marten flight placed twelve Infiltrator Mk II battle armor troopers directly atop the mercenaries' Union-class DropShip. Two Martens were destroyed during the approach. The remaining aircraft completed their runs and withdrew. Unable to fight effectively with battle armor on their hull and their DropShip under threat, the surviving mercenaries surrendered.

Not all operations ended as favorably. During the fall of Robinson in 3144, a DMM battalion commander ordered a Marten flight to extract an infantry company isolated behind Sword of Light lines. The assigned company commander objected to the mission, judging it too hazardous, but the major overruled him and the Martens launched. Four of the sixteen aircraft were destroyed simply penetrating to the pickup zone. None of the remaining Martens or their embarked infantrymen returned. Concentrated fire brought down every airframe, killing both crews and passengers. The AFFS inspector-general's office had begun reviewing the major's conduct at the time of his death during the subsequent withdrawal.`,[`srm-2`,`front`,`#0`,null],{engine:`Magna 50 ICE`,armor:`Durallex Light Ferro-Fibrous`},`The Maultier is a 15-ton hover APC originally designed for the Star League Defense Force as a fast infantry transport. Capable of deploying a full platoon at high speed, it was valued for its ability to insert troops quickly and withdraw before enemy forces could respond. The design was considered lost after the fall of the Star League until Taurian scientists rediscovered it in 3019, and Taurus Territorial Industries on Sterope successfully replicated the vehicle for the Taurian Defense Force. The Maultier has since become a mainstay infantry transport across the Periphery, with production expanding to include Marian Arms on Alphard. Its combination of speed, simplicity, and reliable troop-carrying capacity has made it a valued asset for commanders who prioritize rapid infantry deployment over heavy firepower.`,`The Magna 50 internal combustion engine propels the Maultier to a top speed of 151 km/h, making it one of the fastest APCs available. This speed is the vehicle's greatest tactical asset, allowing crews to deliver infantry to objectives and withdraw before hostile forces can effectively engage. Star League-era hover technology enables the driver to lower the rear skirt at full speed, with the vehicle only needing to decelerate when troops are actually disembarking. This feature dramatically reduces the time the Maultier spends stationary and vulnerable during deployment operations.

Defensive armament is limited to a single Pinard Reaper Streak SRM-2 rack with one ton of ammunition. The Streak system's lock-on requirement prevents wasted shots, maximizing the limited ammunition supply, but the vehicle is clearly not designed for sustained combat. Durallex Light Ferro-Fibrous armor provides reasonable protection for a vehicle of this weight class, though any sustained engagement with dedicated combat vehicles will quickly overwhelm the Maultier's thin plating. The Neil 6000-x communications system and TracTex Alpha-1 targeting system round out the electronics suite, both proving simple enough for Periphery technicians to maintain and reproduce.`,`The Maultier entered service with the Star League Defense Force during the mid-twenty-sixth century as one of several standard hover APCs fielded by infantry divisions. Produced by Aldis Industries on Terra, it served as a complement to heavier transports like the Maxim, often operating as a point vehicle for APC lances tasked with securing beachheads ahead of main force elements. The design saw extensive action during the latter half of the Star League period and remained in frontline service until the collapse of the Star League.

The devastation of the Succession Wars effectively ended Maultier production. Aldis Industries ceased operations, and the technical knowledge required to produce the vehicle's advanced components was lost along with countless other Star League technologies. The design was presumed extinct until 3019, when a Taurian farmer on Celano discovered the remains of a Star League storehouse while plowing his fields. Among the recovered materials were several intact Maultiers and enough technical documentation for Concordat scientists to attempt replication.

Taurus Territorial Industries on Sterope undertook the reproduction effort, though the Concordat's limited technological base forced significant compromises. The ferro-fibrous armor and Streak missile system were beyond Taurian manufacturing capability at the time, requiring the initial production run to use standard armor and a conventional SRM launcher. The Taurian Defense Force fielded this downgraded variant against pirates along the Concordat's borders well before the Fourth Succession War. Gradual technological recovery allowed TTI to reintroduce ferro-fibrous armor in the early 3040s, and Pinard Protectorates Limited successfully duplicated the Streak system by 3054, enabling production of the full-specification Maultier.

The Capellan Confederation's need for a short-range escort during its operations against the St. Ives Compact created unexpected demand. The Confederation began purchasing Maultiers and organizing infantry companies with lances of three Maultiers supported by a single Blizzard hover APC for long-range covering fire. The Trinity Alliance further expanded the customer base, with both the Magistracy of Canopus and the Confederation placing regular orders. Marian Arms on Alphard eventually secured a production license, adding a second manufacturing source that broadened the vehicle's availability across the Periphery and near Periphery.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`feedsWeaponTag`],$v:[[`streak-srm-2`,`front`,`#0`,null],[`ammo-is-streak-srm-standard`,null,`#1`,`streak-srm-2`]]},{manufacturer:`Taurus Territorial Industries`,primaryFactory:`Sterope`,communications:`Neil 6000-x`,targeting:`TracTex Alpha-1`},`Durallex Light Ferro-Fibrous`,{front:18,left:12,right:12,rear:11,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#0`,null,null],{communications:`CBR ComSat`,targeting:`Corean B-Tech`},{engine:`GM 225 Turbine ICE`,armor:`Republic UltraWeave`},`The Mauna Kea Command Vessel is one of the most common naval combat vessels in the Inner Sphere, serving in wet navies across hundreds of worlds since the Age of War. Named for a volcano on Terra's Hawaii island whose summit lookout warned of approaching danger from the north, the vessel was designed by the Hegemony's Earthwerks Incorporated to serve as a blue water command platform. Earthwerks' decision to license the design at below-standard rates proved to be a gamble that paid off, as the sheer volume of licensed production established the Mauna Kea as the default naval command vessel for any world with significant bodies of water.`,`Built to command other ships at sea, the Mauna Kea prioritizes durability and communications over speed or heavy armament. A GM 225 turbine ICE engine provides adequate speed to accompany a naval task force, while simple construction materials keep licensing agreements manageable for manufacturers across the Inner Sphere. Fifteen tons of Republic UltraWeave armor protect the hull and superstructure, shielding the crew and command staff from most threats the vessel is likely to encounter in littoral and blue water operations.

The offensive suite consists of a Mydron Model B autocannon and a Holly fifteen-tube long-range missile launcher, each fed by two tons of ammunition. The LRMs support other task force members during engagements while the Mydron is held in reserve for direct defense. Five 20mm Gatling machine guns are spread around the hull in point-defense and anti-boarding stations, placed in pairs on port and starboard with a single gun covering the stern. A CBR ComSat communications system and Corean B-Tech targeting array provide the vessel's command and sensor capability. Eight tons of cargo space allows customization, with room often allocated for a marine platoon and expanded command facilities.`,`Earthwerks laid the first keel in 2463, establishing a production line at Calloway VI that would run for centuries. The vessel earned its reputation during the Age of War in the Free Worlds League, where blue water navies remained strategically important on worlds with extensive coastlines. The licensing strategy ensured that manufacturers across the Inner Sphere could produce their own Mauna Keas at nominal cost, and the sheer number of licenses distributed since 2463 means that these vessels, regardless of the local name their builders give them, stand in important positions on the oceans of many worlds.

Although Earthwerks has not produced a new hull in the Free Worlds League in centuries, other licensees have maintained continuous production. Nav Hull on New Samarkand served the Draconis Combine without interruption through every era, while Skye Pleasure Craft Ltd produced intermittent runs for the Lyran Alliance. Lebedev Nautical Enterprises on Tikonov supplied the Capellan Confederation during the Star League and early Succession Wars. Some operators have installed a second LRM-15 at the expense of the cargo bay for greater firepower, while others have added torpedo launchers for anti-submarine capability. During the Jihad era, Earthwerks developed an upgraded specification using an LB 10-X autocannon with Artemis IV fire control, modernizing the ancient platform for continued service.`,`Mauna Kea Command Vessel`,[{tag:`cargo`,amount:8,doors:1}],{front:55,left:43,right:43,rear:44,turret:55,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`machine-gun`,`#1`,`right`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`#2`,`right`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`#3`,`left`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`#4`,`left`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`#5`,`rear`,null,null],{manufacturer:`Scarborough Manufacturers`,primaryFactory:`Al Na'ir`,communications:`Scarborough Talky 2`,targeting:`Scarborough Tracky 1`},{engine:`PowerTech 165 Highlift`,armor:`ProtecTech 6`},`The Maxim (I) Heavy Hover Transport represents Scarborough Manufacturers' answer to the growing demand for dedicated battle armor carriers in the Draconis Combine military. Introduced in 3060, the design sacrifices much of the original Maxim's heavy weaponry in favor of a significantly expanded infantry bay capable of carrying a full squad of battle armor troopers alongside conventional infantry.

The designation "Maxim (I)" distinguishes this infantry-focused variant from the combat-oriented models produced at the same Scarborough facility on Al Na'ir. While less heavily armed than its predecessor, the Maxim (I) fills a critical logistical role in combined-arms formations where rapid battle armor deployment takes priority over organic fire support.`,`The Maxim (I) trades the original's extensive missile battery for a more focused weapons suite designed around supporting its embarked troops. A turret-mounted Streak SRM-6 provides accurate close-range firepower, backed by a pair of extended-range medium lasers that offer ammunition-independent engagement capability. A TAG unit retained in the turret allows the crew to designate targets for nearby Arrow IV batteries and semi-guided LRM munitions, giving the transport indirect fire coordination ability that most APCs lack.

The expanded twelve-ton infantry bay is the heart of the redesign. The interior accommodates a full platoon of battle armor with room to spare, or can carry a substantially larger force of conventional infantry. ProtecTech 6 ferro-fibrous armor replaces the original ArcShield plating, providing improved protection at reduced weight. The same PowerTech 165 Highlift engine maintains the familiar 129 kilometer per hour top speed. The primary weakness remains the hover skirt, and the reduced weapons load means the Maxim (I) is heavily dependent on its embarked troops and supporting units for protection once the infantry has deployed.`,`Maxim (I) Heavy Hover Transport`,{front:25,left:23,right:23,rear:16,turret:20,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`er-medium-laser`,`turret`,`#1`,null],[`is-tag`,`turret`,`#3`,null],[`c3-computer-master`,`#1`,null,null],[`ecm-suite`,`#2`,null,null],[`er-medium-laser`,`#5`,`turret`,null],[`is-tag`,`#6`,`turret`,null],`Maxim New Standard TargetTrack`,{engine:`PowerTech 165 Highlift ICE`,armor:`ArcShield V`},`Designed and produced by Maxim Transport Industries beginning in 2689, the Maxim Heavy Hover Transport was the brainchild of a retired infantry general on Al Na'ir who envisioned a single vehicle capable of delivering an entire infantry platoon to the battlefield. Before the Maxim, commanders relied on lances of three or four smaller APCs to deploy a single platoon, a logistically cumbersome arrangement that limited tactical flexibility.

The Maxim proved an immediate success across the Inner Sphere, earning a reputation as the gold standard for military hover transports. Popular with commanders for its combination of speed, firepower, and carrying capacity, the Maxim is equally respected by the infantry who ride in its spacious and comfortable bay. Though Maxim Transport Industries itself is long gone, the design endures through multiple successor manufacturers, a sign of the soundness of the original concept.`,`The Maxim mounts an impressive weapons array for a transport vehicle. A turret-mounted SureShot Mk VI SRM-6 serves as the primary close-range weapon, supplemented by side-mounted SureShot II SRM-2 launchers. Three LongFire V LRM-5 racks provide sustained long-range fire support, with two forward-facing and one rear-mounted to cover withdrawals. A trio of heavy machine guns in the turret rounds out the armament, giving the Maxim effective anti-infantry capability during troop deployment.

Powered by a PowerTech 165 Highlift internal combustion engine, the Maxim reaches a top speed of 129 kilometers per hour. Five and a half tons of ArcShield V armor provide adequate protection for its weight class, though the hover skirt remains a persistent vulnerability to concentrated fire. The six-ton infantry bay accommodates a full platoon with padded seating and enough space for troops to check weapons and move comfortably. This combination of firepower and carrying capacity makes the Maxim far more capable than a simple transport, though commanders must weigh its combat value against the risk of losing an entire platoon in a single vehicle.`,`Maxim Transport Industries was founded on Al Na'ir as a startup led by a retired infantry general who understood firsthand the limitations of existing troop transports. When the Maxim entered service in 2689, it was the first hover vehicle capable of carrying a full infantry platoon in a single chassis. The design's unexpected success allowed MTI to expand rapidly, opening additional production facilities on Skye and Sian. The company had plans for further expansion to Atreus and Kathil, though these facilities were never realized.

Despite the Maxim's commercial triumph, MTI's subsequent attempts to diversify into other military products proved to be dismal failures and a significant drain on profitability. The company limped along for centuries on the strength of its sole successful product. In 3037, a massive scandal erupted within MTI involving accusations of embezzlement, inappropriate conduct between coworkers, and rumors of bizarre hazing rituals. The scandal proved fatal, and MTI was dissolved.

Prominent corporations moved quickly to acquire the defunct company's assets. Scarborough Manufacturers purchased the original MTI plant on Al Na'ir, while Cyclops Incorporated absorbed the Skye facility. The Sian plant saw the most dramatic transition when Hellespont Industrials seized control with an armed force. Commander Max Cooper, the guard commander at the Sian facility and a significant MTI shareholder, attempted to hold the plant by force during the dissolution. His troops, composed of infantrymen and their Maxim transports, held the facility for twelve hours before Hellespont's corporate militia overwhelmed the defenders. Cooper's forces retreated and attempted a second assault hours later, but the swift hovertanks were no match for the now-entrenched Hellespont defenders. Cooper lost his life in the failed counterattack.

Under their new owners, Maxim production continued without interruption. Each manufacturer maintained the original design specifications, ensuring parts compatibility across the Inner Sphere. The Maxim remained the standard infantry transport through the Late Succession Wars and into the technological renaissance that followed the discovery of the Helm Memory Core. Scarborough Manufacturers introduced the first major upgrade in 3052, integrating Streak missile technology, ferro-fibrous armor, and a TAG system into the proven chassis. The upgrade program brought modern capabilities to the venerable design while preserving the reliable foundation that had served for over three centuries.

During the FedCom Civil War, Maxim-equipped infantry forces saw extensive action across multiple theaters. A common tactic saw Maxim platoons establish perimeters under heavy fire, deploy their infantry, and then fall back to provide long-range fire support until the infantry required extraction. In Free Worlds League space, Capellan soldiers used a company of Maxims to flank a combined-arms force during a raid on a Sirius-system world, routing the defenders when massed LRM fire spooked the raiders into retreating. The concentrated SRM fire from the Maxim formation crippled one BattleMech at a time as the panicked raiders fled the system.

The rise of battle armor across the Inner Sphere created new challenges and opportunities for the Maxim. Both factory and field modifications emerged to accommodate the heavier, bulkier battle armor suits, while other variants optimized the chassis for electronic warfare and networked combat. Production continued across all three successor manufacturers through the Jihad, the Republic era, and into the ilClan period, with the Maxim remaining one of the most widely fielded hover transports in human-occupied space.`,`Maxim Heavy Hover Transport`,{front:20,left:20,right:20,rear:12,turret:16,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`lrm-5`,`front`,`#0`,null],[`lrm-5`,`front`,`#1`,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#5`,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#6`,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#7`,null],[`ammo-lrm-standard`,null,`#12`,`lrm-5`],`Designed and produced by Maxim Transport Industries beginning in 2689, the Maxim Heavy Hover Transport was the brainchild of a retired infantry general on Al Na'ir who envisioned a single vehicle capable of delivering an entire infantry platoon to the battlefield. Before the Maxim, commanders relied on lances of three or four smaller APCs to deploy a single platoon, a logistically cumbersome arrangement that limited tactical flexibility.

The Maxim proved an immediate success across the Inner Sphere, earning a reputation as the gold standard for military hover transports. Popular with commanders for its combination of speed, firepower, and carrying capacity, the Maxim is equally respected by the infantry who ride in its spacious and comfortable bay. Though Maxim Transport Industries itself is long gone, the design endures through multiple successor manufacturers, a reflection of the soundness of the original concept.`,[`streak-srm-2`,`right`,`#2`,null],[`streak-srm-2`,`left`,`#3`,null],[`ammo-lrm-standard`,null,`#10`,`lrm-5`],[`srm-6`,`turret`,`#7`,null],[`ammo-srm-standard`,null,`#11`,`srm-6`],`Designed and produced by Maxim Transport Industries beginning in 2689, the Maxim Heavy Hover Transport was the brainchild of a retired infantry general on Al Na'ir who envisioned a single vehicle capable of delivering an entire infantry platoon to the battlefield. Before the Maxim, commanders relied on lances of three or four smaller APCs to deploy a single platoon, a logistically cumbersome arrangement that limited tactical flexibility.

The Maxim proved an immediate success across the Inner Sphere, earning a reputation as the gold standard for military hover transports. Popular with commanders for its combination of speed, firepower, and carrying capacity, the Maxim is equally respected by the infantry who ride in its spacious and comfortable bay. Though Maxim Transport Industries itself is long gone, the design endures through multiple successor manufacturers, evidence of the soundness of the original concept.`,{manufacturer:`Field Refit`,primaryFactory:`Various`,communications:`Maxim New Standard I/O`,targeting:`Maxim New Standard TargetTrack`},[`lrm-5`,`#1`,`front`,null],[`lrm-5`,`#2`,`front`,null],[`streak-srm-2`,`#3`,`right`,null],[`streak-srm-2`,`#4`,`left`,null],[`srm-6`,`#5`,`turret`,null],`Designed and produced by Maxim Transport Industries beginning in 2689, the Maxim Heavy Hover Transport was the brainchild of a retired infantry general on Al Na'ir who envisioned a single vehicle capable of delivering an entire infantry platoon to the battlefield. Before the Maxim, commanders relied on lances of three or four smaller APCs to deploy a single platoon, a logistically cumbersome arrangement that limited tactical flexibility.

The Maxim proved an immediate success across the Inner Sphere, earning a reputation as the gold standard for military hover transports. Popular with commanders for its combination of speed, firepower, and carrying capacity, the Maxim is equally respected by the infantry who ride in its spacious and comfortable bay. Though Maxim Transport Industries itself is long gone, the design endures through multiple successor manufacturers, proof of the soundness of the original concept.`,[`ammo-is-streak-srm-standard`,`#8`,null,`streak-srm-2`],[`lrm-5`,`front`,`#0`,null,null],[`lrm-5`,`front`,`#1`,null,null],[`lrm-15`,`turret`,`#2`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#3`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#4`,null,null],[`ammo-lrm-standard`,null,`#5`,`lrm-15`,null],[`ammo-lrm-standard`,null,`#6`,`lrm-15`,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,null,`#8`,`machine-gun`,!0],{engine:`PowerTech 165 Highlifter`,armor:`ArcShield Heavy Ferro-Fibrous`},`The Mk II's spacious sixteen-ton infantry bay is its defining feature, capable of carrying a full platoon of battle armor or an entire company of conventional infantry. Adjustable handholds and equipment hooks line the walls, while storage bins offer space for ammunition, power packs, and other combat essentials. Multifunction fold-down stations accommodate both standard power armor and quad suits, with a quick release system for rapid egress.

A pair of Helga Series three-tube Multi-Missile Launchers provide the sole offensive armament, mounted in the turret. Though light by Maxim standards, the MML-3s offer tactical flexibility through their ability to fire standard LRM or SRM loads as well as specialty munitions like Thunder LRM mines. Two tons of ammunition stored in a CASE-protected bin ensure the crew and passengers survive any catastrophic detonation. Five and a half tons of ArcShield Heavy ferro-fibrous armor provide above-average protection for the weight class. The PowerTech 165 Highlifter fusion engine maintains the traditional Maxim top speed of 129 kilometers per hour while eliminating the logistical burden of fuel resupply. The primary drawback is the near total reliance on embarked troops for close defense once the infantry has deployed.`,`The Maxim Mk II emerged from the Republic Armed Forces' "Anyone Can Be a Hero" recruiting campaign, which dramatically expanded the need for heavy infantry carriers capable of deploying battle armor in large numbers. Both Cyclops Industries and Scarborough Manufacturers submitted competing proposals, each drawing on their decades of experience producing the original Maxim. When the RAF selected the Maxim as the basis for their new transport, neither company could independently meet the production requirements or the RAF's demanding specifications.

Scarborough approached Cyclops with a joint venture proposal, but the partnership proved contentious from the start. Heated debates erupted over the appropriate battlefield role for the Mk II. Amidst significant media coverage, Cyclops's executive VP of development, Shiloh Connolly, resigned over the direction of the project. Connolly publicly stated that the final specifications ignored the Maxim's lengthy and successful history of combat support roles, producing a vehicle designed to avoid fights rather than win them. Scarborough's engineers countered that reducing the weapons load would keep the Mk II out of combat and extend its service life, allowing the RAF to invest more in battle armor and other equipment. The RAF ultimately sided with Scarborough's philosophy, and the Maxim Mk II entered production in 3083.

The entire first production run went to the regiments of the Hastati Sentinels. Subsequent runs were divided between the Sentinels, Triarii Protectors, and Principes Guards, with approximately ninety percent being the standard configuration. The RAF standard procedure called for each Mk II to transport a full platoon of battle armor, though additional configurations could accommodate two motorized infantry platoons, four platoons of jump infantry, or five platoons of conventional foot soldiers. Multi-role loadouts typically included two squads of battle armor alongside two platoons of jump or foot infantry.

The Mk II Infantry Support variant, produced exclusively on Skye and known colloquially as the "Shiloh" after the departed Cyclops executive, addressed the concerns Connolly had raised. By halving the infantry bay and installing light PPCs and machine guns, the Shiloh provided substantially more organic firepower. The variant was exported to the Lyran Commonwealth, where it was assigned to units in the Bolan and Donegal provinces. Beginning in 3088, the RAF also deployed standard Mk IIs to prefecture militias, expanding the vehicle's presence throughout Republic territory.

Production at both the Skye and Al Na'ir facilities continued through the Republic era and into the Dark Age. When Clan Jade Falcon seized Skye, the Cyclops facility fell under Falcon control, though production of the Mk II continued for the new owners. Following the formation of the ilClan, the Skye plant transferred to Clan Wolf Empire authority. The Al Na'ir facility passed through Word of Blake control during the Jihad, then Republic of the Sphere governance, before returning to Draconis Combine ownership as the Republic contracted. Both plants remain active producers of the Mk II through the current era, ensuring the design continues to serve multiple successor states simultaneously.

The ECM variant emerged as a field modification in 3087, downgrading the turret weaponry to a single Streak SRM-2 while adding a Guardian ECM suite. This configuration proved popular with units operating in electronic warfare environments, where the ECM protection offset the reduced firepower. The full infantry bay was retained, maintaining the Mk II's primary transport mission while adding a valuable force-multiplier capability to the formation.`,{front:30,left:20,right:20,rear:19,turret:20,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},`Named for the Hebrew word meaning "chariot," the Merkava was Leopard Armor's signature heavy tank and the primary armored vehicle of the Terran Alliance and its successor state, the Terran Hegemony. The original design entered service with the Alliance in 2294, and a succession of upgraded marks followed over the next two centuries. Each iteration built upon the tank's reputation for rugged construction and ease of field maintenance. The Merkava became a symbol of Hegemony military power during the Age of War, serving on dozens of fronts across known space. Its lineage influenced subsequent heavy tank designs for centuries, with vehicles such as the Manticore and Von Luckner drawing upon battlefield lessons learned by Merkava crews.`,`The Merkava is a turret-based heavy tank designed around a balanced mix of direct fire and missile support. In its definitive Mk. VIII configuration, the tank mounts a Class 5 Armstrong autocannon as its primary weapon, supplemented by a Harpoon-15 long-range missile launcher for indirect fire support. A Harpoon-4 short-range missile launcher provides close-range punch, while a pair of M-3 machine guns handle anti-infantry duties, one mounted in the turret and the other on the hull front. The Vlar 300 fusion engine gives the 75-ton tank a respectable top speed of 64 kph, a significant improvement over the 54 kph achieved by earlier ICE and primitive fusion variants.

Ten tons of PanzerSlab armor provide solid protection across all facings, with near-even distribution between hull and turret. The Merkava's chief weakness was its inability to evolve alongside increasingly powerful weapons technology. Even the fusion-powered marks carried armament considered outdated by the late Star League era. Earlier variants suffered additional drawbacks, including the fuel dependency of internal combustion engines and the substandard protection of primitive armor that proved especially vulnerable to energy weapons.`,`The Merkava's history spans nearly eight centuries, from its debut with the Terran Alliance in 2294 through its revival during the Jihad. Leopard Armor of Terra produced the original design as a mainstay battle tank for Alliance ground forces. As the Alliance evolved into the Terran Hegemony, the tank was continually upgraded. The Mk. VI, introduced in 2384, became the standard heavy tank of the Hegemony Armed Forces during the turbulent Age of War. To the general public, the Merkava symbolized Hegemony military power as much as the mighty WarShips of the Hegemony Navy, earning it the nickname "King of the Battlefield."

The ICE-powered Merkavas earned their reputation across numerous campaigns, but operational limitations became apparent as the Hegemony expanded its borders. During a six-month campaign to wrest control of Kentares IV from Federated Suns forces in 2431, the constraints of combustion-powered vehicles were painfully exposed. The Hegemony responded by commissioning the Mk. VII in 2435, the first Merkava to mount a fusion engine. The new mark proved itself during the Battle of Tybalt, where it helped secure the world as a Hegemony protectorate against Capellan opposition.

By 2439, the older Mk. VI had been relegated to militia duties and training roles. Four of these obsolescent tanks were stripped of their LRM launchers, fitted with remote-control systems, and pressed into service as target drones for a secret weapons test at the Yakima firing range on Terra. On February 5th of that year, Colonel Charles Kincaid piloted the prototype Mackie BattleMech against the four remotely operated Merkavas and destroyed all of them without sustaining any damage. The brutal efficiency of the demonstration stunned observers and cemented the BattleMech's place as the dominant weapon of ground warfare. The tank once celebrated as the "King of the Battlefield" had helped usher in its own replacement.

The Mk. VIII entered service in 2465 with a more refined fusion engine derived from BattleMech research, and it served the Hegemony Armed Forces through the Reunification War. Leopard Armor discontinued Merkava production in 2601, judging that further modifications could not keep pace with advancing technology. Surviving Mk. VIIIs were transferred to militia formations or sold to Star League member states. By the fall of the Star League, few remained intact, and those that did were cannibalized for their valuable fusion engines during the Succession Wars.

The Merkava might have remained a historical footnote if not for the discovery of the Hegemony Memory Core. During the Jihad, Earthwerks Incorporated recognized the tank's simple construction as an advantage for rapid wartime production. The company established a makeshift manufacturing facility on Tiber in the Principality of Regulus and began producing the modernized Mk. IX. By 3073, the Regulan Hussars were receiving the new tanks in large numbers, giving the ancient Merkava lineage an unexpected second life.`,{front:36,left:32,right:32,rear:26,turret:34,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#1`,null,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,null,`#9`,`machine-gun`,!0],`Michaelson Heavy Industries`,`General Motors|Ceres Metals Industries|Ceres Metals Industries`,{engine:`Nissan 95`,armor:`Durallex Light`},`The Minion Advanced Tactical Vehicle is a 20-ton hovercraft developed through an unusual joint venture between General Motors of Kathil and Ceres Metals Industries of Tikonov, linking manufacturers from the Federated Suns and Capellan Confederation under the political umbrella of the Federated Commonwealth. Conceived around 3061 as an open-battlefield strike vehicle, its commercial rollout was delayed by uncertainty surrounding the Clan threat. The outbreak of the FedCom Civil War revived demand almost immediately, and the design was adapted to serve a dedicated urban combat role alongside the larger Morningstar City Command Vehicle.

The Minion's integration with C³ Command/Control/Communications networks defines its battlefield purpose. Built from the outset to serve as a network node rather than a standalone combatant, it fills a niche in combined-arms formations for both Federated Suns and Capellan Confederation forces.`,`The Minion's Nissan 95 fusion powerplant gives it a top speed exceeding 150 kph, placing it among the faster combat vehicles in its weight class. Its five and a half tons of Durallex Light armor provide meaningful protection for a hovercraft, allowing it to absorb punishment while continuing to maneuver. The primary armament of two Defiance P5M Medium Pulse Lasers delivers accurate, sustained fire at close to medium range, with the pulse mechanism compensating for the minor targeting fluctuations inherent to high-speed hover movement.

On open streets and flat terrain, the Minion's exceptional speed forces opponents to track its movement while its pulse weapons strip armor. In urban environments, however, narrow streets and blocked intersections can severely restrict this tactical flexibility, which is the design's most significant operational limitation.

The Brightstar 17 Mk. II targeting system was developed specifically for use with pulse lasers and integrates with a C³ slave unit, linking the Minion to a broader Command/Control/Communications network. This connectivity is the vehicle's defining tactical asset. The Minion feeds targeting data to friendly units across the battlefield, functioning as a mobile sensor node that multiplies the effectiveness of the entire formation rather than simply contributing its own firepower.`,`The Minion originated as a joint development project made possible by the political integration of the Federated Commonwealth. General Motors of Kathil and Ceres Metals Industries of Tikonov collaborated on the design beginning around 3061, with both manufacturers contributing to the tooling and production architecture. Initial sales projections were dampened when uncertainty surrounding the ongoing Clan threat slowed procurement interest across the Inner Sphere.

The FedCom Civil War proved to be the design's commercial salvation. As fighting engulfed Kathil, local commanders identified a pressing need for fast, network-capable urban combatants that could integrate into Command/Control/Communications nets alongside BattleMechs. The Minion was rapidly adapted to this role, pairing with the Morningstar in a dedicated city-support team configuration. Katherine Steiner-Davion's loyalists were among the first to deploy Minions from the Kathil production runs, using them during the fighting on Kathil and Tikonov. By late 3065, Victor Steiner-Davion's forces had seized control of both facilities, turning the design against its previous operators. The Minion saw particularly intense use during the assault on New Avalon.

The Tikonov facility's proximity to Capellan space created political complications. Despite a Federated Suns prohibition on military technology transfers, production notes were shared with a Ceres Metals facility on Warlock, enabling a second Capellan production line. When Capellan forces retook Tikonov during the later civil war period, they reassigned the Tikonov output to their own formations and began retooling the nearby Warlock plant for additional production. The Tikonov facility passed through independent and then Republic of the Sphere control during the Jihad and early Dark Age before eventually returning to Capellan ownership. The Kathil line remained under Federated Suns control throughout, sustaining steady production into the ilClan era.`,`Minion Advanced Tactical Vehicle`,{front:28,left:22,right:22,rear:16,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`medium-pulse-laser`,`#1`,`front`],[`medium-pulse-laser`,`#2`,`front`],{engine:`Fusion 150`,armor:`Compound Alpha`},`The Mithras Light Tank is a Clan combat vehicle designed to serve as both a swift cavalry unit and a versatile fire support platform. Developed by Clan scientists in the mid-twenty-ninth century, the design emerged as a practical answer to the need for fast, hard-hitting light armor capable of engaging targets across multiple range brackets. The Mithras occupies a well-established niche in Clan vehicle doctrine, operating as a quick-response unit equally suited to harrying flanks, supporting Elemental assaults, or sustaining pressure on retreating forces. Though its thin armor limits staying power in sustained engagements, the design's combination of speed and weapons flexibility has made it a reliable fixture across multiple Clans throughout the Succession Wars era and beyond.`,`The Mithras is operated by a two-man crew. The driver manages vehicle movement and sensor operations, while the gunner handles the weapons and communication systems using an integrated Combined Weapon System. The CWS allows both turret weapons to be aimed and fired simultaneously, a significant tactical advantage. The absence of a secondary gun sight means the front-mounted laser cannot be fired if the vehicle's sensors are disabled, reducing the gunner's options when the CWS becomes inoperative.

The primary weapon is a Type 25 Ultra Autocannon/2 mounted in the turret for full arc coverage. Its advanced autoloader allows variable rates of fire, up to twelve rounds per minute at full speed. The recoil mechanism is prone to fouling the autoloader track, however, which can jam the system and render the gun useless at critical moments. Two Series 2b Extended Range Medium Lasers complement the cannon. One is mounted coaxially in the turret to cover the autocannon's dead zone, while the second is fixed in the hull front for suppression when the turret is engaged elsewhere.

The Mithras carries only three tons of armor, but the Compound Alpha Ferro-Fibrous construction provides protection equivalent to more than four tons of standard plating. The vehicle can absorb hits from all but the largest weapons as a result. The rear armor is the design's primary weakness, as a single hit from even a light or medium weapon can breach it, leaving the vehicle vulnerable to catastrophic damage from behind.`,`The Mithras traces its origins to the early decades of Clan society, when Clan scientists worked to provide their ground forces with a fast, adaptable tracked vehicle suited to the varied terrain of the Clan homeworlds. Introduced in 2845 during the Early Succession Wars period, the design drew on the lessons of the Pentagon Civil Wars and the Clans' subsequent drive to standardize their military equipment. The Mithras emerged as a pragmatic light cavalry platform, filling the gap between heavier tanks and the purely Elemental-focused ground warfare doctrine that many Clans preferred.

During the early homeworld era, Clan Ice Hellion operated a production line at the Hector MotorWorks Facility Gamma on Hector, turning out the design for their own touman as well as for broader Clan use. Clan Ghost Bear maintained a separate facility on Bearclaw, which supplied the Ghost Bear touman and those of allied Clans throughout the Succession Wars and Clan Invasion periods.

The Clan Invasion brought the Mithras into contact with Inner Sphere forces, where the design proved well-suited to the fast-paced flanking operations that Clan commanders favored in conquered occupation zones. The Fire Mandrill Kindraa Kline accumulated a notable concentration of the vehicles, having captured a number of tanks from Clan Ice Hellion following the Ice Hellions' failed Hellion's Fury campaign against their Clan rivals. The Kindraa's Twenty-first Vanguard Cluster found the design well-matched to their defensive doctrine, using the tanks to fix and harass enemy positions while Elemental forces maneuvered to deliver the decisive blow.

The Ghost Bear facility on Bearclaw transferred to the Rasalhague Dominion during the Civil War, and then to Clan Star League forces during the Jihad period, reflecting the political upheaval of the era. The Hector facility changed hands as well, passing from Clan Ice Hellion to Clan Goliath Scorpion during the Jihad, before Clan Coyote ultimately assumed control in the early Dark Age. Engineers at the Hector facility developed the ERLL variant during the Jihad period, substantially upgrading the vehicle's long-range firepower to meet the demands of the changed operational environment.`,{front:16,left:14,right:14,rear:9,turret:14,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},{communications:`TharHes HQ CommSet`,targeting:`Diplan IX SideSweeper`},{engine:`Omni 130 Fusion`,armor:`Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/4`},`The Mobile Headquarters is the original dedicated command vehicle of the Star League Defense Force, designed and manufactured by Star League Defense Industries on Mars beginning in 2620. For over a century, the Mobile HQ served as the nerve center of large-scale planetary operations, gathering information from ground forces, aerospace assets, and orbiting spacecraft to present a comprehensive battlefield picture to the commanding officer. The vehicle changed little during its production run, evidence of the soundness of its original design. Its destruction during the Amaris Coup and the subsequent loss of replacement technology made surviving examples among the most prized military assets in the Inner Sphere.`,`A typical Mobile HQ is divided into two main sections. The communications room seats up to six operators with standing room for twelve more, dominated by a TriHolo table at its center that displays surrounding terrain, individual troop positions, and known enemy formations in three-dimensional detail. The sophisticated battle computer tied into the table can project future movements and suggest courses of action based on whatever variables the commander enters. Three communications stations allow the commander and a staff of seven commtechs to maintain constant contact with forces in the field. The powerful transmitters are capable of piercing most jamming systems and can connect to distant JumpShips using a collapsible main array mounted on the roof.

A complex dynamic suspension system keeps the interior stable, allowing normal activity within despite the vehicle's speed on rough terrain. A single turret-mounted Defiance B3M medium laser provides minimal self-defense, reflecting the vehicle's intended position well behind the front lines. StarSlab/4 armor provides adequate protection against light weapons and shrapnel. The TharHes HQ CommSet and Diplan-IX SideSweeper targeting system round out the electronics package, though many surviving examples have lost some or all of these systems to age and battle damage.`,`Star League Defense Industries produced the Mobile HQ on Mars for over a century, equipping every major SLDF formation with at least one of these sophisticated command platforms. The vehicle became so integral to large-scale operations that its loss could cripple a command's ability to coordinate forces across a planet. When Stefan Amaris launched his coup in 2767, the SLDI factory on Mars was destroyed, ending production permanently. No other manufacturer possessed the technical expertise to replicate the vehicle's advanced electronics suite.

The Succession Wars proved catastrophic for the remaining Mobile HQ fleet. The vehicles were prime targets for capture or destruction, and those that survived often suffered irreparable damage to their electronics. By the early thirty-first century, most surviving examples were only partially functional, with working computers or TriHolo tables replaced by more conventional tracking methods. Some vehicles were gutted entirely, their functional equipment transplanted into smaller command vans to preserve at least some coordination capability. Others had their fusion engines stripped and sold to keep light BattleMechs operational, receiving internal combustion engines as replacements. When Wolf's Dragoons appeared in the Inner Sphere, they were notable for possessing five seemingly brand-new and fully operational Mobile HQs, a fact that astonished military observers across every Successor State. As Star League technology became available again during the technological renaissance, efforts were made to restore surviving vehicles, gradually increasing the number of operational Mobile HQs in service.`,{front:16,left:19,right:19,rear:16,turret:10,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},{$k:[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`],$v:[[`communications-equipment`,`#0`,null],[`medium-laser`,`#1`,`turret`]]},{engine:`Omni ICE (fusion stripped)`,armor:`Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/4`},[`communications-equipment`,`#0`,null],`The Mobile Headquarters is the original dedicated command vehicle of the Star League Defense Force, designed and manufactured by Star League Defense Industries on Mars beginning in 2620. For over a century, the Mobile HQ served as the nerve center of large-scale planetary operations, gathering information from ground forces, aerospace assets, and orbiting spacecraft to present a comprehensive battlefield picture to the commanding officer. The vehicle changed little during its production run, a reflection of the soundness of its original design. Its destruction during the Amaris Coup and the subsequent loss of replacement technology made surviving examples among the most prized military assets in the Inner Sphere.`,{$k:[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`,`feedsWeaponTag`],$v:[[`communications-equipment`,`#0`,null,null],[`lrm-10`,`#1`,`turret`,null],[`ammo-lrm-standard`,`#2`,null,`lrm-10`]]},`Rebel Industrial Technologies, Inc.`,`The Mobile Long Tom stands among the most feared indirect-fire weapons in the Inner Sphere, a rolling siege battery capable of reducing fortifications and destroying armored formations from distances that place its crews entirely beyond the reach of retaliation. Built by Rebel Industrial Technologies, Inc. of Mars and introduced in 2602, the design was conceived for the Star League Defense Force as a mobile decisive fire-support option capable of breaking any fixed defense without exposing assault troops to unnecessary risk.

Taking the form of a convoy of up to six tracked vehicles centered on an Armstrong Industries Long Tom Rifle Mobile Chassis System, the Mobile Long Tom converts a weapon of immense destructive power into a self-sufficient mobile battery that can relocate after firing to deny counterbattery response. When the Star League collapsed and these systems passed to Successor States forces, their fearsome reputation only grew during the brutal wars that followed. Few weapons have so consistently shaped the calculus of defensive warfare across three centuries of Inner Sphere conflict.`,`The Mobile Long Tom achieves its destructive power through extreme range and raw hitting power rather than any pretension toward survivability. The convoy's centerpiece is the Armstrong Industries Long Tom Rifle Mobile Chassis System, an artillery piece with a reach measured in kilometers that is equally suited to destroying fixed fortifications, disrupting massed formations, and systematically demolishing urban terrain. Four SperryBrowning machine guns provide minimal close-range defense against infantry but offer no meaningful protection against combat vehicles or BattleMechs. The crew depends entirely on supporting arms for anti-aircraft and anti-armor coverage.

Power comes at a significant cost in mobility. The Robel 110 ICE engine provides road movement capability but precludes cross-country travel when the full convoy is assembled, and the ICE power plant demands constant refueling. Three ammunition carriages carry approximately seventy-five rounds in total, enough for just over four minutes of continuous fire before the battery must reposition to await resupply. StarSlab/1 armor protects the crew from small arms and artillery fragments but cannot withstand dedicated anti-vehicle fire. A single pass by a hostile aerospace fighter or an unsupported flanking BattleMech can destroy the entire system. The Mobile Long Tom's survivability depends entirely on concealment, distance, and the vigilance of its escorts.`,`The Mobile Long Tom predates the height of the Star League itself. Rebel Industrial Technologies introduced the design in 2602 at their Mars facility, constructing it as a mobile fire support platform for the Star League Defense Force's expanding campaign requirements. While most armies fielded conventional artillery in fixed emplacements or towed configurations, the SLDF demanded a weapon capable of relocating after firing, denying counterbattery response and maintaining offensive momentum during extended campaigns. The resulting LT-MOB-25 satisfied that demand by concentrating everything necessary for sustained bombardment into a convoy of tracked vehicles that could be broken apart, moved independently, and reassembled within hours.

The Mobile Long Tom found its first major institutional role as part of the Terran Hegemony's Main Guardian Defense System. Rather than station permanent SLDF ground forces on every world lacking a Space Defense System, the Hegemony equipped major population centers with networks of automated Guardian drone tanks controlled by central computers, supported by Mobile Long Tom batteries to provide the decisive firepower those drones could not carry. When the SLDF undertook its liberation campaign during the Amaris Civil War, those computers and their drone complements were methodically destroyed to prevent the enemy from recapturing them. The Mobile Long Tom batteries survived, left behind as isolated fire support assets whose doctrinal purpose had been eliminated along with the systems they once supported.

When the Star League dissolved and the Exodus stripped the Inner Sphere of its finest military minds and most advanced technology, the Mobile Long Tom proved both too valuable and too simple to abandon. Rebel Industrial Technologies continued production on Mars through the first century of the Succession Wars under successive periods of Rim Worlds Republic and later ComStar stewardship, keeping artillery batteries operational across every major power. The system's simplicity was its principal advantage: ICE power plants, conventional munitions, and uncomplicated electronics that could be maintained with Succession Wars-era industrial capacity.

The history of the Mobile Long Tom is written in the rubble of the cities it has bombarded. In 2888, Draconis Combine forces raiding the Davion world of Errai discovered that two Mobile Long Toms firing from an urban position could neutralize an entire raiding force before it could establish a defensive perimeter, their rounds dismantling cover and forcing a withdrawal rather than face sustained bombardment. On Berenson, a prolonged engagement between House Marik and House Liao saw each side's batteries hunting the other for nearly a week, with espionage and sabotage ultimately determining the outcome. In 3002, a Marik strike against Treth in the Lyran Commonwealth turned on the defenders' decision to use a single Mobile Long Tom to simulate a larger battery, successfully deceiving the attackers into a premature withdrawal before their objectives were secured.

These engagements reinforced a doctrine that placed Mobile Long Tom batteries at the core of garrison and defensive planning across all major powers. Batteries of four to six units attached to regimental battle groups controlled the ground around any fixed position with the implicit threat of fire from kilometers in any direction. The psychological weight of artillery is disproportionate to its mechanical specifications. Soldiers unable to see what is destroying them, hearing only the distant concussion before impact, fight differently than those facing direct fire.

The LT-MOB-25F, introduced in 2695, extended the system's capabilities with ferro-fibrous armor, CASE protection, and a more powerful engine that improved road speed. Multiple manufacturers took on production, including Bithinian Ballistics, Lycomb-Davion IntroTech, Austen-Armstrong Industries, and Taurus Territorial Industries, dispersing the design's production base across multiple factions. When the Jihad silenced the Mars facility, those dispersed production lines ensured continuity. The 3132 Blackout demonstrated that indirect fire remained among the few weapons capable of neutralizing the agile OmniMechs favored by raiders, driving a post-Blackout production revival across the Inner Sphere. The LT-MOB-95, introduced by the Federated Suns in 3097, rationalized the entire system into a single compact chassis through Kallon Weapon Industries, eliminating the convoy requirement and the logistical burden it had always imposed while preserving the Long Tom's fundamental destructive reach.`,`Mobile Long Tom Artillery`,[`machine-gun`,`#3`,`right`,null],[`machine-gun`,`#4`,`left`,null],[`ammo-long-tom-standard`,`#8`,null,`long-tom-artillery`],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,`#9`,null,`machine-gun`],`Kallon Weapon Industries`,`communications-equipment`,{manufacturer:`Brooks Incorporated`,primaryFactory:`Andurien`,communications:`Maxell Maxell 650`,targeting:`Maxell Maxell TA 85 with TAG`},{engine:`Vlar 300 XL`,armor:`StarSlab StarSlab/7 Heavy Ferro-Fibrous`,jumpJets:`N/A N/A`},`The Moltke Main Battle Tank is a seventy-five-ton fusion-powered tracked combat vehicle developed by Brooks Incorporated for the Duchy of Andurien during the Jihad, entering production in 3076. Commissioned directly by the Duchy to address critical equipment shortfalls without relying on outside suppliers, the Moltke was designed to provide local armored forces with a capable frontline vehicle drawing on the structural philosophy of the ancient Augustus A-3. Brooks retained the Augustus's distinctive triple-turret arrangement and sloping track guards while replacing the old diesel powerplant with an extralight fusion engine and fitting the design with modern heavy ferro-fibrous armor and advanced electronics. The result was a tank that offered considerably greater speed, firepower, and survivability than its Age of War ancestor while maintaining a visual continuity that tied the new design to the Duchy's established manufacturing identity. The Moltke rapidly became the Duchy's primary armored asset during and after the Jihad period.`,`The Moltke is powered by a Vlar 300 XL fusion engine, reaching a top speed of approximately sixty-four kilometers per hour and reducing crew requirements to five compared to the Augustus A-3's ten. StarSlab/7 Heavy Ferro-Fibrous armor provides substantial protection for its weight class. A Maxell 650 communications system paired with a Maxell TA 85 targeting computer incorporating TAG allows the Moltke to serve as an artillery spotter in addition to its direct-fire role, giving combined-arms formations a vehicle capable of directing indirect fire assets from the front line.

The M1's turret carries a Diverse Optics Sunbeam extended-range laser flanked by two Imperator Automatic Weaponry light Gauss rifles, concentrating long-range precision firepower in the triangular mount inherited from the Augustus. Three tons of rifle ammunition support sustained engagements. The M2 trades this long-range combination for close-support capability, replacing the main laser with a Snub-Nose PPC and adding five multi-missile launchers at the vehicle's front with six tons of ammunition, providing exceptional short-range saturation fire and ammunition flexibility. The M3 retains the triangular turret but replaces the light Gauss rifles with a pair of Kali Yama Weapons Industries LB 10-X autocannons with cluster and standard ammunition options, supplemented by a FlameTech Inferno plasma rifle for anti-infantry and anti-stealth work and TAG for artillery coordination. The M3's biggest weakness, shared across all variants, is a lack of dedicated anti-infantry weapons beyond the plasma rifle's area effect.`,`The Moltke's development began as a direct response to the pressures of the Jihad, when the Duchy of Andurien found itself stretched by the demands of sustaining forces without reliable access to outside supply chains. The Word of Blake's disruption of interstellar commerce and the broader chaos of the conflict left many smaller powers scrambling for indigenous solutions, and the Duchy turned to Brooks Incorporated to produce a modern heavy tank that could be built and maintained entirely within Duchy space. Brooks drew on the legacy of their Age of War Augustus A-3, retaining its structural character while bringing the design fully into the modern technological era.

The M1 entered production at Andurien in 3076, with initial output distributed among militia forces and Free Worlds League regulars stationed in the Duchy. Outside parties inquired early about purchasing the Moltke for their own forces, and Brooks forwarded these requests to Duchy government offices for approval, establishing a pattern of controlled export that would define the tank's distribution through the Jihad period. The M2 variant followed in 3077, intended to complement the M1's long-range precision fire with heavier short-range capability, giving combined-arms formations a more flexible armored component. The M3 arrived in 3080, incorporating lessons from Jihad-era engagements, particularly the value of TAG designation for artillery coordination and anti-infantry systems in an environment increasingly complicated by battle armor and stealth-equipped infantry.

Following the Jihad's conclusion, distribution expanded cautiously beyond Duchy forces. Sales opened to the Principality of Regulus and the Duchy of Oriente, with the M1 and M2 seeing the broadest export uptake. The Duchy declined to authorize sales outside former League worlds that were not politically aligned with Andurien interests, keeping the Moltke's distribution concentrated within the remnants of the Free Worlds League sphere. The tank's single production facility at Andurien remained active through the Dark Age under Duchy of Andurien oversight, sustaining output for a design that had become the backbone of Duchy armored formations.`,{front:62,left:47,right:47,rear:32,turret:50,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`gauss-rifle-light`,`turret`,`#0`,null],[`gauss-rifle-light`,`turret`,`#1`,null],[`ammo-is-light-gauss-rifle-standard`,null,`#5`,`gauss-rifle-light`],{manufacturer:`General Motors|Ceres Metals Industries|Ceres Metals Industries`,primaryFactory:`Kathil|Tikonov|Warlock`,communications:`Overlook J11`,targeting:`Brightstar 17 Mk. II`},{engine:`VOX 280 XL`,armor:`Durallex Standard`},`The Morningstar City Command Vehicle is a 60-ton wheeled combat vehicle representing the second major joint venture between General Motors of Kathil and Ceres Metals Industries of Tikonov. Designed from inception as a city command vehicle (CCV) capable of coordinating combined-arms maneuvers in the absence of BattleMechs, it serves as the command hub and companion unit to the lighter Minion hover tank.

The C³ master computer at the heart of the Morningstar's design defines its battlefield purpose, making it the nerve center of any urban formation it joins. The partnership between General Motors and Ceres Metals that produced both the Morningstar and the Minion was unlikely to survive the political upheavals of the FedCom Civil War, and the Morningstar stands as the final product of that collaboration.`,`The Morningstar is powered by a VOX 280 XL fusion engine, giving the 60-ton vehicle a respectable cruising speed for its class. Nine tons of Durallex Standard armor provides solid protection, particularly suited to the urban environments where the CCV was designed to operate. The vehicle is not intended for extended independent campaigns, carrying limited ammunition, but its cargo bay can be configured to transport a squad of battle armor troops or a standard infantry platoon, adding tactical flexibility to its command role.

Primary armament is a Mydron Excel Autocannon 10, which delivers consistent firepower against armored targets at moderate range while the vehicle maintains its command posture. Three Slingshot Machine Guns provide close-range point defense against infantry and light vehicles in densely built-up areas. The Morningstar is not engineered for extended independent action, and a lone Morningstar stripped of its network is a comparatively modest threat.

The Overlook J11 communications system integrates a C³ master computer, the central reason for the vehicle's existence. This system allows the Morningstar to coordinate a full Command/Control/Communications net, linking BattleMechs and subordinate vehicles through shared targeting data across an entire command company. The Brightstar 17 Mk. II targeting system is shared with the Minion, a deliberate design choice ensuring full compatibility across the combined-arms team. Without its C³ net, the Morningstar loses much of its battlefield value and becomes dependent on its autocannon and point defense weapons alone.`,`The Morningstar was developed in the final stages of design at General Motors' Kathil plant, conceived as a city command vehicle to lead urban defensive operations. Its development ran parallel to that of the Minion, with both designs emerging from the same joint venture. While the Minion provided front-line combat capability, the Morningstar supplied command authority, and the two were intended from the outset to operate as an integrated pair. The second joint effort between General Motors and Ceres Metals Industries, the Morningstar reflected a deepening cooperation between the two manufacturers in the years before the FedCom Civil War made that collaboration untenable.

The FedCom Civil War validated the city command vehicle concept under live-fire conditions. Morningstars led urban assaults across multiple contested worlds, coordinating mixed lances of tanks and infantry to seize smaller cities and production sites while BattleMechs contested the major capitals. The Federated Suns fielded most of the extant Morningstars during the civil war period, deploying them extensively on Kathil and Tikonov. House Liao expressed significant interest in acquiring the design, and Capellan intelligence obtained enough production data from Tikonov to support eventual independent production at the Warlock facility. When Capellan forces retook Tikonov, the production line was redirected to supply Confederation formations.

During the Jihad, the Tikonov facility briefly operated under independent management before passing to Republic of the Sphere control. The Kathil line remained continuously under Federated Suns ownership, maintaining a reliable supply of Morningstars into the Dark Age period. Both the Federated Suns and the Capellan Confederation separately pursued upgraded designs featuring dual C³ master computers, reflecting the high value both states placed on the command vehicle concept. The Word of Blake also adapted the Morningstar platform into a specialized mobile communications hub during the Jihad, producing limited numbers at Tikonov for their own operational requirements.`,`Morningstar City Command Vehicle`,{front:43,left:29,right:29,rear:19,turret:24,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},`York Vehicle Y2 Facility|New York Vehicle Y2 Facility`,`Consolidated Type 2M w/ECM Suite`,`Consolidated Type V TTS`,{engine:`York Vehicle Y2 Facility 240`,armor:`York Vehicle Y2 Facility Compound JX4`},`The Morrígú's primary armament consists of two Series 1 extended-range large lasers and two Type XV "Crossbow" LRM-15 launchers, all mounted in a turret for full traverse. This configuration delivers concentrated fire at extended range and reflects the Blood Spirits' long-standing preference for direct energy weapons, a philosophy evident across multiple Blood Spirit designs including the Blood Kite. Mounting all primary weapons in the turret allows the Morrígú to engage targets at any bearing without repositioning the hull, a useful capability given its limited ground speed.

For close defense, a Pattern J1 SRM-2 launcher is mounted on each flank, covering threats that close inside the main engagement envelope. A Consolidated Type 2M ECM suite adds electronic protection, complicating enemy targeting solutions. Both the communications and targeting systems are Consolidated products, providing reliable electronics performance.

The Morrígú carries thirteen tons of Compound JX4 armor, more substantial than most Clan combat vehicles at this weight class. However, the Fusion 240 powerplant produces a top speed of only 54 km/h, limiting tactical flexibility considerably. This vehicle is designed for static fire positions at range rather than mobile engagements. Commanders who rely on the Morrígú in fluid situations where the battle line shifts quickly will find its pace a genuine liability. It carries no jump jets, and repositioning under pressure offers no quick solution.`,`The Morrígú was commissioned in the early 3060s by Khan Karianna Schmitt of Clan Blood Spirit, intended as a visible assertion of continued strength in the aftermath of the Absorption War. Alongside the Crimson Langur OmniMech and the ongoing ProtoMech development effort, the vehicle was one of several projects meant to signal that the Blood Spirits remained capable of fielding competitive military hardware.

Khan Schmitt chose the vehicle's name personally. Her Welsh heritage was held in near-sacred regard among the Blood Spirits, tracing lineage directly to Colleen Schmitt, the Clan's founding Khan. Colleen Schmitt had descended from Colonel Hanni Schmitt, the last commander of the Royal Black Watch Regiment of the Star League, a lineage treated as nearly holy within Blood Spirit culture. Drawing on that ancestry, Khan Karianna Schmitt chose a name from Celtic mythology: the Morrígú, a goddess of war said to appear wherever battle was joined. The name was a deliberate statement of purpose.

Production at the York Vehicle Y2 Facility on York began in 3064, and the vehicle was distributed across every Galaxy in the Blood Spirit Touman, including the newly established Iota Galaxy: Retribution. A complete Star of Morrígú vehicles appeared within the Twenty-second Combat Force, a Trinary formed by Kindraa Mick-Kreese of Clan Fire Mandrill, signaling the strengthening bond between the Blood Spirits and their Crusader-aligned neighbors in the Homeworlds. That the vehicle found favor with the most fanatically Crusader of the Kindraa factions was seen as fitting, given the Blood Spirits' own Crusader leanings.

The Wars of Reaving ultimately destroyed Clan Blood Spirit as a political entity. Before the York facility was lost, however, Clan Diamond Shark had acquired the production plans. In 3068 the Sharks established the New York Vehicle Y2 Facility on Trondheim, named explicitly in reference to its predecessor. Where the original production served the Blood Spirits exclusively, the Trondheim facility operated as a commercial venture, making the Morrígú available to any buyer. During the Jihad, the facility suffered significant personnel losses but maintained production at reduced capacity, and it carried the design forward through the Dark Age and into the ilClan era under Diamond Shark, and later Sea Fox, stewardship.`,`Morrigu Fire Support Vehicle`,{front:63,left:41,right:41,rear:28,turret:35,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`clan-srm-2`,`#1`,`right`,null],[`clan-srm-2`,`#2`,`left`,null],[`clan-lrm-15`,`#5`,`turret`,null],`ammo-clan-hag-40-standard`,`ammo-mech-mortar-standard`,{manufacturer:`Corean Enterprises`,primaryFactory:`New Avalon`,communications:`Corean Transband-J9`,targeting:`Corean B-Tech with TAG`},{engine:`GM 115 ICE`,armor:`StarGuard CIV`},`The Musketeer Hover Tank was developed by Corean Enterprises of New Avalon and entered service with the Armed Forces of the Federated Suns in 3065. Designed as a dedicated rotary autocannon platform, it arrived during a period of intense interest in RAC-based weapons systems across the Federated Suns. Corean engineers prioritized speed, sustained firepower, and operational economy, producing a hover tank capable of delivering rapid, concentrated damage across a wide range of engagements. Its combination of high cross-terrain mobility, continuous autocannon output, and solid crew protection made it attractive for both offensive strike roles and screening operations. The Musketeer became one of the first production vehicles to fully exploit RAC technology at scale, establishing Corean Enterprises as a leading supplier for the AFFS ground forces and earning a reputation as a cost-effective frontline combat vehicle.`,`The Musketeer is built around a single turret-mounted Mydron Tornado Rotary AC/5, fed by a substantial three-ton magazine that sustains fire well in excess of one minute at maximum rate. This endurance distinguishes the Musketeer from lighter autocannon designs and gives it exceptional battlefield persistence. For close-range engagements, a pair of Holly Mark II Streak SRM 2-Packs provide accurate secondary fire without wasting ammunition on missed shots. A forward-fixed TAG designator rounds out the weapon suite, allowing the crew to direct guided artillery support onto targets without closing to weapon range.

Propulsion comes from a GM 115-rated internal combustion engine, enabling a top speed approaching 120 kilometers per hour on flat terrain with a cruising range of nearly 1,500 kilometers on standard combustible fuels. Seven tons of StarGuard CIV ferro-fibrous armor provide protection sufficient to withstand a hit from the heaviest standard autocannon. An integrated CASE system shields the crew from catastrophic detonation of ammunition or fuel, addressing the particular vulnerability of ICE-powered vehicles to internal fires.

The Musketeer's primary limitation is its dependence on the single RAC main gun. A jam or mechanical failure at a critical moment leaves the vehicle with only the Streak launchers for close-in defense. The hover chassis, while fast on open ground, loses much of its mobility advantage in heavily wooded terrain, dense rubble, or steep gradients, restricting the design's effectiveness in urban and forested environments.`,`The Musketeer emerged from the Federated Suns' rapid embrace of rotary autocannon technology following its development in the early 3060s. As RAC weapons spread through the Inner Sphere, manufacturers throughout the Federated Suns moved quickly to incorporate the new systems into both BattleMech and vehicle designs. Corean Enterprises secured the contract for the Musketeer in 3065, introducing the design to the AFFS as political tensions within the Federated Commonwealth were reaching a critical threshold.

The timing proved consequential. The Musketeer entered full production only months before the FedCom Civil War erupted, and the design found itself in combat on both sides of the conflict almost immediately. Corean Enterprises' facility on New Avalon placed it at the center of the fighting, with Musketeer units participating in the final battles for the planet itself. This early combat exposure on both sides of the Civil War cemented the design's battlefield reputation and generated significant demand for expanded production.

Following the Civil War, the AFFS moved to consolidate control over the Musketeer's production run, securing an exclusive five-year contract with Corean Enterprises and prioritizing militia units on worlds facing the Draconis Combine and Capellan Confederation. An armor-enhanced configuration, the (Armor) variant, entered service in 3068, offering commanders the option of trading burst-fire capability for improved vehicle survivability. By the time of the Jihad the Musketeer had become a well-established element of Federated Suns ground forces, deployed across multiple commands and proving itself effective in the sustained attritional combat of that era.

A further modernization produced the (3080 Upgrade) configuration, refining the design's systems based on accumulated field experience from both the Civil War and Jihad. The upgrade extended the Musketeer's operational life into the Dark Age period. Production of all variants continued on New Avalon under Federated Suns ownership through most of the Republic era, with the New Avalon facility eventually transitioning to Draconis Combine control following the collapse of the Republic of the Sphere.`,[`is-tag`,`#1`,`front`,null],[`rotary-ac-5`,`#2`,`turret`,null],[`streak-srm-2`,`#3`,`turret`,null],[`streak-srm-2`,`#4`,`turret`,null],`ammo-is-rotary-ac-2-standard`,`The Myrmidon Medium Tank entered production in 3060 as a New Earth Trading Company initiative to meet rising demand for affordable tracked firepower in an increasingly unstable Inner Sphere. Taking its name from legendary Greek warriors, the tank was designed from the outset as an accessible alternative to heavier, costlier BattleMech units. Drawing on the proven Manticore platform as a conceptual baseline but built around a more economical power plant, the Myrmidon delivers long-range firepower competitive with lighter BattleMechs at a fraction of the acquisition cost.

NETC marketed the design primarily to smaller state militias and independent commands unable to maintain full BattleMech forces, making the Myrmidon a fixture of Chaos March and periphery battlefields from the Civil War era into the thirty-second century.`,`The Myrmidon is a 40-ton tracked medium tank built around a Nissan 200 fusion engine, yielding a road speed of 86 km/h and a cross-country pace of 54 km/h. That performance falls short of hover alternatives and lighter wheeled designs, but the tracked suspension delivers reliable mobility across a wide range of terrain conditions. The Myrmidon carries no jump capability.

Firepower centers on a single turret-mounted Parti-Kill Heavy Cannon, a particle projection cannon providing long-range striking power equivalent to the primary armament of many medium BattleMechs. A SureShot Mk VI SRM-6 rounds out the turret, adding close-range punch against infantry and lightly armored targets. The combined loadout allows engagement at multiple ranges, though the SRM system carries limited ammunition for sustained operations.

Protection comes from an ArcShield Maxi II standard armor suite. Coverage is adequate for a medium design but leaves the Myrmidon vulnerable to concentrated fire from heavier opponents. The fusion engine eliminates the catastrophic ammunition explosion risk present in combustion-powered vehicles, a selling point for smaller militaries with limited maintenance facilities. The reduced crew requirement compared to older medium tanks eases manpower demands, though it limits situational awareness during sustained combat.`,`NETC developed the Myrmidon in the late 3050s in direct response to rising demand for affordable combined-arms firepower in the Chaos March and across the broader Inner Sphere. The collapse of the Federated Commonwealth and the ongoing Clan threat had left dozens of smaller worlds and independent commands scrambling for military equipment they could afford and maintain. NETC's engineers reviewed the successful Manticore chassis and chose to produce a lighter, less expensive alternative rather than compete in the heavier tank market. The decision to accept a more modest power plant was explicitly commercial, not tactical.

Initial production at New Earth supplied primarily the Lyran Alliance, which was fortifying its Clan invasion corridor in the late 3050s and early 3060s. A portion of NETC output went to independent militaries throughout the Chaos March, where the Myrmidon quickly earned a solid reputation in low-intensity conflicts against Capellan-backed guerrilla forces. The practical economics of the design attracted consistent mercenary interest, and NETC established a steady export market through the Civil War period.

Jalastar Aerospace, a Federated Suns manufacturer best known for its Manticore production on Panpour, entered a licensing agreement with NETC during this period to produce the standard Myrmidon for AFFS contracts. The arrangement complicated Jalastar's internal sales priorities, given the Myrmidon's overlap with their Manticore line. Jalastar's engineers would later leverage their familiarity with the design to develop a substantially modified anti-infantry variant.

The Jihad proved catastrophic for original Myrmidon production. Word of Blake forces assaulted New Earth in the mid-3070s, and the fighting effectively destroyed NETC's manufacturing capacity on the planet. With the original production line gone, the Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium approached NETC in the mid-3080s about acquiring a production license. NETC accepted the arrangement, and Maxwell began producing the Myrmidon at their facility on Maxwell around 3088, securing the design's continued availability into the Republic era and beyond.`,{front:35,left:25,right:25,rear:24,turret:35,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`ammo-srm-standard`,null,`#2`,`srm-6`],[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`sponson`,`feedsWeaponTag`],[`machine-gun-light`,`right`,`#1`,!0,null],[`machine-gun-light`,`right`,`#2`,!0,null],[`machine-gun-light`,`left`,`#4`,!0,null],[`machine-gun-light`,`left`,`#5`,!0,null],[`machine-gun-light`,`left`,`#6`,!0,null],`is-light-machine-gun-array`,{manufacturer:`New Samarkand Metals`,primaryFactory:`New Samarkand`,communications:`Olmstead 3000`,targeting:`Omicron IX`},{engine:`General Motors 270 XL`,armor:`New Samarkand Metals Teppeki Hardened with CASE`},`The Narukami Heavy Tank is a 90-ton tracked combat vehicle built by New Samarkand Metals on New Samarkand and introduced to Draconis Combine Mustered Soldiery service in 3097. Named for the Shinto deity of thunder, the design reflects the Combine's long tradition of invoking mythological imagery for its military hardware. New Samarkand Metals developed the Narukami in response to DCMS requirements for a heavy armored platform capable of engaging threats at extended ranges, filling a role that lighter and more common designs could not adequately perform. The tank proved successful enough to generate a dedicated C3-equipped variant in 3108, extending its service life and operational flexibility. Both variants have become fixtures in DCMS armored formations, particularly among the New Samarkand Regulars and the Legions of Vega.`,`The Narukami's development traces to the late 3080s, when DCMS planners identified a gap in their heavy armor inventory. The Jihad had inflicted severe losses on Combine armored formations, and the rebuilding period revealed that the DCMS lacked a dedicated long-range heavy tank capable of holding defensive lines and contesting fortified positions. New Samarkand Metals received the development contract, drawing on their experience with large tracked vehicles to design the Narukami from the ground up as a purpose-built fire support platform rather than an adaptation of an existing hull.

First units entered service in 3097, distributed initially to the New Samarkand Regulars where proximity to the manufacturing facilities simplified resupply. The design's battlefield performance demonstrated both its strengths and its limits. During a reconnaissance operation on Sylvan in early 3145, two platoons of Narukamis held a DropShip landing zone against a superior Federated Suns force, absorbing volleys and withdrawing in good order while maintaining fire discipline. The engagement reinforced the vehicle's reputation as a resilient defensive platform, though it also exposed the cost that attritional warfare extracted from units operating far from their supply bases.

New Samarkand Metals introduced the BC3 variant in 3108, redesigning the weapon configuration to support C3 network operations. The variant saw action on Barlow's Folly during Duke Corwin Sandoval's invasion in 3141, where a pair of BC3 tanks contributed to significant AFFS losses before being overrun, illustrating both the variant's firepower and its susceptibility to combined-arms pressure when cut off from its support network. The Narukami family's performance across these engagements cemented its place as a capable fire support platform that performs best within organized defensive or networked tactical schemes.`,{$k:[`tag`],$v:[[`quirk-hard-to-pilot`],[`quirk-fast-reload`],[`quirk-narrow-low-profile`]]},{front:38,left:27,right:27,rear:21,turret:27,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`standard-gauss-rifle`,`#4`,`turret`,null],{manufacturer:`Galtor Naval Yards|Galtor Naval Yards`,primaryFactory:`Exeter|Galtor`,communications:`Lynx Shur`,targeting:`Sonar Sync Tracker`},{engine:`Doorman 270 Naval HyPerOx`,armor:`SeaSlab/14.5`},`The Neptune Submarine emerged from a late Second Succession War strategic reassessment within the Federated Suns. As commanders grew frustrated with the vulnerability of surface and orbital command infrastructure to aerospace strikes and DropShip assault, House Davion experimented with relocating critical control nodes to the ocean floor. Submerged command posts could direct land engagements from positions largely impervious to conventional bombardment, provided they could be adequately protected. Galtor Naval Yards, a commercial shipbuilder operating in the Draconis March, answered this requirement with the Neptune class in 2950. Designed around the principle of quiet, sustained underwater patrol and facility defense, the Neptune combined robust torpedo armament with a reliable naval propulsion system. Though cramped and logistically demanding, it established submarine warfare as a legitimate tool in planetary defense doctrine throughout the Federated Suns and, eventually, beyond.`,`The Neptune displaces one hundred tons and is driven by a Doorman 270 Naval HyPerOx propulsion system capable of pushing the vessel to a maximum speed of 54 kilometers per hour whether submerged or running on the surface. The engine can operate in open-cycle diesel mode on the surface or switch to hydrogen peroxide combustion for deep-water operation, providing tactical flexibility across varying depth requirements. A conventional double-hull construction separates the pressure hull from the outer hull, providing structural redundancy and a rated dive depth exceeding one kilometer. Six crew members occupy the interior, and their working conditions are particularly cramped given the volume consumed by machinery and ammunition storage.

The primary offensive system is the Sea Devastator 20-rack torpedo battery, a marinized version of long-range missile technology with excellent range and firepower against surface and subsurface targets. Two Sea Harvester six-rack torpedo launchers provide close-range punch, ensuring the Neptune can engage across the tactical envelope. A Sutel XII large laser serves as a secondary weapon for surface engagements and situations where torpedo expenditure must be conserved. The Neptune's chief tactical weakness is limited torpedo reloads, which constrains its ability to sustain prolonged combat operations without resupply support.`,`The Neptune traces its origins to a doctrinal dilemma that plagued commanders throughout the early Succession Wars. By the late Third Succession War, it had become axiomatic that a fixed planetary command post could not survive the opening hours of a major invasion. Mobile headquarters offered tactical flexibility but sacrificed the coordination capacity that fixed installations provided. House Davion answered by placing critical command infrastructure underwater, where conventional attacks could not easily reach it.

Galtor Naval Yards, a commercial vessel builder operating in the Draconis March, received the development contract in the 2940s and completed the first Neptune in 2950. The design entered service with the AFFS as a command post escort and facility defense platform. Though not widely distributed in early production, its value became apparent as planetary defense planners recognized that controlling the undersea domain around important coastal facilities was as strategically critical as defending the surface.

The Fourth Succession War brought unprecedented demand. Border planets along the Kuritan and Capellan fronts deployed four or more Neptune-class submarines to protect underwater command centers, and the newly formed Federated Commonwealth sent a flood of orders into Galtor Naval Yards from Lyran worlds as well. Overwhelmed, GNY subcontracted production to several other Davion companies to meet demand. By the War of 3039, Neptune submarines had become standard equipment on virtually any inhabited world with substantial bodies of water and a meaningful defensive infrastructure.

The fall of Galtor to Draconis Combine forces during the Fourth Succession War displaced GNY's administrative headquarters and disrupted production at the Galtor facility. Output fluctuated with subsequent changes in planetary control over the following decades. Capture and authorized production during periods of Combine occupation ensured the Neptune appeared in Draconis Combine inventories as well, making it one of the more widespread naval vehicles in the Inner Sphere despite its comparatively modest origins.`,[`srt-6`,`front`,`#0`,null],[`srt-6`,`front`,`#1`,null],[`lrt-20`,`front`,`#2`,null],[`ammo-lrt-standard`,null,`#5`,`lrt-20`],{chassis:`Yelm Weapons`,engine:`Vlar 160`},`The Nightshade ECM VTOL was developed by Yelm Weapons on New Earth as the Inner Sphere's first dedicated airborne electronic warfare platform, entering service with the Star League Defense Force in 2597. Designed around the Guardian ECM suite, the lightweight 25-ton VTOL was built to suppress enemy electronics and provide real-time reconnaissance support for combined-arms operations. Its high cruising speed offered meaningful survivability against conventional fire, though its light armor made sustained engagements hazardous. The destruction of Yelm's production facilities during the Amaris Crisis and subsequent Succession Wars drove the design into near-extinction. The Nightshade experienced a revival in 3069 when the Meers-Skobel Defense Corporation, operating under contract to the Word of Blake, introduced a thoroughly modernized variant incorporating C³i networking technology, restoring the design as a capable scouting and electronic warfare asset for Blake's Protectorate Militia divisions.`,`The Nightshade's defining characteristic is speed. At 129 km/h cruise and 194 km/h flank, the VTOL can reposition across a battlefield rapidly, making it difficult for conventional ground forces to track and engage. The original Yelm model mounted a Randall Medium Laser for self-defense, paired with a NIRAD 210 Guardian ECM suite, the combination that gave the design its historical significance as the first combat platform to field the Guardian system. However, the original's light armor left it extremely vulnerable to sustained fire. Pilots depended on avoidance rather than direct engagement, and a single burst of autocannon fire could disable the vehicle entirely.

The Meers-Skobel production version addressed the armor shortcoming by adopting FiberTech Light Plus ferro-fibrous plating, providing roughly 35 percent better coverage for comparable weight. The Moscovia Light Particle Projector Cannon replaced the original medium laser, offering improved standoff range appropriate for a design that prioritizes evasion over direct combat. The Johnston VRR 2.0 communications suite and Lamprey VXX targeting system equip the MSDC production model and complement the C³i integration that makes this variant a meaningful force multiplier when operating alongside other Blake-networked units. Even in its improved form the Nightshade is fragile, and its value lies entirely in its electronics rather than its combat punch.`,`The Nightshade traces its origins to Yelm Weapons on New Earth, where the design entered production in 2597 as the Star League Defense Force's dedicated airborne electronic countermeasure platform. A second Yelm production line at Fletcher provided additional output, and the design proved popular enough that a Royal variant with enhanced electronics entered service in 2717 for select SLDF commands.

The Amaris Crisis ended the Nightshade's golden era. The New Earth facility was destroyed during the conflict, and Fletcher's factory suffered a similar fate in the fighting that followed the Liberation of Terra. With both production sources gone and spare parts no longer available, the design entered a long decline. Units still in service during the Succession Wars were progressively stripped of their Guardian ECM components, which were pulled to equip other platforms. By the late 2800s, operational Nightshades had effectively ceased to exist as a fielded type, reduced to warehouse curiosities in the inventories of backwater planetary militias.

The design's revival came through the Word of Blake's appetite for recovered Star League technology. In 3063, Dame Yelena Torowicz founded the Meers-Skobel Defense Corporation on Terra, contracting with the Word to research and modernize archived designs. MSDC identified the Nightshade's airframe as a suitable platform for Blake's growing C³i network doctrine. The result, unveiled in 3069, was a substantially redesigned vehicle using ferro-fibrous armor, a light particle cannon, and full C³i suite. The Word deployed this model widely across Protectorate Militia formations, and a companion variant mounting a light autocannon with target acquisition gear for indirect fire support followed in 3070. Unconfirmed reports from Capellan Confederation border worlds during the Jihad described a locally produced Nightshade variant with a light autocannon and TAG, though the Confederation denied manufacturing any such design.

When the Republic of the Sphere absorbed former Protectorate worlds after the Jihad, production at the Terra facility continued under ROS stewardship. The C³i-equipped (Light PPC) variant was phased out, as the Republic had no doctrinal need for C³i-reliant platforms and the technology itself was being actively suppressed. The light autocannon variant continued in production and served as the primary Nightshade model through the Dark Age period.`,{manufacturer:`Meers-Skobel Defense Corporation`,primaryFactory:`Terra`,communications:`Johnston VRR 2.0`,targeting:`Lamprey VXX`},{chassis:`Meers-Skobel Defense Corporation`,engine:`Vlar 160`,armor:`FiberTech Light Plus`},{manufacturer:`Quikscell Company`,primaryFactory:`Ares`,communications:`Endicott Type 6G Maser`,targeting:`Dwyerson Mark IV`},{engine:`Nissan 200 XL`,armor:`Hellespont Heavy Ferro-Fibrous`},`The Nisos Attack WiGE is a 35-ton wing-in-ground-effect combat vehicle introduced in 3122 by the Quikscell Company at their factory on Ares. Named for a figure from classical Terran mythology who helped place Aegeus on the throne of Athens, the vehicle was designed to fill the gap between traditional scout units and heavier dragoon formations. Quikscell deliberately targeted the advanced reconnaissance market, building the Nisos to outperform comparable units in the interdictor role. The vehicle quickly became a favorite among Capellan Confederation Armed Forces scout and cavalry battalions, where its combination of speed, firepower, and crew comfort gave it a reputation for reliability unusual among Quikscell products.`,`The Nisos is powered by a Nissan 200 XL fusion engine, giving it an impressive top speed of over 150 kilometers per hour. Six tons of Hellespont Heavy Ferro-Fibrous armor provide excellent protection for a vehicle in its weight class, allowing it to survive engagements that would destroy lighter reconnaissance craft. The primary armament consists of a turret-mounted Ceres Arms Crusher plasma rifle supported by a Harpoon-6 SRM launcher, providing both direct-fire suppression and close-range striking power. Two tons of plasma rifle ammunition and one ton of missile reloads sustain the vehicle through extended engagements, though the reliance on ammunition limits operational endurance without resupply.

Quikscell invested considerable effort in crew accommodations. Both the driver and gunner benefit from comprehensive controls built to ultramodern ergonomic standards, while the commander's station is unusually spacious and comfortable. All three crew stations feature personal storage compartments and adjustable five-point restraints, reducing fatigue during long sorties. The weapon bays use modular construction, allowing field technicians to reconfigure armament loadouts with minimal downtime and support requirements, much like an OmniVehicle but at a fraction of the cost. Despite these advantages, the Nisos remains a light vehicle, and sustained fire from heavier opponents can quickly overwhelm its defenses.`,`Quikscell Company developed the Nisos on Ares to meet growing Capellan Confederation demand for fast, versatile reconnaissance platforms in the early thirty-second century. The Ares facility had suffered devastating losses during the Jihad, with 85 percent capital and personnel casualties reducing operations to barely half capacity. By the 3120s, the plant had recovered sufficiently to launch new product lines, and the Nisos represented a departure from Quikscell's traditional focus on inexpensive, disposable combat vehicles.

The Nisos proved its worth during the Capellan invasion of Palos in 3134. Elements of Warrior House Dai Da Chi and McCarron's Armored Cavalry clashed with the Principes Guards south of the planetary capital, Xer, in the Grael River Delta. The terrain was a labyrinth of seasonal flooding, quicksand, and dense undergrowth that grounded conventional aerial reconnaissance assets and hampered infantry operations. Heavy rain and strong winds kept Capellan aerial recon units grounded for days at a time. A Capellan screening force of VTOLs and conventional fighters moved into the bayou, followed by a battalion of CCAF BattleMechs. The Guards deployed along the only passable stretch of terrain and inflicted heavy casualties in the first hour, giving up only two kilometers.

After the Guards' aerospace fighters sortied from Xer and scattered the Capellan air support, a platoon of Nisoses split from the main Capellan force and made a flanking advance through the marsh. The WiGEs bypassed the bayou's natural obstacles with ease, outmaneuvering the heavier defending forces while aerial defenders were temporarily absent. Within two hours, the Guards were in disarray. The Nisos platoon struck from behind, then melted back into the swamp before the defenders could regroup. Major Ruth Janson ordered defending units to withdraw and regroup farther into the bayou, but the Nisoses continued their harassing actions and raced toward the Guards' supply depot. The withdrawal became a rout as the Guards scrambled to protect their supplies. By the end of the day, the Capellans had crossed the bayou and gained access to the M7 Expressway. The Guards were forced to retreat 100 kilometers to the planetary capital. The Capellans conquered Palos three weeks later.`,[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`,null],[`quirk-modular-weapons`,`#0`],[`quirk-modular-weapons`,`#1`],{front:28,left:22,right:22,rear:22,turret:25,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},`ammo-is-extended-lrm-standard`,`Barcella Beta Compound 2110 Ferro-Fibrous`,`The Odin's primary asset is its advanced active probe, capable of detecting hidden units at ranges up to one hundred fifty meters. Fully integrated with the vehicle's communications and targeting systems, the probe uses magnetic resonance, infrared, and electromagnetic intercepts to build a detailed tactical picture of the surrounding terrain. This makes the Odin invaluable for surveying cluttered terrain and identifying concealed enemy positions.

Powered by a lightweight fusion engine, the Odin can reach speeds of up to 130 km/h on paved surfaces, though the need to protect the vehicle's suspension typically limits off-road speeds to around 90 km/h. Ferro-fibrous armor provides minimal protection, sufficient to resist small-arms fire and incidental damage from collisions during high-speed movement, but wholly inadequate against serious military threats. The vehicle relies on speed and stealth rather than durability to survive.

Turret-mounted pulse lasers provide limited defensive capability, trading range for improved accuracy against fast-moving targets. A Streak missile launcher eliminates wasted ammunition, while a nose-mounted small laser allows the driver to engage threats in the forward arc. These weapons are controlled by the gunner, who also operates the sensor suite, placing heavy demands on crew coordination.`,`The Odin entered Clan service in 2844, during a period when the Clans were still establishing their industrial infrastructure across the Kerensky Cluster. The design filled a critical gap in Clan force structures, providing a purpose-built reconnaissance vehicle that freed BattleMechs from scouting duties many warriors considered distasteful. Production lines were established on worlds belonging to nearly every Clan, from Tamaron to Ironhold, Huntress to Tranquil.

The Odin's most notable early combat deployment came with Clan Jade Falcon, whose warriors initially relegated the vehicle to training and support roles. This dismissive attitude proved costly when Jade Falcon intelligence-gathering efforts suffered on the Bear world of Tokasha. Poor-quality reconnaissance led to disastrous engagements against Clan Ghost Bear defenders. Other Clans took note of the Jade Falcons' experience and modified their training and deployment procedures to ensure Odin crews received proper support and tactical integration.

Unlike most Clan warriors, Odin crew members train together for their entire career. The vehicle's small size and sophisticated systems demand a level of teamwork unusual in Clan military culture. This close working relationship between driver and gunner proved essential for effective operation of the sensor suite during high-speed maneuvers.

During the Clan Invasion era, Odin production continued on most homeworld facilities. The vehicle saw service across the occupation zones, though it rarely participated in front-line combat. By the Civil War period, some facilities had changed hands through Clan political upheavals. The Jade Falcon plant on Ironhold eventually ceased production entirely. Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain began producing the Odin after Clan Ghost Bear consolidated control of the former Rasalhague Republic, bringing Inner Sphere manufacturing capability to the design for the first time.

In the post-Jihad era, the Rasalhague Dominion continued Odin production at Alshain. Several homeworld facilities saw ownership changes as Clans were absorbed or restructured during the Wars of Reaving and subsequent political realignments. The design's simplicity and proven track record ensured it remained in production wherever Clan military forces required dedicated reconnaissance assets.`,{front:6,left:6,right:6,rear:4,turret:6,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},`ammo-clan-narc-standard`,`GuideRite w/Laser Coordination Link`,{engine:`HeavyLoad 285 ICE with PowerBoost`,armor:`SimplePlate Manufacturers Standard`},`The Ontos was one of three prototype designs built by Grumman Amalgamated, chosen for full production in 2832 because its primary energy weapon armament never required ammunition resupply during extended engagements. Reportedly inspired by an ancient Terran vehicle of the same name, the tank earned a reputation as a formidable close-range combatant valued for its ability to sustain fire long after ammunition-dependent vehicles had exhausted their stores.

Produced at Grumman's facility on Shiro III and later by Kallon Weapon Industries on Nanking, the Ontos became a staple of heavy armor formations throughout the Inner Sphere. The Free Worlds League in particular embraced the design, making it a backbone of Marik heavy tank regiments. Despite its slow speed and limited engagement range, the Ontos remains in production centuries after its introduction, a sign of the enduring value of its design philosophy.`,`Weighing 95 tons, the Ontos moves at a modest 54 km/h on its tracked drivetrain, powered by a HeavyLoad 285 internal combustion engine with PowerBoost augmentation. The tank's core armament consists of eight turret-mounted medium lasers, a configuration that demands extensive support systems. More than three times the weight and space occupied by the lasers themselves is devoted to power amplifiers and heat sinks necessary to prevent the ICE from overheating.

Early production models suffered from heat buildup that could overwhelm the power amplifiers when all lasers fired simultaneously, a flaw corrected by implementing a sequential firing system. Despite these inefficiencies, the energy weapon loadout grants the Ontos a significant logistical advantage, allowing crews to sustain operations through extended campaigns without resupply. Two LongFire LRM 5 racks mounted above the laser array provide limited long-range fire support, enabling the tank to engage targets from cover during its slow advance into effective laser range. Standard armor provides reasonable protection for its weight class, though the tonnage devoted to the weapon support systems limits the available coverage.`,`The Ontos originated on Shiro III, where Grumman Amalgamated developed three competing prototype heavy tanks during the late 29th century. According to popular lore, one designer drew inspiration from an ancient Terran combat vehicle, much to the amusement of his colleagues. All three prototypes performed well during extensive testing, but Grumman executives selected the Ontos after observing that rival designs exhausted their ammunition bins at least four times during several hours of constant-fire testing, while the Ontos required only minimal missile resupply. The only delay to full production was solving the heat dissipation problem that threatened to damage the power amplifiers.

By the time the Succession Wars reshaped the Inner Sphere, the Ontos had spread far beyond Grumman's production lines. Kallon Weapon Industries established a second manufacturing line on Nanking, significantly expanding the tank's availability. When the Free Worlds League captured Shiro III from the Capellan Confederation during the early Succession Wars, they gained direct control of Grumman Amalgamated's primary facility. The League embraced the Ontos wholeheartedly, and the tank became a cornerstone of Marik heavy armor doctrine, with larger-than-average vehicle formations providing the support needed to offset the design's limited range.

The Ontos proved particularly effective in ambush roles. Commanders learned to position the tanks in concealed locations while BattleMechs drew enemy forces into prepared kill zones. Numerous accounts describe entire formations being destroyed in moments by hidden Ontos companies. One notable engagement occurred on Nanking, where a company of Ontos tanks defended against Marik raiders testing the Federated Commonwealth's strength. Both sides deployed Ontos companies, and the resulting clash saw BattleMech forces on each side annihilated by the opposing tanks before the armor units turned on one another directly. Only three operational tanks survived the battle, convincing the Marik raiders to abandon their assault.

The technological renaissance of the 3050s breathed new life into the design. In 3053, Grumman Amalgamated undertook a comprehensive modernization, replacing the original ICE powerplant with a fusion engine and upgrading the weapons suite with pulse lasers, an integrated missile system, and modern ferro-fibrous armor. This upgrade addressed longstanding complaints about heat management and insufficient firepower at range. The upgraded Ontos proved its worth against the Clans as an effective Elemental hunter in close quarters and a reliable stopgap against Clan advances. It also gained a mixed reputation during the FedCom Civil War, when its urban deployment profile often put Ontos tanks close to NewsCams.

A light Gauss variant followed in 3065, trading the characteristic laser battery for long-range ballistic weapons. Grumman initially restricted sales to the Capellan Confederation and Free Worlds League, generating considerable commercial interest. During the Jihad, Grumman introduced variants with multi-missile launchers and environmental sealing, responding to the increasingly unpredictable battlefields of that conflict.

The Ontos HEAT variant emerged from an unlikely source. The Quikscell Company on Richvale acquired components and design data from the struggling Irian BattleMechs Unlimited facilities on Shiro III, purchasing stockpiled equipment at drastically reduced prices. Grumman Amalgamated responded with outrage, launching lawsuits alleging intellectual property theft. The legal dispute centered on the Ontos-X, an experimental prototype tested on Despair in 3072 by a consortium of Lyran corporations. Grumman claimed this unlicensed prototype formed the basis for Quikscell's design. Despite the litigation, Quikscell continued production.

In the ilClan era, Grumman answered Quikscell's competition by opening a new factory on Trellisane within the Wolf Empire. Subsidized by Wolf merchant caste interests and funded through Free Guild investment, this facility produced the Chemical Laser variant, mounting large chemical lasers in place of the traditional medium laser battery. The new armament dramatically extended the Ontos's effective range while maintaining its signature volume of fire.`,[`medium-laser`,`turret`,`#2`,null],[`medium-laser`,`turret`,`#3`,null],[`medium-laser`,`turret`,`#4`,null],[`medium-laser`,`turret`,`#5`,null],[`medium-laser`,`turret`,`#6`,null],[`medium-laser`,`turret`,`#7`,null],[`medium-laser`,`turret`,`#8`,null],[`medium-laser`,`turret`,`#9`,null],{engine:`General Motors 285 Cold-Start Fusion`,armor:`Durallex Light Ferro-Fibrous`},`The Ontos was one of three prototype designs built by Grumman Amalgamated, chosen for full production in 2832 because its primary energy weapon armament never required ammunition resupply during extended engagements. Reportedly inspired by an ancient Terran vehicle of the same name, the tank earned a reputation as a formidable close-range combatant valued for its ability to sustain fire long after ammunition-dependent vehicles had exhausted their stores.

Produced at Grumman's facility on Shiro III and later by Kallon Weapon Industries on Nanking, the Ontos became a staple of heavy armor formations throughout the Inner Sphere. The Free Worlds League in particular embraced the design, making it a backbone of Marik heavy tank regiments. Despite its slow speed and limited engagement range, the Ontos remains in production centuries after its introduction, a reflection of the enduring value of its design philosophy.`,{front:40,left:32,right:32,rear:30,turret:36,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`medium-laser`,`#1`,`turret`,null],[`medium-laser`,`#2`,`turret`,null],[`medium-laser`,`#3`,`turret`,null],`clan-large-chemical-laser`,`ammo-clan-large-chemical-laser-standard`,`SimplePlate Manufacturers Standard`,`The Ontos was one of three prototype designs built by Grumman Amalgamated, chosen for full production in 2832 because its primary energy weapon armament never required ammunition resupply during extended engagements. Reportedly inspired by an ancient Terran vehicle of the same name, the tank earned a reputation as a formidable close-range combatant valued for its ability to sustain fire long after ammunition-dependent vehicles had exhausted their stores.

Produced at Grumman's facility on Shiro III and later by Kallon Weapon Industries on Nanking, the Ontos became a staple of heavy armor formations throughout the Inner Sphere. The Free Worlds League in particular embraced the design, making it a backbone of Marik heavy tank regiments. Despite its slow speed and limited engagement range, the Ontos remains in production centuries after its introduction, proof of the enduring value of its design philosophy.`,`GuideRite with Laser Coordination Link`,`The Ontos was one of three prototype designs built by Grumman Amalgamated, chosen for full production in 2832 because its primary energy weapon armament never required ammunition resupply during extended engagements. Reportedly inspired by an ancient Terran vehicle of the same name, the tank earned a reputation as a formidable close-range combatant valued for its ability to sustain fire long after ammunition-dependent vehicles had exhausted their stores.

Produced at Grumman's facility on Shiro III and later by Kallon Weapon Industries on Nanking, the Ontos became a staple of heavy armor formations throughout the Inner Sphere. The Free Worlds League in particular embraced the design, making it a backbone of Marik heavy tank regiments. Despite its slow speed and limited engagement range, the Ontos remains in production centuries after its introduction, evidence of the enduring value of its design philosophy.`,{manufacturer:`Grumman Amalgamated|Refit - Various`,primaryFactory:`Shiro III|Various`,communications:`Easy Talk`,targeting:`GuideRite with Laser Coordination Link`},[`er-medium-laser`,`#6`,`turret`,null],[`environmental-sealing`]],designs:JSON.parse(`{"450":{"tro":{"production":"~0","components":"~1","text":{"overview":"~2","capabilities":"~3","deployment":"A field modification observed across multiple commands, this variant replaces the twin machine guns with a single medium laser, power amplifier, and heat sinks. The change addresses widespread complaints about the standard armament, but the extra equipment occupies most of the available cargo space and sharply reduces the vehicle's transport usefulness. Most examples originate from field conversions rather than factory work. The variant has been observed in service with forces well beyond the Dragoons, reflecting the scattered distribution of Kestrels through the secondary market.","battleHistory":"~4"}},"name":"Kestrel VTOL","model":"(ML)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~5","era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":12,"armorAllocation":"~6","equipment":"~7"},"451":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Blackwell Corporation","primaryFactory":"Outreach","communications":"~8","targeting":"Garret E2b"},"components":"~1","text":{"overview":"~2","capabilities":"~3","deployment":"Possibly the rarest VTOL variant in the Inner Sphere, the Kestrel Scout was developed by Blackwell for the Seventh Kommando and Special Recon Group around 3030. The troop compartment was removed entirely to accommodate a VTOL mast mount carrying a sophisticated camera system, a remote sensor dispenser, and additional communications equipment. A VTOL jet booster provided burst speeds approaching 260 kilometers per hour. Only a handful were ever built, and production ceased when the Dragoons' reorganization folded the Special Recon Group into the New Home Guards. The variant's limited armor and armament rendered it useless in any role other than pure reconnaissance.","battleHistory":"~4"}},"quirks":"~9","name":"Kestrel VTOL","model":"(Scout)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":12,"armorAllocation":"~6","equipment":{"$k":"~10","$v":["~11",["vtol-jet-booster","#1",null,null,null],["machine-gun","#2","front",null,null],"~12",["~13","#4","rear",null,null],["vtol-mast-mount","#5","rotor",null,null],["~14","#6",null,true,"machine-gun"]]}},"452":{"tro":{"production":"~0","components":"~1","text":{"overview":"~2","capabilities":"~3","deployment":"The small laser conversion replaces the machine guns with paired small lasers, power amplifier, and heat sinks, offering marginally better anti-infantry capability without the ammunition dependency of the standard armament. Like the other Kestrel weapon variants, the added equipment significantly reduces available cargo space. This modification appears to have originated as a field solution and has been documented in service with several commands that acquired Kestrels through the secondary market.","battleHistory":"~4"}},"name":"Kestrel VTOL","model":"(SL)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":1.5,"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":12,"armorAllocation":"~6","equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":[["small-laser","front","#0"],["small-laser","front","#1"]]}},"453":{"tro":{"production":"~0","components":"~1","text":{"overview":"~2","capabilities":"~3","deployment":"The SRM variant replaces the machine guns with an SRM-2 and a ton of ammunition, providing the most meaningful anti-vehicle capability of any Kestrel field conversion. The weight and space requirements of both the launcher and its ammunition load substantially reduce the vehicle's transport capacity. This is the most recent of the field modification series and has been documented across a wide range of factions, consistent with the broad distribution of Kestrels through combat losses and sales over the decades.","battleHistory":"~4"}},"name":"Kestrel VTOL","model":"(SRM)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":3,"era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":12,"armorAllocation":"~6","equipment":"~16"},"454":{"tro":{"production":"~17","components":{"chassis":"Aldis Industries","engine":"GM 350 XL","armor":"~18"},"text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"The PPC variant is fielded by commanders operating within established C3 networks, where the slave computer's targeting data sharing offsets the loss of the LB autocannon's ammunition flexibility. Three machine guns improve its performance against infantry and battle armor at close range. It is most effective in combined-arms formations where multiple C3-equipped units can share targeting locks, substantially improving hit probability for the entire group. Units operating independently or outside C3 networks generally prefer the standard variant's broader munitions options.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Kinnol MBT","model":"(PPC)","tonnage":70,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":65,"left":40,"right":40,"rear":30,"turret":40,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["c3-computer-slave","#1",null,null],"~23",["mml-5","#3","front",null],"~24",["machine-gun","#5","front",null],["standard-ppc","#6","turret",null],"~25","~26",["ammo-lrm-standard","#9",null,"mml-5"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#10",null,"mml-5"],["ammo-srm-standard","#11",null,"mml-5"],["~14","#12",null,"machine-gun"]]}},"455":{"tro":{"production":"~17","components":{"chassis":"Aldis Industries","engine":"GM 350 XL"},"text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"The RAC variant is deployed in the Dark Age era against the lighter, faster opponents that became prevalent as large standing armies fragmented. The rotary AC/5 provides high sustained fire output, effective against infantry carriers, light vehicles, and exposed battle armor. The reactive armor suite offers improved resistance to missile-heavy threats common among irregular forces, though it reduces protection against energy weapon attacks. Commanders typically deploy this variant in environments dominated by missile-equipped adversaries where the reactive armor's benefits outweigh its drawbacks.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Kinnol MBT","model":"(RAC)","tonnage":70,"era":"late-rep","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"reactive","armorAllocation":{"front":66,"left":40,"right":40,"rear":30,"turret":40,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["mml-5","#1","front",null],"~23","~27",["ammo-lrm-standard","#4",null,"mml-5"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#5",null,"mml-5"],"~28","~29",["~30","#8",null,"rotary-ac-5"],["ammo-srm-standard","#9",null,"mml-5"]]}},"456":{"tro":{"production":"~31","components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell 140","armor":"~32"},"text":{"overview":"~33","capabilities":"~34","deployment":"The standard Kite entered service in 3105 and found buyers across Inner Sphere and Clan militaries alike, distributed through Clan Sea Fox commercial networks. It serves primarily in reconnaissance lances and Stars alongside artillery or aerospace assets, where its TAG and Active Probe capabilities provide the most value. The fuel cell powerplant suits it well for prolonged low-intensity operations where noise and thermal signature management matter, and Trellshire, SFF-NG1, and SFF-SH2 all produced this variant throughout the Dark Age period.","battleHistory":"~35"}},"quirks":"~36","name":"~37","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"hover","chassisModifications":["armored-motive"],"tech":"clan","era":"late-rep","engineType":"compact","cruiseMP":9,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~38","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~39",["angel-ecm","#1",null,null],["clan-srm-2","#2","front",null],["clan-srm-2","#3","front",null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#4","turret",null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#5","turret",null],["clan-light-tag","#6","turret",null],["~40","#7",null,"ap-gauss-rifle"],["ammo-srm-standard","#8",null,"clan-srm-2"]]}},"457":{"tro":{"production":"~31","components":{"armor":"~32"},"text":{"overview":"~33","capabilities":"~34","deployment":"The (Fusion) variant entered production in 3147, replacing the fuel cell powerplant with a fusion engine to improve operational range and flexibility in high-intensity combat environments. Operators accept the additional cost and maintenance burden for better performance in sustained operations. The Fusion variant is primarily marketed to Clan forces and well-resourced Inner Sphere commands with the logistics infrastructure to support the increased requirements, and all three production facilities shifted to manufacturing it during the ilClan era.","battleHistory":"~35"}},"quirks":"~36","name":"~37","model":"(Fusion)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"hover","chassisModifications":["armored-motive"],"tech":"clan","era":"dark-ages","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":9,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~38","equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":[["angel-ecm","#0",null],["~42","#1","turret"],["~42","#2","turret"]]}},"458":{"tro":{"production":"~43","components":{"engine":"140 Fusion","armor":"~44","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"~45","capabilities":"~46","deployment":"The Kokou serves primarily in garrison and resource protection roles, deployed in fixed defensive positions where its pulse laser armament's high rate of fire can be maximized and its limited mobility is not a tactical liability. Star Adder commanders assigned it to planetary garrisons and resource security tasks, using its reliable firepower to hold territory without committing more capable frontline assets. As the vehicle spread through inter-Clan trading and the Hell's Horses Block B production, its deployment profile broadened across multiple toumans in similar second-line garrison roles. Tacticians recommend pairing the Kokou with faster screening forces to protect against the flanking maneuvers that the vehicle's limited speed makes difficult to counter.","battleHistory":"~47"}},"name":"Kokou Defense Tank","tonnage":70,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":2,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":45,"left":30,"right":30,"rear":22,"turret":36,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~48"},"459":{"tro":{"production":"~43","components":{"engine":"140 XL Fusion","armor":"~44","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"~45","capabilities":"~46","deployment":"The XL variant retains the standard Kokou's weapons suite and protection while replacing the engine with an XL fusion plant, increasing the vehicle's cruising speed. This improvement addresses the most persistent criticism of the standard model, giving commanders the option to reposition between defensive lines or respond to rapidly developing threats without sacrificing firepower. The XL saw limited adoption among Star Adder and Hell's Horses formations that valued enhanced mobility, deployed in assignments requiring a combination of the Kokou's defensive firepower and greater strategic responsiveness than the standard engine allows.","battleHistory":"~47"}},"name":"Kokou Defense Tank","model":"(XL)","tonnage":70,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","engineType":"clan_xl","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":45,"left":30,"right":30,"rear":22,"turret":45,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~48"},"460":{"tro":{"production":"~49","components":{"engine":"DAV 50 Fusion","armor":"ProtecTech Heavy Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"~50","capabilities":"The Kruger's primary strength lies in its combination of speed and firepower for its weight class. A DAV 50 fusion engine propels the vehicle to a cruising speed of 75 kilometers per hour, with a maximum road speed approaching 120 kilometers per hour on improved surfaces. Two Bright-Bloom Extended Range Medium Lasers provide a reach beyond what many light vehicles can match, enabling the Kruger to engage targets at distance before withdrawing. The Broadstar Micro-Pulse communications suite is well-regarded in reconnaissance roles, supporting the vehicle's primary mission of gathering and relaying battlefield information, and the Broadstar Pintel-Star targeting system complements it with reliable acquisition performance.\\n\\nThe design carries significant trade-offs. ProtecTech Heavy Ferro-Fibrous armor provides reasonable protection for the vehicle's weight class, but the Kruger is a reconnaissance asset, not a line combatant. Its light protection makes sustained engagement against heavier vehicles or concentrated fire dangerous, reinforcing the doctrine that Kruger crews should report contact rather than fight for information. The wheeled drive train performs exceptionally on improved roads and hard terrain, giving the vehicle its impressive top speed, but limits cross-country capability in forests, soft ground, and water obstacles that tracked or hover designs handle more easily.","deployment":"The modern Kruger is distributed primarily among Federated Suns planetary militia and March Militia commands across the spinward half of the Inner Sphere, with smaller numbers in nearby Periphery border states seeking affordable light reconnaissance assets. The design functions best in urban and open terrain, where its road speed and communications range can be fully exploited. Scout platoon commanders assign Krugers to advance reconnaissance, flank screening, and messenger duties when electronic communications are compromised by jamming or terrain. Most Krugers operate in pairs or small sections rather than independently, relying on mutual support when contact with heavier forces becomes unavoidable. Since the transition of the New Avalon facility to Draconis Combine administration, Krugers have begun appearing in DC garrison formations alongside units inherited from the Federated Suns.","battleHistory":"~51"}},"quirks":"~52","name":"Kruger Combat Car","tonnage":10,"motiveType":"wheeled","era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":7,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":16,"left":13,"right":13,"rear":14,"turret":13,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":[["er-medium-laser","turret","#0"],["er-medium-laser","turret","#1"]]}},"461":{"tro":{"production":"~49","components":{"engine":"DAV 50 Fusion","armor":"ProtecTech"},"text":{"overview":"~50","capabilities":"The Original Kruger shares the modern vehicle's chassis, drive train, and communications suite, but replaces advanced components with conventional alternatives to reduce maintenance demands. The same DAV 50 fusion engine provides identical speed performance, and the Broadstar Micro-Pulse and Pintel-Star systems give crews the same communications and targeting capability as the updated model. Two standard medium lasers replace the extended-range variants, shortening effective engagement range but reducing energy weapon upkeep requirements. Standard armor protection replaces the ferro-fibrous plating of the modern variant, accepting reduced protection in exchange for simplified repair logistics.\\n\\nThe trade-offs are meaningful in contested environments. The shorter reach of its conventional lasers limits the Original to engagements at closer ranges, and the reduced armor makes standing fights with even modestly armed opponents inadvisable. As a pure scout, the Original performs well, relying on speed and communications rather than firepower. It is best suited to garrison and patrol missions where the probability of heavy contact is low and logistical simplicity outweighs the tactical advantages of advanced components.","deployment":"The Original Kruger is offered by Avalon Arms as an alternative for buyers lacking the logistical infrastructure to maintain the full-technology combat car. It is most common in lower-technology garrison commands and Periphery border forces where advanced armor repair and energy weapon calibration present significant supply challenges. The Original retains the same communications and drive systems as the modern variant, maintaining equivalent speed and reconnaissance capability, but its standard protection and conventional lasers reduce maintenance complexity. For commands that need a fast scout with reliable electronics but cannot sustain the demands of ferro-fibrous armor and extended-range weapon upkeep, the Original offers a practical alternative with a lower logistical footprint.","battleHistory":"~51"}},"quirks":"~52","name":"Kruger Combat Car","model":"(Original)","tonnage":10,"motiveType":"wheeled","era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":{"front":15,"left":10,"right":10,"rear":11,"turret":10,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":[["medium-laser","turret","#0"],["medium-laser","turret","#1"]]}},"462":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Industrial Complex Raven Rho|New Kent Armorworks|Roche VehiclePlex Gamma|Sheridan CV-T3-6|Swedenborg Heavy Industries","primaryFactory":"Hellgate|New Kent (Celebes)|Roche|Sheridan|Kirchbach","communications":"Build 1685 Tacticom","targeting":"Series VI Integrated TTS"},"components":{"engine":"180 Fusion","armor":"Magnum Ferro-Fibrous Composite"},"text":{"overview":"The Ku Wheeled Assault Tank is a Clan combat vehicle developed in 2842 from the SLDF Turhan hull, redesigned for straightforward close-assault operations. Built to close with and destroy enemy formations, the Ku prioritizes offensive firepower and crew endurance over defensive complexity. Produced initially across several Clan Homeworld facilities and later adopted by Clan Hell's Horses for their Inner Sphere campaigns, the Ku has served multiple Clans as a durable and proven assault platform. Though not a technically sophisticated design, its combination of extended-range weaponry, protected crew spaces, and reasonable speed makes it effective in the roles Clan armored forces demand. The (IFV) ‘Turhan II’ variant, developed by the Horses late in the Jihad period, stands as a more capable but expensive evolution of the same combat philosophy.","capabilities":"Powered by a class-180 fusion plant, the Ku achieves a flanking speed of 65 kph, allowing it to keep pace with wheeled and tracked formations across improved terrain. The wheeled drive system performs well on roads and urban surfaces but limits cross-country mobility, restricting the Ku to environments where the terrain cooperates with its movement profile.\\n\\nThe Ku mounts four complementary weapon systems designed to cover multiple engagement ranges. A Type 9 Ultra Autocannon 10 forms the primary battery, capable of variable burst fire that can concentrate damage or sustain rate of fire as the situation demands. A Series 7J Extended Range Large Laser provides long-range precision strike capability. A Pattern JX Streak-4 SRM Launcher adds reliable medium-range punch without wasting shots, while a Series 1 Extended Range Small Laser handles close targets. Seven tons of Magnum ferro-fibrous composite armor is distributed with a slight forward bias, appropriate for an assault vehicle expected to advance into contact. The crew compartment is fully pressurized for hostile atmosphere operations, equipped with a heating element, field rations storage, and fold-down crew bunks that allow the vehicle to operate away from logistical support for extended periods. Sanitary facilities are limited, requiring crews to develop strict field procedures to maintain crew effectiveness on long deployments.","deployment":"The Ku saw wide service across the Clan Homeworlds, deployed by multiple Clans from factory lines on Hellgate, New Kent, Roche, and Sheridan. In the Inner Sphere, Clan Hell's Horses fields the Ku through their Kirchbach production line, integrating it into armored Trinaries alongside OmniMechs and other wheeled vehicles. The design suits the Horse preference for cost-effective, high-firepower ground vehicles that reduce dependence on BattleMech production.","battleHistory":"The Ku traces its origins to the SLDF Turhan assault vehicle, which early Clan forces retained from the Pentagon exodus. Clan engineers redesigned the platform in 2842, producing a purpose-built assault tank with a singular mandate: close with the enemy and inflict maximum damage. The redesign retained the Turhan's basic hull and crew systems while replacing its weapons with a direct-fire suite suited to Clan tactical doctrine.\\n\\nProduction was established at multiple Homeworld facilities, including Industrial Complex Raven Rho on Hellgate, New Kent Armorworks on New Kent, Roche VehiclePlex Gamma on Roche, and Sheridan CV-T3-6 on Sheridan. These lines supplied Ku tanks to Clan Snow Raven, Clan Sea Fox, Clan Cloud Cobra, and Clan Star Adder over the centuries, with ownership of individual factories shifting as Clans contested resources across the Homeworlds.\\n\\nThe Ku's best-documented engagement came on Arcadia in 3002, where Clans Ghost Bear and Steel Viper clashed over a manufacturing complex. The Vipers assigned only a vehicle Trinary from the Ninety-third Viper Assault Cluster to defend the site, considering it a low priority. A Star of Ku assault tanks crippled or destroyed an equal number of Ghost Bear OmniMechs before the Vipers ultimately yielded control of the facility, a result that briefly elevated the Ku's reputation within the Homeworld military community.\\n\\nClan Hell's Horses brought the Ku to the Inner Sphere when they began production at Swedenborg Heavy Industries on Kirchbach in 3072, followed by the (IFV) ‘Turhan II’ variant in 3074. The Turhan II shares parts commonality with the Enyo, reducing supply complexity for the Horses' logistics chain. The Horses have shown limited interest in selling either version, keeping the Ku largely within their own touman outside of salvage."}},"name":"Ku Wheeled Assault Tank","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wheeled","tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":29,"right":29,"rear":26,"turret":20,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["clan-streak-srm-4","front","#0",null],["clan-er-large-laser","turret","#1",null],["er-small-laser-clan","turret","#2",null],["clan-autocannon-uac-10","turret","#3",null],["~54",null,"#4","clan-streak-srm-4"],["~55",null,"#5","clan-autocannon-uac-10"],["~55",null,"#6","clan-autocannon-uac-10"]]}},"463":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Vandenberg Mechanized Industries","primaryFactory":"Pinard","communications":"O/P AIR 19","targeting":"O/P 411"},"components":{"engine":"Vlar 130 Fusion","armor":"Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/2"},"text":{"overview":"The Lamprey Transport Helicopter is a 30-ton VTOL infantry transport developed by Vandenberg Mechanized Industries on Pinard for the Taurian Concordat. Introduced in 3119, it was purpose-built to solve one of the most persistent problems in combined-arms warfare: delivering and extracting infantry under fire. Vandenberg prioritized reliability, ease of maintenance, and battlefield toughness above all other design goals. The result is a vehicle that can absorb considerable punishment while completing its mission. The Lamprey's reputation spread quickly after its combat debut with the Pleiades Hussars, and subsequent action across multiple campaigns has driven exports beyond Taurian space. Mercenary and periphery units have embraced the design for the same qualities that endeared it to Taurian regulars: it delivers its passengers, it comes home, and it keeps flying.","capabilities":"The Lamprey's armament is built around the infantry support mission. Two Harpoon 4 SRM 4 launchers deliver sustained suppressive fire against light vehicles and infantry, while a Scattergun machine gun discourages enemies from engaging at close range. The SRM loadout produces excellent damage per salvo for a vehicle of the Lamprey's size, making it effective at both pre-landing suppression and active fire support during troop insertion. The combination is lethal for clearing contested landing zones, though the Lamprey lacks the firepower to engage armored opposition effectively and should not be used as a dedicated anti-armor platform.\\n\\nA Vlar 130 fusion engine delivers exceptional speed for a 30-ton combat vehicle, with a cruising speed of 97 kilometers per hour and a maximum dash capability reaching 151 kilometers per hour. This speed is central to the design's survivability. The Lamprey is not built to absorb concentrated fire, and pilots rely on aggressive movement and minimal exposure time over hot landing zones to stay alive. StarSlab/2 composite armor provides adequate protection for the transport role, though the armor budget is modest by the standards of a purpose-built combat vehicle. Experienced operators describe the controls as unusually responsive and precise. Rookies, however, regularly return from missions with half a rotor destroyed or shredded control surfaces, a reminder that the design must be flown aggressively to realize its full potential.","deployment":"The Lamprey serves as a standard infantry transport VTOL in Taurian Concordat airmobile and combined-arms formations, assigned wherever rapid troop insertion and extraction under fire is required. Its armament allows it to provide organic fire support before, during, and after delivering its passengers, a capability that distinguishes it from lighter transports that cannot contribute to the fight itself. The design is well suited to contested airspace where conventional transports cannot loiter safely and where infantry must be placed precisely to exploit fleeting tactical opportunities.\\n\\nExport sales have established the Lamprey with several mercenary units and periphery factions. Favorable maintenance requirements and accessible parts availability suit organizations operating without deep supply chains, expanding the design's footprint well beyond its Taurian origins.","battleHistory":"The Lamprey's combat debut came during a pirate raid on Brockway in 3119, while elements of the Pleiades Hussars were conducting war games near the Taurian/Federated Suns border. The raiders destroyed a section of the twenty-five-kilometer Monterey-Lapland viaduct, stranding the bulk of the Hussars in the Lapland Archipelago and apparently cutting them out of the fight. Eight Lampreys overflew the Monterey spaceport, hammering the pirates with concentrated SRM fire and combat-dropping battle armor squads directly onto the tarmac. Ten minutes into the engagement, the Hussars had captured the pirate DropShip and turned its guns on the remaining raiders. The surviving pirates retreated into the surrounding hills, but the Lampreys proved equally capable in the pursuit phase: one flight harried the retreating units while the other airlifted conventional infantry into blocking positions. The Hussars ultimately destroyed one pirate lance and salvaged another. All eight Lampreys survived intact.\\n\\nThe design's record continued to grow in subsequent years. In 3139, Lampreys defending the Duchy of Andurien world of Shiro III faced elements of the resurrected Free Worlds League raiding the Grumman Amalgamated factory complex. Pressed into a delaying action while ground forces established defensive positions, the Lampreys exploited their speed and SRM armament aggressively, setting fires at key chokepoints and stalling the League advance. They returned to rearm and pick up battle armor squads, then rejoined the fight and helped force a Marik heavy BattleMech to retreat. The operation became a minor training case study in VTOL fire support doctrine.\\n\\nIn 3141, on Pompeii in the Marian Hegemony, a flight of Lampreys supported a two-week jungle sweep against entrenched guerrilla fighters. Operating over dense terrain, the Lampreys provided continuous overwatch for conventional infantry forces working through the jungle floor. When the guerrillas were finally cornered, the Lampreys caught them in the open and disabled one of a pair of modified IndustrialMechs the guerrillas had pressed into service, driving the other off with sustained fire. The engagement reinforced the Lamprey's reputation as a capable asset in low-intensity and counterinsurgency operations as well as conventional assaults.\\n\\nThe design's durability extended to individual airframes. One Lamprey serving with the Black Daisy mercenary company accumulated a string of engagements spanning decades without taking enemy fire damage, despite changing owners nearly forty times across the company's history. The vehicle's reputation for resilience became a minor legend in mercenary circles, a running example of Vandenberg's manufacturing quality outlasting the careers of the pilots who flew it."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"quirk-easy-to-pilot"}],"name":"Lamprey Transport Helicopter","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":4,"era":"late-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":9,"armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":20,"right":20,"rear":16,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~56","$v":[["srm-4","front","#0",null,null],["srm-4","front","#1",null,null],"~57",["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#3","srm-4",null],"~58"]}},"464":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Joint Equipment Systems|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Hadrian Mechanized Industries|Quikscell Company|Aldis Industries","primaryFactory":"Alshain|Ares|Kalidasa|Layover|Oliver|Pandora|Pompey|Richvale|Terra","communications":"Communicator","targeting":"~59"},"components":"~60","text":{"overview":"The Laser Carrier is a sixty-ton tracked combat vehicle that replaces the standard missile carrier's launchers with eight medium lasers. Introduced in 2874 during the Succession Wars as a field modification of the standard carrier chassis, the Laser Carrier was designed to eliminate ammunition dependency entirely. While never as popular or effective as the missile variants, the Laser Carrier found a niche with units operating in supply-poor environments where ammunition resupply could not be guaranteed.","capabilities":"The Laser Carrier mounts eight forward-firing medium lasers as its sole armament, drawing power from the InterComBust 180 engine's generator capacity. This configuration provides unlimited ammunition endurance, eliminating the resupply dependency that constrains missile and autocannon carriers. The vehicle retains the standard carrier chassis with fifty-four kilometer per hour top speed and forty-eight points of Simple Armor Plate Standard protection.\\n\\nThe medium laser battery's effective range is considerably shorter than LRM systems, limiting the Laser Carrier's ability to engage from standoff distances. Heat management poses a challenge during sustained fire, as the eight lasers generate substantial thermal load. A Communicator system and FireScan with IndirecTrack targeting provide standard coordination capability.","deployment":"The Laser Carrier serves across the Inner Sphere in units that prioritize sustained-fire capability over raw damage output. Production mirrors the missile carrier network, with Quikscell, Joint Equipment Systems, Aldis Industries, and Hadrian Mechanized Industries all contributing to a broad manufacturing base.","battleHistory":"The Laser Carrier emerged during the Succession Wars as units attempted to replace the missile systems on standard carrier chassis with other weapon types. The TRO:3039 account notes that 'a few units attempted to replace the missile systems with other weapon systems such as lasers or AC/2s, none were popular or as effective as the original.' Despite this assessment, the Laser Carrier entered production at Quikscell facilities and other manufacturers by 2874.\\n\\nThe design's chief advantage is unlimited ammunition endurance, as the eight medium lasers draw power from the InterComBust 180 engine's generator capacity. Production spread across the same extensive network as the missile carriers, with Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Richvale, plus Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain, Aldis Industries on Terra, and Hadrian Mechanized Industries on Pompey. The Laser Carrier continues in service as a niche platform for sustained-fire operations where resupply is impractical."}},"name":"Laser Carrier","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"era":"early-sw","armorAllocation":"~61","equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":[["medium-laser","front","#0"],["medium-laser","front","#1"],["medium-laser","front","#2"],["medium-laser","front","#3"],["medium-laser","front","#4"],["medium-laser","front","#5"],["medium-laser","front","#6"],["medium-laser","front","#7"]]}},"465":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Majesty Metals and Manufacturing|Pinard Protectorates Limited","primaryFactory":"Dunianshire|Perdition","communications":"Communicator","targeting":"~59"},"components":"~62","text":"~63"},"name":"Light SRM Carrier","tonnage":40,"motiveType":"wheeled","era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":16,"left":14,"right":14,"rear":12,"turret":16,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~64","~65","~66",["srm-6","turret","#3",null],"~67","~68","~69","~70"]}},"466":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Quikscell Company","primaryFactory":"Broken Wheel","communications":"Communicator","targeting":"~59"},"components":"~62","text":"~63"},"name":"Light Thunderbolt Carrier","tonnage":40,"motiveType":"wheeled","era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":16,"left":14,"right":14,"rear":12,"turret":15,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["thunderbolt-5","turret","#0",null],["thunderbolt-5","turret","#1",null],["thunderbolt-5","turret","#2",null],["thunderbolt-5","turret","#3",null],["thunderbolt-5","turret","#4",null],["~71",null,"#5","thunderbolt-5"],["~71",null,"#6","thunderbolt-5"],["~71",null,"#7","thunderbolt-5"]]}},"467":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Curtiss Industries|Kressly Warworks","primaryFactory":"New Stevens|Epsilon Eridani","communications":"Century Model 770","targeting":"TGI 2331C/TGI F-190"},"components":"~72","text":{"overview":"~73","capabilities":"~74","deployment":"Originally fielded by SLDF armor and infantry regiments as a formation-disruption asset, the standard Lightning was designed for coordinated group attacks against slower opponents. The vehicle's one-shot missile profile made it ideal for rapid, aggressive strikes, though its limited endurance constrained it to short engagements or harassment operations requiring frequent withdrawal to rearm. Kressly Warworks resumed production of the original configuration alongside the newer Word of Blake variants in 3067, supplying it to Blakist militia and garrison formations throughout the Protectorate.","battleHistory":"~75"}},"name":"~76","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":11,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~77","equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":["~78","~79",["srm-4-os","right","#2"],["srm-4-os","left","#3"]]}},"468":{"tro":{"production":"~80","components":"~81","text":{"overview":"~73","capabilities":"~74","deployment":"One of several Jihad-era variants produced by Kressly Warworks under Word of Blake direction, the ERML replaces the original medium pulse lasers with a pair of extended-range medium lasers and substitutes the one-shot SRM racks with seven front-mounted Rocket Launcher 15 systems. The massed rocket pods provided devastating short-range firepower suited to the Lightning's pass-and-fire mission profile, while the extended-range lasers improved the vehicle's ability to engage targets beyond close combat distance.","battleHistory":"~75"}},"name":"~76","model":"(ERML)","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":11,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~77","equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":["~82","~83","~84","~85","~86","~87","~88",["er-medium-laser","front","#7"],["er-medium-laser","front","#8"]]}},"469":{"tro":{"production":"~80","components":"~81","text":{"overview":"~73","capabilities":"~74","deployment":"A minor modification of the ERML configuration, the ERSL variant trades one of the two extended-range medium lasers for a pair of extended-range small lasers. This swap slightly reduces the vehicle's reach but increases its close-range hit probability against fast-moving targets, making it better suited to engagements against light BattleMechs and other hovercraft.","battleHistory":"~75"}},"name":"~76","model":"(ERSL)","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":11,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~77","equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":["~82","~83","~84","~85","~86","~87","~88",["er-small-laser","front","#7"],["er-small-laser","front","#8"],["er-medium-laser","front","#9"]]}},"470":{"tro":{"production":"~80","components":"~81","text":{"overview":"~73","capabilities":"~74","deployment":"Developed by Precentor Robert Havasy to counter Capellan Shadow Lances equipped with stealth armor, the RL variant carries seven Rocket Launcher 15 racks and two anti-missile systems in place of the original armament. The anti-missile systems were intended to negate some of the advantages held by the Allied Mercenary Command, while the massed rocket pods exploited the vulnerability of stealth-armored BattleMechs to concentrated short-range fire before they could bring their electronic countermeasures to bear.","battleHistory":"~75"}},"name":"~76","model":"(RL)","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":11,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~77","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["rocket-launcher-15","front","#0",null],["rocket-launcher-15","front","#1",null],["rocket-launcher-15","front","#2",null],["rocket-launcher-15","front","#3",null],["rocket-launcher-15","front","#4",null],["rocket-launcher-15","front","#5",null],["rocket-launcher-15","front","#6",null],["is-ams","front","#7",null],["is-ams","front","#8",null],["ammo-is-ams-standard",null,"#9","is-ams"]]}},"471":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Curtiss Industries","primaryFactory":"New Stevens","communications":"Century Model 770","targeting":"TGI 2331C/TGI F-190"},"components":"~72","text":{"overview":"~73","capabilities":"~74","deployment":"Restricted to SLDF Royal units authorized access to upgraded equipment, the Royal variant replaced the expendable one-shot SRM-4 launchers with a pair of Streak SRM-2 systems and a single ton of ammunition, giving it sustained missile capability across multiple engagements. The addition of a TAG designator allowed the Royal Lightning to mark targets for artillery and semi-guided munitions from supporting formations. Production was limited to the Curtiss Industries facility on New Stevens and ceased during the Succession Wars.","battleHistory":"~75"}},"name":"~76","model":"(Royal)","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":11,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~77","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["is-tag","front","#0",null],["medium-pulse-laser","front","#1",null],["medium-pulse-laser","front","#2",null],["streak-srm-2","front","#3",null],["streak-srm-2","front","#4",null],["~89",null,"#5","streak-srm-2"]]}},"472":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Refit - Comstar","communications":"Century Model 770","targeting":"TGI 2331C/TGI F-190"},"components":{"chassis":"Curtiss Industries","engine":"General Motors GM 210"},"text":{"overview":"~73","capabilities":"~74","deployment":"A ComStar prototype developed during the Jihad, the CX-3 serves as a radical departure from the standard Lightning configuration. Three Rocket Launcher 15 systems are mounted in sponson turrets, providing wider firing arcs than the original fixed-mount armament. The vehicle is protected by vehicular stealth armor paired with a Guardian ECM Suite, dramatically reducing its electronic signature at the cost of nearly all original offensive capability. Only a single medium pulse laser is retained from the base design. Produced in unknown numbers at an undisclosed facility, the CX-3 was evaluated for potential mass production but was considered too specialized and expensive for widespread adoption.","battleHistory":"~75","notes":"Vehicle overweight and Heat wrong in TRO:. No errata at this time."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag"],"$v":[["quirk-easy-to-pilot"],["quirk-prototype"],"~90"]},"name":"~76","model":"CX-3","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"sponsonTurrets":true,"sponsonMounts":true,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":11,"armorType":"vehicular-stealth","armorAllocation":{"front":10,"left":8,"right":8,"rear":6,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","location","sponson"],"$v":["~91",["medium-pulse-laser","#1","front",null],["rocket-launcher-15","#2","right",true],["rocket-launcher-15","#3","right",true],["rocket-launcher-15","#4","right",true],["rocket-launcher-15","#5","left",true],["rocket-launcher-15","#6","left",true],["rocket-launcher-15","#7","left",true]]}},"473":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~92","primaryFactory":"~93","communications":"Communicator","targeting":"~59"},"components":"~60","text":{"overview":"~94","capabilities":"The LRM Carrier mounts three forward-firing FarFire LRM-20 launchers as its sole armament, a configuration that prioritizes raw missile volume over versatility. An InterComBust 180 internal combustion engine propels the vehicle to a maximum speed of fifty-four kilometers per hour, adequate for repositioning between fire missions but insufficient for mobile warfare. A Communicator system and FireScan with IndirecTrack targeting handle battlefield coordination, the latter being essential for the indirect fire role that defines the carrier concept.\\n\\nEight tons of ammunition provide sustained fire far longer than most comparable platforms, though this high rate of consumption demands reliable supply lines for extended operations. The vehicle carries only three tons of Simple Armor Plate Standard protection, its most critical weakness. Unable to withstand even modest counterbattery fire, the carrier relies entirely on standoff distance and terrain masking for survival. To broaden market appeal, the launching systems accept a wide variety of missile ammunition from different manufacturers, though Quikscell does not warranty vehicles loaded with non-FarFire munitions.","deployment":"Due to Quikscell's multiple plants scattered across the Inner Sphere, every major military fields LRM Carriers in significant numbers. The company's trade-in program during the War of 3039 eliminated most non-Quikscell carriers from mainline service, leaving only a handful in the hands of pirate groups or Periphery planets too poor to pay shipping expenses. The LRM Carrier typically serves in dedicated fire support lances, providing indirect missile barrages from concealed positions behind terrain features.","battleHistory":"The origins of the modern missile carrier stretch back to the Age of War, when Quik Products Inc developed the first standardized tracked platforms for massed missile delivery. These early designs, constructed using primitive technology, were crude but effective, establishing the basic formula of maximum firepower on a minimal chassis that would define the weapons carrier concept for centuries. As Quik Products evolved into the Quikscell Company, the original primitive carriers were refined into the modern designs that entered widespread service by the twenty-fifth century.\\n\\nDuring the Succession Wars, the desperate need for cheap fire support led dozens of manufacturers across the Inner Sphere to produce their own versions, many of them little more than unauthorized copies of Quikscell designs built to even lower quality standards. Shortly before the Fourth Succession War, Quikscell launched a series of lawsuits against these clone manufacturers, successfully shutting down many smaller competitors and forcing larger firms to cease production of derivative designs. The company further consolidated its market dominance by offering to exchange any non-Quikscell carrier for legitimate replacement vehicles and parts, effectively cornering the missile carrier market. One company not targeted by the legal attacks was Joint Equipment Systems, which had negotiated in good faith with Quik Products Inc centuries before.\\n\\nLRM Carrier production spread across an extensive network of facilities. Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Broken Wheel turned out thousands of units across every era. Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain, Aldis Industries on Terra, Amaris Armored Vehicles on Timbuktu, the King Dumount Defense Facility on Castile, and Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium on Maxwell all contributed to a production network spanning the entire Inner Sphere.\\n\\nThe first significant modernization came in 3055, when Quikscell introduced an upgraded variant for the Federated Commonwealth. By incorporating ferro-fibrous armor and Artemis IV fire control systems linked to each launcher, engineers dramatically improved missile accuracy while adding a C3 slave unit for networked targeting. The reduced engine size was considered an acceptable trade for the enhanced combat capability. Oliver and Richvale became the primary production sites for this upgrade package.\\n\\nThe Word of Blake produced their own radical revision during the Jihad era, utilizing an extralight fusion engine to boost both speed and armor protection while retaining Artemis IV systems and adding a C3i computer. Aldis Industries on Terra manufactured this variant throughout the Blakist occupation. In the Dark Age, Joint Equipment Systems developed the LRM Carrier C for the Rasalhague Dominion, mounting the launchers in a partial-traverse turret with hardened armor inspired by the success of the Viking IIC. The LRM Carrier's continued evolution across more than six centuries of service stands as sign of the enduring viability of cheap, massed missile platforms in modern warfare."}},"name":"LRM Carrier","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":"~61","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["lrm-20","front","#0",null],["lrm-20","front","#1",null],["lrm-20","front","#2",null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#3","lrm-20"],"~95","~96","~97"]}},"474":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company","primaryFactory":"Oliver|Richvale","communications":"Communicator with C3 Link","targeting":"FireScan with IndirecTrack and Artemis FCS"},"components":{"engine":"InterComBust 120 ICE","armor":"~98"},"text":{"overview":"~94","capabilities":"The 3055 upgrade significantly enhanced the LRM Carrier's combat effectiveness at the cost of mobility. The InterComBust 180 engine was replaced with a smaller 120-rated model, reducing top speed to just thirty-two kilometers per hour. This freed sufficient tonnage for Lexington Ltd. High Grade ferro-fibrous armor, nearly doubling the vehicle's protection, and Artemis IV fire control systems linked to each of the three FarFire LRM-20 launchers. The improved tracking dramatically increased missile accuracy against maneuvering targets.\\n\\nA C3 slave unit enables networked targeting data from friendly units, compensating for the carrier's reduced ability to reposition. Ammunition capacity increased to twelve tons, providing extended fire endurance. The slower speed was considered acceptable for a vehicle designed to remain in a fixed firing position, and the combination of improved accuracy and survivability made the upgrade popular across multiple militaries.","deployment":"The upgraded LRM Carrier entered service with Federated Commonwealth regimental combat teams beginning in 3055. When the Free Worlds League conquered Oliver during Operation Guerrero, sales opened to the rest of the Inner Sphere, and the upgraded carriers spread to most major vehicle regiments including Periphery forces. Oliver and Richvale served as the primary production sites for the upgrade package.","battleHistory":"The origins of the modern missile carrier stretch back to the Age of War, when Quik Products Inc developed the first standardized tracked platforms for massed missile delivery. These early designs, constructed using primitive technology, were crude but effective, establishing the basic formula of maximum firepower on a minimal chassis that would define the weapons carrier concept for centuries. As Quik Products evolved into the Quikscell Company, the original primitive carriers were refined into the modern designs that entered widespread service by the twenty-fifth century.\\n\\nDuring the Succession Wars, the desperate need for cheap fire support led dozens of manufacturers across the Inner Sphere to produce their own versions, many of them little more than unauthorized copies of Quikscell designs built to even lower quality standards. Shortly before the Fourth Succession War, Quikscell launched a series of lawsuits against these clone manufacturers, successfully shutting down many smaller competitors and forcing larger firms to cease production of derivative designs. The company further consolidated its market dominance by offering to exchange any non-Quikscell carrier for legitimate replacement vehicles and parts, effectively cornering the missile carrier market. One company not targeted by the legal attacks was Joint Equipment Systems, which had negotiated in good faith with Quik Products Inc centuries before.\\n\\nLRM Carrier production spread across an extensive network of facilities. Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Broken Wheel turned out thousands of units across every era. Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain, Aldis Industries on Terra, Amaris Armored Vehicles on Timbuktu, the King Dumount Defense Facility on Castile, and Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium on Maxwell all contributed to a production network spanning the entire Inner Sphere.\\n\\nThe first significant modernization came in 3055, when Quikscell introduced an upgraded variant for the Federated Commonwealth. By incorporating ferro-fibrous armor and Artemis IV fire control systems linked to each launcher, engineers dramatically improved missile accuracy while adding a C3 slave unit for networked targeting. The reduced engine size was considered an acceptable trade for the enhanced combat capability. Oliver and Richvale became the primary production sites for this upgrade package.\\n\\nThe Word of Blake produced their own radical revision during the Jihad era, utilizing an extralight fusion engine to boost both speed and armor protection while retaining Artemis IV systems and adding a C3i computer. Aldis Industries on Terra manufactured this variant throughout the Blakist occupation. In the Dark Age, Joint Equipment Systems developed the LRM Carrier C for the Rasalhague Dominion, mounting the launchers in a partial-traverse turret with hardened armor inspired by the success of the Viking IIC. The LRM Carrier's continued evolution across more than six centuries of service stands as proof of the enduring viability of cheap, massed missile platforms in modern warfare."}},"name":"LRM Carrier","model":"(3055 Upgrade)","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":2,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~99","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~100","~101","~102","~103","~104","~105","~106","~107","~108","~109"]}},"475":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Aldis Industries","primaryFactory":"Terra","communications":"with C3i","targeting":"with Artemis FCS"},"components":{"engine":"XL Fusion","armor":"Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"~94","capabilities":"The Word of Blake variant is a radical departure from the standard carrier concept. An extralight fusion engine replaced the original internal combustion powerplant, boosting top speed while freeing tonnage for substantially increased ferro-fibrous armor protection. A C3i computer provides improved networked targeting without the vulnerabilities of standard C3 systems, while Artemis IV fire control on each launcher maintains the accuracy improvements introduced by the 3055 upgrade.\\n\\nThe three LRM-20 launchers remain unchanged from the standard configuration, preserving the carrier's core fire support capability. The combination of increased speed, heavier armor, and advanced electronics transforms a disposable fire support platform into a more survivable battlefield asset, though the extralight engine makes field maintenance considerably more complex.","deployment":"The Word of Blake variant was suspected to be produced at a secret facility, as the extralight fusion engine refit would be extremely difficult to accomplish in the field. Aldis Industries on Terra is believed to have manufactured this variant during the Jihad era. Production ceased following the liberation of Terra in 3078.","battleHistory":"The origins of the modern missile carrier stretch back to the Age of War, when Quik Products Inc developed the first standardized tracked platforms for massed missile delivery. These early designs, constructed using primitive technology, were crude but effective, establishing the basic formula of maximum firepower on a minimal chassis that would define the weapons carrier concept for centuries. As Quik Products evolved into the Quikscell Company, the original primitive carriers were refined into the modern designs that entered widespread service by the twenty-fifth century.\\n\\nDuring the Succession Wars, the desperate need for cheap fire support led dozens of manufacturers across the Inner Sphere to produce their own versions, many of them little more than unauthorized copies of Quikscell designs built to even lower quality standards. Shortly before the Fourth Succession War, Quikscell launched a series of lawsuits against these clone manufacturers, successfully shutting down many smaller competitors and forcing larger firms to cease production of derivative designs. The company further consolidated its market dominance by offering to exchange any non-Quikscell carrier for legitimate replacement vehicles and parts, effectively cornering the missile carrier market. One company not targeted by the legal attacks was Joint Equipment Systems, which had negotiated in good faith with Quik Products Inc centuries before.\\n\\nLRM Carrier production spread across an extensive network of facilities. Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Broken Wheel turned out thousands of units across every era. Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain, Aldis Industries on Terra, Amaris Armored Vehicles on Timbuktu, the King Dumount Defense Facility on Castile, and Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium on Maxwell all contributed to a production network spanning the entire Inner Sphere.\\n\\nThe first significant modernization came in 3055, when Quikscell introduced an upgraded variant for the Federated Commonwealth. By incorporating ferro-fibrous armor and Artemis IV fire control systems linked to each launcher, engineers dramatically improved missile accuracy while adding a C3 slave unit for networked targeting. The reduced engine size was considered an acceptable trade for the enhanced combat capability. Oliver and Richvale became the primary production sites for this upgrade package.\\n\\nThe Word of Blake produced their own radical revision during the Jihad era, utilizing an extralight fusion engine to boost both speed and armor protection while retaining Artemis IV systems and adding a C3i computer. Aldis Industries on Terra manufactured this variant throughout the Blakist occupation. In the Dark Age, Joint Equipment Systems developed the LRM Carrier C for the Rasalhague Dominion, mounting the launchers in a partial-traverse turret with hardened armor inspired by the success of the Viking IIC. The LRM Carrier's continued evolution across more than six centuries of service stands as evidence of the enduring viability of cheap, massed missile platforms in modern warfare."}},"name":"LRM Carrier","model":"(WoB)","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"xl","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":20,"left":18,"right":18,"rear":15,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["c3i-computer","#0",null,null],"~101","~102","~103","~104","~105","~106","~107","~108","~109"]}},"476":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~92","primaryFactory":"~93"},"components":{"engine":"240 ICE","armor":"Heavy Ferro-Fibrous (Clan)"},"text":{"overview":"~94","capabilities":"The LRM Carrier C mounts its three LRM-20 launchers in a partial-traverse turret, dramatically expanding the vehicle's field of fire compared to the forward-fixed configuration of all previous variants. An upgraded 240-rated internal combustion engine increases ground speed to approximately forty-three kilometers per hour, improving tactical mobility. The launchers accept Clan-specification ammunition, increasing damage per missile compared to Inner Sphere equivalents.\\n\\nHardened armor inspired by the Viking IIC provides robust protection capable of weathering Elemental swarms prevalent in Clan warfare, though the increased weight of hardened plating limits total coverage. The semi-modular turret design allows for quick weapon swaps or rapid field repairs, a practical feature that has made the variant popular with mercenary commands as well as Clan forces.","deployment":"Joint Equipment Systems developed the LRM Carrier C for the Rasalhague Dominion following the Jihad, when the devastated Bear touman needed upgraded hardware. The success of the Viking IIC's hardened armor inspired the design. Quikscell plants on Kalidasa and Pandora also produce the C variant, ensuring availability beyond Dominion borders. Other Clans quickly trialed for or copied the innovations, and mercenary commands have embraced the design for its modular turret system.","battleHistory":"The origins of the modern missile carrier stretch back to the Age of War, when Quik Products Inc developed the first standardized tracked platforms for massed missile delivery. These early designs, constructed using primitive technology, were crude but effective, establishing the basic formula of maximum firepower on a minimal chassis that would define the weapons carrier concept for centuries. As Quik Products evolved into the Quikscell Company, the original primitive carriers were refined into the modern designs that entered widespread service by the twenty-fifth century.\\n\\nDuring the Succession Wars, the desperate need for cheap fire support led dozens of manufacturers across the Inner Sphere to produce their own versions, many of them little more than unauthorized copies of Quikscell designs built to even lower quality standards. Shortly before the Fourth Succession War, Quikscell launched a series of lawsuits against these clone manufacturers, successfully shutting down many smaller competitors and forcing larger firms to cease production of derivative designs. The company further consolidated its market dominance by offering to exchange any non-Quikscell carrier for legitimate replacement vehicles and parts, effectively cornering the missile carrier market. One company not targeted by the legal attacks was Joint Equipment Systems, which had negotiated in good faith with Quik Products Inc centuries before.\\n\\nLRM Carrier production spread across an extensive network of facilities. Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Broken Wheel turned out thousands of units across every era. Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain, Aldis Industries on Terra, Amaris Armored Vehicles on Timbuktu, the King Dumount Defense Facility on Castile, and Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium on Maxwell all contributed to a production network spanning the entire Inner Sphere.\\n\\nThe first significant modernization came in 3055, when Quikscell introduced an upgraded variant for the Federated Commonwealth. By incorporating ferro-fibrous armor and Artemis IV fire control systems linked to each launcher, engineers dramatically improved missile accuracy while adding a C3 slave unit for networked targeting. The reduced engine size was considered an acceptable trade for the enhanced combat capability. Oliver and Richvale became the primary production sites for this upgrade package.\\n\\nThe Word of Blake produced their own radical revision during the Jihad era, utilizing an extralight fusion engine to boost both speed and armor protection while retaining Artemis IV systems and adding a C3i computer. Aldis Industries on Terra manufactured this variant throughout the Blakist occupation. In the Dark Age, Joint Equipment Systems developed the LRM Carrier C for the Rasalhague Dominion, mounting the launchers in a partial-traverse turret with hardened armor inspired by the success of the Viking IIC. The LRM Carrier's continued evolution across more than six centuries of service stands as reflection of the enduring viability of cheap, massed missile platforms in modern warfare."}},"name":"LRM Carrier","model":"C","tonnage":60,"tech":"clan","era":"late-rep","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"hardened","armorAllocation":{"front":12,"left":12,"right":12,"rear":12,"turret":12,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["clan-lrm-20","turret","#0",null],["clan-lrm-20","turret","#1",null],["clan-lrm-20","turret","#2",null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#3","clan-lrm-20"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#4","clan-lrm-20"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#5","clan-lrm-20"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#6","clan-lrm-20"]]}},"477":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Novis-AT-H, Inc.","primaryFactory":"Detroit","communications":"Ceres Metals Model 777 with Angel ECM Suite","targeting":"Apple Churchill 3000 with TAG and Bloodhound Active Probe"},"components":{"engine":"Magna 145","armor":"Hellespont Lite Stealth"},"text":{"overview":"The Luduan Scout Vehicle is a 25-ton Capellan hovercraft built by Novis-AT-H, Inc. on Detroit. Introduced in 3136, the design was conceived as a direct response to weaknesses exposed during the Capellan Crusades, where the CCAF's emphasis on raw firepower had left the Confederation dangerously short of specialized reconnaissance and electronic warfare platforms. Rather than a frontline combatant, the Luduan serves as a force multiplier, providing target designation and sensor coverage to combined-arms formations. Its ability to detect hidden enemies while shielding friendly forces from detection made it an immediate success, and the design spread rapidly through the Confederation's military after entering service.","capabilities":"The Luduan's mission revolves around detection and designation rather than direct engagement. A Bloodhound Active Probe identifies even well-concealed enemy units, while a turret-mounted TAG laser designator calls in precision artillery and missile strikes from supporting formations. An Angel ECM Suite rounds out the electronics package, jamming enemy sensors and shielding nearby friendly forces from detection. This combination of active and passive electronic warfare makes the Luduan exceptionally effective at both locating hidden opponents and concealing allied movements. RAF commanders learned to expect significantly increased artillery accuracy whenever Luduans appeared on the battlefield.\\n\\nOffensive armament is deliberately minimal. Three turret-mounted Mydron Mini Magshot Gauss Rifles provide self-defense capability, drawing from a single ton of ammunition stored in the vehicle's body. The Magshots can discourage unarmored infantry or lightly protected targets, but lack the hitting power to threaten most combat vehicles or BattleMechs. The Magna 145 fusion engine pushes the hovercraft past 180 kilometers per hour, giving it the speed to reposition quickly between spotting runs. Hellespont Lite Stealth armor reduces the vehicle's sensor signature, though actual protection is light at only three tons. Survival depends entirely on speed, stealth, and avoiding direct fire rather than absorbing it.","deployment":"The Luduan's specialized mission means it is not required in large numbers, but its presence has spread to nearly every formation in the Confederation since entering service. Militia units commonly employ Luduans as scouts or attach them to artillery vehicle platoons, where the TAG designator directly multiplies the platoon's effectiveness. More prestigious outfits pair them with Arrow IV-equipped BattleMech lances, creating integrated fire-support teams capable of devastating precision strikes. The Confederation's rapid territorial gains into Republic and Federated Suns space have generated new tactical demands, as CCAF garrisons on newly conquered worlds rely on the Luduan's sensor suite to locate and suppress resistance activity across populations that bitterly oppose Capellan rule.","battleHistory":"The Capellan Crusades of the early thirty-second century revealed critical deficiencies in the CCAF's force structure. Years of secret military buildup had prioritized main battle units and sheer firepower, leaving the Confederation without the specialized combined-arms assets needed to sustain large-scale offensive operations. Capellan commanders struggled to locate concealed defenders and coordinate indirect fire with the precision their expanding campaigns demanded. The recent fighting demonstrated that possessing materiel was not enough, the Confederation needed the right kind of materiel.\\n\\nNovis-AT-H, Inc. on Detroit developed the Luduan to address this gap. The company, already an established Magistracy military manufacturer producing the Tamerlane strike sled, leveraged its existing hover vehicle expertise to deliver the new design quickly. Production began in 3136, and the Luduan's adoption across the CCAF was remarkably swift. Within a few years it had spread to nearly every unit in the Confederation, including formations without organic artillery support.\\n\\nThe design proved its worth during the Capellan advance into former Republic and Federated Suns territory. On Taygeta, a Davion world that had resisted Capellan occupation for centuries, elements of Lockhardt's Ironsides constructed a network of firebases to contain resistance activity while allowing Davion resistance fighters and remnant AFFS forces to operate freely elsewhere. A sharply fought ambush of a Capellan convoy demonstrated both the danger of the Davion strategy and the Luduan's tactical value. Forced to use the roads, the convoy was vulnerable, but Luduan crews detected the ambush and forced the Davion attackers to spring their trap prematurely. Piercing through Davion jamming, the Luduans swept across the battle zone at high speed, marking enemy positions and calling in devastating artillery fire from nearby Capellan firebases. The Davion force was shattered. The Luduan's speed and stealth armor meant that few of the scout vehicles were lost during the engagement."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"~110"}],"name":"Luduan Scout Vehicle","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"hover","era":"dark-ages","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":11,"armorType":"vehicular-stealth","armorAllocation":{"front":12,"left":10,"right":10,"rear":8,"turret":8,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["angel-ecm","#0",null,null],["bloodhound-active-probe","#1",null,null],["gauss-rifle-magshot","#2","turret",null],["gauss-rifle-magshot","#3","turret",null],["gauss-rifle-magshot","#4","turret",null],["is-tag","#5","turret",null],["~111","#6",null,"gauss-rifle-magshot"]]}},"478":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Killosh Industries","primaryFactory":"Bharat","communications":"Hesperus 5GT","targeting":"Lynx RM"},"components":{"engine":"Magna 350","armor":"~112"},"text":{"overview":"~113","capabilities":"~114","deployment":"The base Magi served primarily with SLDF Mechanized Infantry Divisions assigned to the Periphery theater during the Star League era. After centuries of obscurity, it resurfaced in the thirty-first century with ComStar's Com Guards, where it served in combined-arms formations. Following the ComStar Schism, surviving vehicles were distributed between ComStar and the Word of Blake, with the latter deploying them extensively in Protectorate Militia regiments for urban security operations.","battleHistory":"~115"}},"name":"~116","tonnage":70,"hasTurret":false,"era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~117","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["medium-laser","#1","front",null],"~118",["machine-gun","#3","front",null],["medium-laser","#4","right",null],["medium-laser","#5","left",null],["~14","#6",null,"machine-gun"]]}},"479":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Hesperus 5GT","targeting":"Lynx RM"},"components":{"engine":"Magna 350 Light","armor":"~112"},"text":{"overview":"~113","capabilities":"~114","deployment":"The Urban Combat Support Vehicle modification was developed by Word of Blake technicians to transform the Magi into a dedicated urban combat support platform. The standard fusion engine was replaced with a light model, freeing mass for a C3i computer and a six-ton infantry bay. The machine gun complement was doubled to four weapons bundled into a single array, with ammunition stores increased accordingly. These modifications made the UCSV variant a common fixture in Blake Protectorate Militia formations during the Jihad era.","battleHistory":"~115"}},"name":"~116","model":"(UCSV)","tonnage":70,"hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":6,"era":"jihad","engineType":"cell","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~117","equipment":{"$k":"~10","$v":["~119",["c3i-computer","#1",null,null,null],["medium-laser","#2","front",null,null],"~12",["machine-gun","#4","front",null,null],["machine-gun","#5","front",null,null],["machine-gun","#6","front",null,null],["is-machine-gun-array","#7","front",null,null],["medium-laser","#8","right",null,null],["medium-laser","#9","left",null,null],["~14","#10",null,true,"machine-gun"],["~14","#11",null,true,"machine-gun"],["~14","#12",null,true,"machine-gun"]]}},"480":{"tro":{"production":"~120","components":"~121","text":{"overview":"~122","capabilities":"~123","deployment":"The base Main Gauche configuration serves as a dedicated long-range fire support platform, deployed in conjunction with Galleons or similar light tanks that provide the close-range protection the design lacks. Standard tactical doctrine positions the Main Gauche overlooking the battlefield from elevated or covered terrain, where it can exploit the light Gauss rifle's exceptional range while companion vehicles and infantry screen its flanks and rear.","battleHistory":"~124"}},"name":"~125","tonnage":30,"hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~126","equipment":{"$k":"~56","$v":[["gauss-rifle-light","front","#0",null,null],["machine-gun","front","#1",null,null],"~57",["~127",null,"#3","gauss-rifle-light",null],"~58"]}},"481":{"tro":{"production":"~120","components":"~121","text":{"overview":"~122","capabilities":"~123","deployment":"The C3 variant removes the twin machine guns in favor of a C3 slave computer and an extended-range small laser. This configuration allows the Main Gauche to leverage targeting data from friendly units operating closer to the enemy, significantly improving the light Gauss rifle's accuracy at extreme range. The trade-off in close defense capability is considered acceptable, as the variant's entire tactical purpose centers on remaining at maximum standoff distance while networked units provide forward spotting.","battleHistory":"~124"}},"name":"~125","model":"(C3)","tonnage":30,"hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~126","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~100",["gauss-rifle-light","#1","front",null],["er-small-laser","#2","front",null],["~127","#3",null,"gauss-rifle-light"]]}},"482":{"tro":{"production":"~120","components":{"engine":"GM 180 XL Fusion","armor":"Jolassa 320"},"text":{"overview":"~122","capabilities":"~123","deployment":"Based on the XL variant's chassis, the IFV configuration replaces the light Gauss rifle with a plasma rifle, four light machine guns covering multiple arcs, and a four-ton infantry compartment. This transforms the Main Gauche from a standoff fire support vehicle into an infantry fighting vehicle capable of delivering a squad directly to the battlefield while providing suppressive fire. Additional armor compensates for the closer engagement ranges this role demands.","battleHistory":"~124"}},"name":"~125","model":"(IFV)","tonnage":30,"hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":4,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":21,"right":21,"rear":16,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~128","$v":[["machine-gun-light","front","#0",null,null],["machine-gun-light","front","#1",null,null],["plasma-rifle","front","#2",null,null],["machine-gun-light","right","#3",null,null],["machine-gun-light","left","#4",null,null],["~129",null,"#5",true,"machine-gun-light"],["~130",null,"#6",null,"plasma-rifle"],["~130",null,"#7",null,"plasma-rifle"]]}},"483":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Pesht Motors","primaryFactory":"Unity","communications":"Neil 500","targeting":"Cirxese BallistaCheck and CirxeseRockeCheck with Apollo FCS"},"components":{"engine":"VOX 280 XL","armor":"StarGuard Aurora Reflective"},"text":{"overview":"The Mamono IFV was introduced in 3095 by Pesht Motors as a purpose-built infantry fighting vehicle for the Draconis Combine Mustered Soldiery. The design emerged from a shift in Combine military doctrine that placed increased emphasis on integrating battle armor into combined-arms regiments. While the DCMS already fielded the Hiryo WiGE as a troop transport, that platform's fragility in contested combat zones created a pressing need for a more survivable alternative. Pesht Motors answered with the Mamono, a seventy-ton tracked vehicle manufactured on Unity. Capable of carrying either a full squad of battle armor or a complete infantry platoon, the Mamono bears a visual resemblance to Davion designs like the Goblin and Hasek, though it carries substantially heavier armor and a broader weapons suite. The vehicle has become a common sight in Combine combined-arms formations along the Draconis March border.","capabilities":"The Mamono is built around a VOX 280 XL fusion engine, giving the seventy-ton vehicle a top speed of 64 km/h. While adequate for supporting infantry advances, this speed is unremarkable for its weight class. Ten tons of StarGuard Aurora Reflective armor provide strong protection against energy weapons, a practical choice given the prevalence of laser-equipped BattleMechs along the Combine's borders. CASE protects the crew and passengers from catastrophic ammunition detonations.\\n\\nThe vehicle's turret mounts a Shigunga MRM 40 launcher paired with an Apollo fire control system, significantly improving the normally inaccurate MRM system's hit probability. A Lord's Light 5 snub-nose PPC provides reliable direct-fire capability at shorter ranges, while a Chosatsugai no Kunai anti-missile system offers some defense against incoming missile salvos. Two fixed Sperry Browning light machine guns cover the flanks, providing suppressive fire against unarmored infantry during troop deployment operations.\\n\\nThe eight-ton infantry compartment is the Mamono's core feature, large enough for a full battle armor squad or a conventional infantry platoon. This flexibility makes the vehicle useful across a wide range of combined-arms configurations, though the vehicle's limited top speed can restrict tactical options when operating alongside faster formations.","deployment":"The Mamono has been fielded exclusively by the Draconis Combine Mustered Soldiery since its introduction. The vehicle is most commonly found in infantry and combined-arms regiments operating along the volatile Federated Suns border, where its heavy armor and firepower are valued in the close-quarters fighting that characterizes planetary garrison actions. No formal variants had entered service as of the mid-thirty-second century, a reflection of both the design's relative youth and its adequate performance in the roles for which it was intended.","battleHistory":"The Mamono entered production in 3095 at Pesht Motors' Unity facility, arriving as the Draconis Combine rebuilt its military forces following the devastation of the Jihad. The DCMS had embraced a new combined-arms philosophy that placed battle armor at the heart of its infantry formations, but the existing Hiryo WiGE transports proved too fragile for frontline combat zones where these formations would operate. Pesht Motors, already an established Combine defense contractor with the Hiryo in its product line, leveraged that experience to develop the Mamono as a more survivable alternative.\\n\\nReception among DCMS units was mixed. Conventional infantry regiments embraced the Mamono enthusiastically, praising its heavy armor and substantial firepower compared to lighter transport platforms. Battle armor troopers were considerably less impressed, finding the vehicle's sluggish performance a poor match for the aggressive mobility their tactics demanded.\\n\\nThe Mamono saw its first significant combat during operations along the Federated Suns border. On Pascagoula, three Mamonos of the Fifth Galedon Regulars supported a protracted assault on an outlying spaceport. DCMS High Command had identified a large concentration of AFFS aerospace fighters on the planet, and the kanrei temporarily attached the Lyceum militia to the Fifth for the operation. A combined force of Hiryos and Mamonos moved infantry into defensive positions around the depot, and three Mamonos charged the Davion perimeter. Showering the defenders with missiles and PPC fire, their infantry seized a heavy bunker and exploited the breach. By the end of the engagement, the Combine had captured significant quantities of Davion materiel, including a prototype vehicle and several tons of munitions. After destroying a wing of grounded AFFS aerospace fighters, the Regulars withdrew, leaving the AFFS scrambling to find a new staging area.\\n\\nOn Cimeron during the Federated Suns invasion, the Mamonos of the Twenty-second New Samarkand Regulars participated in the capture of the planetary capital. Loaded with squads of Oni and Kishi battle armor, four Mamonos of the Cold Dish tank brigade seized the main bridge span over the Calumet River. The armored troopers deployed around the bridge's abutments while the IFV platoon formed a roadblock near the southern approach. Approaching AFFS forces attempting to reinforce Mondo Elegro were met with concentrated fire from the entrenched Mamonos. Those who managed to close were engaged by the waiting Combine battle armor. Unable to reinforce the capital, the AFFS capitulated, and Cimeron fell shortly after."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"quirk-poor-performance"}],"name":"Mamono IFV","tonnage":70,"troopSpace":8,"chassisModifications":["limited-amphibious"],"era":"early-rep","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"laser-reflective","armorAllocation":{"front":40,"left":30,"right":30,"rear":30,"turret":30,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~131","$v":["~119",["machine-gun-light","#1","right",null,null],["machine-gun-light","#2","left",null,null],["snub-nose-ppc","#3","turret",null,null],["is-ams","#4","turret",null,null],["mrm-40-apollo","#5","turret",null,null],["ammo-is-ams-standard","#6",null,"is-ams",null],["~129","#7",null,"machine-gun-light",true],["ammo-is-mrm-standard","#8",null,"mrm-40-apollo",null],["ammo-is-mrm-standard","#9",null,"mrm-40-apollo",null]]}},"484":{"tro":{"production":"~132","components":"~133","text":{"overview":"~134","capabilities":"~135","deployment":"The A configuration is the most commonly encountered Manteuffel variant, built around devastating short-range firepower. Its Ultra Autocannon/20 can shatter opposing armor at close range, supported by two medium lasers and an ER small laser for supplementary fire. A C3 slave module maintains the networked targeting capability shared across the Manteuffel family. The A sees heavy use in urban and close-terrain operations where its massive autocannon can be brought to bear at optimal range.","battleHistory":"~136"}},"name":"Manteuffel Attack Tank","model":"A","tonnage":70,"omni":true,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~137","equipment":{"$k":"~138","$v":[["c3-computer-slave","#0",true,null,null],"~139",["medium-laser","#2",true,"front",null],"~140",["er-small-laser","#4",true,"front",null],["autocannon-ultra-d","#5",true,"turret",null],["~141","#6",null,null,"autocannon-ultra-d"],["~141","#7",null,null,"autocannon-ultra-d"],["~141","#8",null,null,"autocannon-ultra-d"],["~141","#9",null,null,"autocannon-ultra-d"],["~141","#10",null,null,"autocannon-ultra-d"]]}},"485":{"tro":{"production":"~132","components":"~133","text":{"overview":"~134","capabilities":"~142","deployment":"Developed in 3068 during the opening years of the Jihad, the C configuration mounts a standard Gauss rifle paired with two machine guns for anti-infantry work. Its defining feature is a C3 master computer, making it the command node for Manteuffel-equipped C3 lances. Commanders operating the C variant coordinate targeting data for an entire lance of networked vehicles, significantly increasing the collective accuracy of the formation.","battleHistory":"~136"}},"name":"Manteuffel Attack Tank","model":"C","tonnage":70,"omni":true,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~137","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","pod","location","feedsWeaponTag","halfTon"],"$v":[["c3-computer-master","#0",true,null,null,null],["case","#1",null,null,null,null],["machine-gun","#2",true,"front",null,null],["machine-gun","#3",true,"front",null,null],["standard-gauss-rifle","#4",true,"turret",null,null],["~143","#5",null,null,"standard-gauss-rifle",null],["~143","#6",null,null,"standard-gauss-rifle",null],["~14","#7",null,null,"machine-gun",true]]}},"486":{"tro":{"production":"~132","components":"~133","text":{"overview":"~134","capabilities":"~135","deployment":"Introduced in 3075, the D configuration converts the Manteuffel into a heavy infantry fighting vehicle, dedicating eight tons of internal space to an infantry compartment. Twin Light PPCs and an LRM-10 provide fire support for the embarked troops, while an anti-missile system defends against incoming ordnance. The D fills a niche role in combined-arms operations, delivering infantry to contested positions under the protection of a heavily armored hull.","battleHistory":"~136"}},"name":"Manteuffel Attack Tank","model":"D","tonnage":70,"omni":true,"troopSpace":8,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~137","equipment":{"$k":"~144","$v":["~119",["is-ams","#1","turret",true,null],["lrm-10","#2","turret",true,null],["light-ppc","#3","turret",true,null],["light-ppc","#4","turret",true,null],["ammo-is-ams-standard","#5",null,null,"is-ams"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#6",null,null,"lrm-10"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#7",null,null,"lrm-10"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#8",null,null,"lrm-10"]]}},"487":{"tro":{"production":"~132","components":"~133","text":{"overview":"~134","capabilities":"The Manteuffel's Magna 350 XL fusion engine drives the seventy-ton vehicle to a top speed of 86 km/h, fast enough to keep pace with most medium BattleMech formations. StarSlab/3 ferro-fibrous armor provides eleven and a half tons of protection, enhanced by CASE to contain ammunition explosions. The vehicle dedicates 23.5 tons of pod space to mission-configurable weaponry, with fifteen tons available in the turret and the remainder distributed across hull mounting points.\\n\\nThe Prime configuration pairs a Rotary AC/5 with three extended-range medium lasers, delivering devastating burst damage at medium range. A Guardian ECM suite disrupts enemy electronic warfare systems, while a C3 slave module allows the Manteuffel to share targeting data across a networked lance. This combination makes the Prime a potent force multiplier in coordinated engagements. The XL engine is a significant vulnerability, as penetrating hits to either side can disable the vehicle entirely. The reliance on ammunition-fed weapons in most configurations also limits sustained endurance without resupply, and crews must manage their stores carefully during extended operations.","deployment":"Introduced in 3087 during the early Republic era, the E configuration carries a BattleMech Taser and LB 10-X autocannon in the turret. The Guardian ECM suite is retained from the Prime, but the C3 slave is removed to accommodate the Taser system. A pair of medium lasers and an ER small laser cover the forward arc. The Taser gives the E a unique capability to temporarily disable enemy vehicles and BattleMechs, making it valuable in situations where targets need to be captured intact.","battleHistory":"~136"}},"name":"Manteuffel Attack Tank","model":"E","tonnage":70,"omni":true,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~137","equipment":{"$k":"~138","$v":["~145","~139",["er-small-laser","#2",true,"front",null],"~140",["medium-laser","#4",true,"front",null],["autocannon-lbx-10","#5",true,"turret",null],["mech-taser","#6",true,"turret",null],["~146","#7",null,null,"autocannon-lbx-10"],"~147",["~148","#9",null,null,"mech-taser"],["~148","#10",null,null,"mech-taser"]]}},"488":{"tro":{"production":"~132","components":"~133","text":{"overview":"~134","capabilities":"~142","deployment":"The Manteuffel Prime is assigned only to the highest-profile RCTs within the LAAF and AFFS, restricted by both its expense and the advanced technologies it employs. The Lyran Guards and Davion Brigade of Guards are primary recipients, along with the Deneb Light Cavalry RCTs, regarded as among the most effective combined-arms brigades in the Inner Sphere. Every Prime configuration can deliver enormous firepower while maintaining the speed to keep up with all but the fastest scout elements.","battleHistory":"~136"}},"name":"Manteuffel Attack Tank","model":"Prime","tonnage":70,"omni":true,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~137","equipment":{"$k":"~138","$v":["~145",["c3-computer-slave","#1",true,null,null],["case","#2",null,null,null],["rotary-ac-5","#3",true,"turret",null],["er-medium-laser","#4",true,"turret",null],["er-medium-laser","#5",true,"turret",null],["er-medium-laser","#6",true,"turret",null],["~30","#7",null,null,"rotary-ac-5"],["~30","#8",null,null,"rotary-ac-5"],["~30","#9",null,null,"rotary-ac-5"]]}},"489":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Defiance Industries|Jalastar Aerospace|Bulldog Enterprises|Majesty Metals and Manufacturing|New Earth Trading Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|TechniCorp","primaryFactory":"Hesperus II|Panpour|Proserpina|Dunianshire|New Earth|Ares|Broken Wheel|Terra","communications":"O/P R Janxiir","targeting":"TharHes TargiTrack 717"},"components":{"engine":"Pitban 240 Fusion","armor":"ArcShield Maxi II"},"text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"The base Manticore remains in service with military forces across the Inner Sphere and beyond, fielded by every Great House and numerous Periphery states. Its long production history and widespread manufacturing base make replacement parts readily available, ensuring the design's continued viability even in less industrialized regions. Many wealthy nobles who cannot afford a full BattleMech formation will field companies of Manticores instead.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","tonnage":60,"era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":42,"left":33,"right":33,"rear":26,"turret":42,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["medium-laser","front","#0",null],["lrm-10","turret","#1",null],"~66","~152",["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#4","lrm-10"],"~68"]}},"490":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Defiance Industries","primaryFactory":"Hesperus II","communications":"TharHes Muse 54-58K","targeting":"~153"},"components":"~154","text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Defiance Industries' upgraded Manticore is found throughout the Lyran Alliance and Federated Suns, where the first units were issued to the Lyran Royal Guards for ceremonial and combat duties. The upgrade was designed for easy field conversion of existing vehicles, and many older Manticores were retrofitted with the new weapons suite during the late Clan Invasion era.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","model":"(3055 Upgrade)","tonnage":60,"era":"clan-inv","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~155","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~156",["large-pulse-laser","turret","#1",null],["lrm-10-artemis-iv","turret","#2",null],["srm-6-artemis-iv","turret","#3",null],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv",null,"#4","lrm-10-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-srm-artemis-iv",null,"#5","srm-6-artemis-iv"],["~89",null,"#6","streak-srm-2"]]}},"491":{"tro":{"production":"~157","components":"~154","text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Bulldog Industries' C3 master variant is deployed primarily by Draconis Combine forces, where integrated C3 networking is a standard tactical doctrine. The replacement of the SRM launcher with the master computer and additional armor shifts the vehicle's role from multi-range combatant to a dedicated fire-coordination platform within C3 lances. The variant anchors Combine combined-arms formations.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","model":"(C3M)","tonnage":60,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~158","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~159",["streak-srm-2","#1","front",null],"~160",["lrm-10-artemis-iv","#3","turret",null],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv","#4",null,"lrm-10-artemis-iv"],["~89","#5",null,"streak-srm-2"]]}},"492":{"tro":{"production":"~157","components":"~154","text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"The C3 slave variant supplements the master version in Draconis Combine service, carrying a Streak SRM-4 alongside the slave computer to retain some short-range capability. Paired with C3 master vehicles, the slave variant leverages shared targeting data to improve the accuracy of its standard PPC at extended ranges. The two C3 variants are typically deployed together in mixed lances.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","model":"(C3S)","tonnage":60,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~155","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~156",["lrm-10-artemis-iv","turret","#1",null],["c3-computer-slave","turret","#2",null],"~152",["streak-srm-4","turret","#4",null],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv",null,"#5","lrm-10-artemis-iv"],["~89",null,"#6","streak-srm-4"],"~161"]}},"493":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Bulldog Enterprises|Jalastar Aerospace|Quikscell Company","primaryFactory":"Proserpina|Panpour|Ares","communications":"TharHes Muse 54-58K","targeting":"~153"},"components":{"engine":"Pitban 240 Light Fusion","armor":"~162"},"text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Produced during the Jihad by multiple manufacturers after Bulldog Enterprises sold the schematics to Quikscell and Jalastar, the HPPC variant is found in Draconis Combine, Capellan Confederation, and Federated Suns service. The light fusion engine and heavy PPC combination reflects the trend toward energy-dominant weapon configurations during the Jihad era.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","model":"(HPPC)","tonnage":60,"era":"jihad","engineType":"cell","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~155","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~163",["heavy-ppc","#1","turret",null],["mml-7","#2","turret",null],"~164",["ammo-lrm-standard","#4",null,"mml-7"],["ammo-srm-standard","#5",null,"mml-7"],"~165"]}},"494":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"New Earth Trading Company","primaryFactory":"New Earth","communications":"TharHes Muse 54-58K","targeting":"~153"},"components":"~154","text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Manufactured by the New Earth Trading Company using unproduced Defiance Industries plans, the LB-X variant proved effective as an anti-battle armor support vehicle during the FedCom Civil War. Its turret-mounted LB 10-X autocannon and Streak SRM-6 provided reliable cluster ammunition delivery against infantry formations, and the medium pulse laser offered supplementary close-range firepower.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","model":"(LB-X)","tonnage":60,"era":"clan-inv","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~155","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["medium-pulse-laser","front","#0",null],"~166",["streak-srm-6","turret","#2",null],["~146",null,"#3","autocannon-lbx-10"],"~167",["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster",null,"#5","autocannon-lbx-10"],["~89",null,"#6","streak-srm-6"]]}},"495":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Jalastar Aerospace","primaryFactory":"Panpour","communications":"TharHes Muse 54-58K","targeting":"~153"},"components":"~154","text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Jalastar Aerospace's experimental rotary autocannon variant trades the original missile-heavy loadout for concentrated direct-fire capability. The RAC/5 paired with a targeting computer and two ER medium lasers provides devastating sustained firepower, though ammunition dependency limits its operational endurance in prolonged engagements. The design has found favor among Federated Suns heavy armor commands.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","model":"(RAC)","tonnage":60,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~155","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~163",["er-medium-laser","#1","front",null],["er-medium-laser","#2","front",null],"~27",["srm-6-artemis-iv","#4","turret",null],"~168","~28",["ammo-is-srm-artemis-iv","#7",null,"srm-6-artemis-iv"]]}},"496":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Defiance Industries|Bulldog Enterprises|Jalastar Aerospace|Quikscell Company","primaryFactory":"Hesperus II|Proserpina|Panpour|Ares","communications":"TharHes Muse 54-58K","targeting":"TharHes Targitrack with Targeting Computer"},"components":{"engine":"240 XL Fusion","armor":"Heavy Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Licensed by Defiance Industries to manufacturers across the Inner Sphere, the Manticore XL entered production in 3117 at facilities on Hesperus II, Proserpina, Panpour, and Ares. The design draws inspiration from the Manticore II, incorporating an extra-light engine and targeting computer while maintaining the original sixty-ton chassis to allow production on existing Manticore factory lines.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","model":"(XL)","tonnage":60,"era":"late-rep","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":42,"left":34,"right":34,"rear":26,"turret":42,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~163",["case","#1",null,null],["medium-laser","#2","front",null],["er-ppc","#3","turret",null],["enhanced-lrm-10","#4","turret",null],["streak-srm-6","#5","turret",null],["~169","#6",null,"enhanced-lrm-10"],"~170"]}},"497":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"Pitban 240 Fusion"},"text":{"overview":"~149","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"The Manticore EC was developed by Clan Burrock during the Golden Century, replacing the original medium laser with improved Clan-grade PPC and LRM systems. The designation 'EC' was applied by Inner Sphere historians to identify early Clan-modified designs that predated standardized Clan vehicle production. The variant saw service during the Clans' early territorial conflicts.","battleHistory":"~151"}},"name":"Manticore Heavy Tank","model":"EC","tonnage":60,"tech":"mis","era":"clan-inv","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":42,"left":36,"right":36,"rear":28,"turret":42,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~64",["clan-improved-lrm-10","turret","#1",null],["enhanced_er_ppc","turret","#2",null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#3","srm-6"],["~171",null,"#4","clan-improved-lrm-10"],["~171",null,"#5","clan-improved-lrm-10"]]}},"498":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Defiance Industries","primaryFactory":"Hesperus II","communications":"TharHes Muse 65-128K","targeting":"TharHes TargiTrack with Advanced Targeting Computer"},"components":{"engine":"GM 280 XXL Fusion","armor":"Durallex Special Heavy Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"The Manticore II is a seventy-ton amphibious heavy tank developed by Defiance Industries on Hesperus II, introduced in 3112 as a heavier and more specialized successor to the original Manticore. Where the original served as a general-purpose infantry support vehicle, the Manticore II was purpose-built for littoral and amphibious operations, filling a tactical niche that few heavy tanks could address. Its combination of heavy firepower, advanced targeting systems, and amphibious capability made it a valuable asset on water-rich worlds, though its specialized nature and high cost limited its adoption compared to its more versatile ancestor. Defiance Industries marketed the vehicle as a complement rather than a replacement for the original Manticore, recognizing that different operational theaters demanded different tools.","capabilities":"The Manticore II's armament centers on a Defiance 980 heavy particle projection cannon paired with two Delta Dart II enhanced LRM-10 launchers, all mounted in the turret and integrated with an advanced targeting computer. This weapons suite provides devastating long-range firepower, allowing the vehicle to engage targets from positions where most opponents cannot effectively return fire. The absence of short-range weapons reflects the vehicle's intended role as a standoff fire support platform rather than a close-quarters brawler.\\n\\nA GM 280 extra-extra-light fusion engine propels the seventy-ton tank to speeds of 64 km/h, providing excellent mobility for its weight class, particularly in wet terrain where the amphibious design truly excels. Nine and a half tons of Durallex Special heavy ferro-fibrous armor offer substantial protection, comparable to many BattleMechs in the same tonnage range. The Manticore II's primary weakness is its extreme expense, driven by the extra-extra-light engine and advanced electronics suite. Many militia and mercenary commanders balk at the cost, and those who attempt to use it as a direct replacement for the original Manticore often find its specialized capabilities wasted in general-purpose roles where the cheaper original performs adequately.","deployment":"The Manticore II is deployed primarily by Lyran Commonwealth Armed Forces units on water-rich worlds where its amphibious capabilities offer the greatest tactical advantage. The LCAF assigns Manticore IIs directly to regiments based on worlds where they will be useful, maintaining a number of independent formations expected to attach to commands as needed. Mercenary commands and Republic militia forces also field the vehicle, though its high cost limits widespread adoption outside well-funded organizations.","battleHistory":"Defiance Industries developed the Manticore II in 3112 at their Hesperus II facility, drawing on centuries of experience producing the original Manticore to create a successor optimized for amphibious warfare. The vehicle was exported to Republic of the Sphere forces and made available for mercenary purchase before the Blackout disrupted interstellar commerce.\\n\\nThe LCAF assigned Manticore IIs to regiments stationed on worlds where water-rich terrain offered tactical opportunities, though some wealthy Lyran and Republic militia commands acquired the vehicle without fully understanding its specialized role. Commanders who deployed it as a conventional heavy tank often found its high cost difficult to justify, while those who embraced its amphibious capabilities discovered a powerful force multiplier.\\n\\nThe Fifth Lyran Guards demonstrated the Manticore II's potential during the Third Battle of Tamarind in 3139. An attached company of Manticore IIs dominated the Zanzibe River Delta north of the planetary capital, denying the Tamarind Regulars the ability to traverse the region. The amphibious tanks pushed down the Zanzibe River itself at the height of the fighting, emerging from the water as a surprise into the rear areas of the Fourth Tamarind Regulars. The attack hit the Regulars' command and supply elements, precipitating the collapse of their resistance.\\n\\nIn January 3136, the Republic Standing Guard on Poznan faced a Capellan invasion aimed at the capital of New Madrid. A platoon of Manticore IIs was rushed to the main continent of Barcelona from the river-crossed continent of Mieszko to reinforce the existing militia presence. The rolling hills around New Madrid offered little chance to employ the vehicles' amphibious capabilities, but their heavy PPCs and LRMs provided valuable support fire against the CCAF advance. When the fighting closed in on the capital, the Manticore IIs were withdrawn and preserved to fight another day.\\n\\nOn nearby Ningpo during an earlier Capellan invasion in 3135, planetary defenders elected to use their Manticore IIs as standard heavy tanks to defend the fertile farmlands of the Qingyuan province. They performed adequately driving away Capellan light armor probing the lines of the Republic militia, but were soon pitched into direct combat against a company of CCAF Pixius tanks. The difference in skill and capability was overwhelming, and all of the Manticore IIs were destroyed at the cost of three Capellan tanks. The Manticore IIs were thus unavailable to contest the wetlands north of the planetary capital, speeding up the fall of the world.\\n\\nThe Manticore II's mixed combat record highlighted a consistent pattern. When employed in its intended amphibious role, the vehicle could dominate engagements and shape entire campaigns. When treated as a conventional heavy tank, its high cost made it a questionable investment compared to the proven original Manticore or other established designs in the same weight class."}},"name":"Manticore II Heavy Tank","tonnage":70,"chassisModifications":["fully-amphibious"],"era":"late-rep","engineType":"xxl","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~158","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~163",["enhanced-lrm-10","#1","turret",null],["enhanced-lrm-10","#2","turret",null],["heavy-ppc","#3","turret",null],["~169","#4",null,"enhanced-lrm-10"],["~169","#5",null,"enhanced-lrm-10"]]}},"499":{"tro":{"production":"~172","components":{"engine":"Galas 70 Micro","armor":"~173"},"text":{"overview":"~174","capabilities":"~175","deployment":"The base Mantis entered service in 3061 with Federated Suns aerospace and armored cavalry formations, where its combination of speed and laser firepower made it ideal for screening, harassment, and rapid pursuit operations. The design saw widespread use across the FedCom Civil War and Jihad periods, with surviving examples continuing in service under Republic of the Sphere and successor state forces. Michaelson's export agreements with Diverse Optics ensured early Free Worlds League access, and the design's simplicity made it popular with garrison and militia forces that valued its ammunition-free weapons loadout and straightforward maintenance requirements.","battleHistory":"The Mantis traces its origins to Michaelson Heavy Industries' successful contract with Imperator Automatic Weaponry of Atreus. The ease with which Michaelson obtained Free Worlds League permission to import light Gauss rifles for the Hawk Moth gunship encouraged the company to pursue additional cross-border technology deals. This led to a partnership with Diverse Optics, who agreed to supply their extended-range small lasers with the stipulation that the first production run would ship to the Free Worlds League at a considerably reduced price.\\n\\nThe Mantis was still undergoing field testing at Michaelson's live-fire range on Ruchbah in 3060, entering full production the following year. It joined an impressive stable of Michaelson VTOL designs including the Sprint Scout helicopter, Yellow Jacket gunship, Cavalry Attack helicopter, and Hawk Moth gunship. The steady stream of successful designs from Michaelson's production lines generated immediate interest in the Mantis even during its prototype phase.\\n\\nDuring the Jihad, Ruchbah fell under Word of Blake blockade, severely impacting Michaelson's operations. The company faced near-bankruptcy during the occupation, eventually selling technology to Brooks Incorporated to remain solvent. Despite these hardships, Mantis production continued in reduced numbers, and the design proved valuable in the guerrilla warfare waged against the Blakist presence. Local cavalry units supplemented by Michaelson's swift VTOLs maintained a persistent low-level resistance throughout the conflict.\\n\\nFollowing the Jihad, Ruchbah came under Republic of the Sphere administration, and Michaelson's factories were rebuilt. The planet later became contested territory during the Dark Age, with both the Spirit Cats and Steel Wolves fighting for control of Michaelson's manufacturing facilities in 3133. Despite repeated ownership changes, Mantis production persisted through each transition, evidence of the design's enduring utility and Michaelson's ability to continue operations regardless of political upheaval."}},"name":"~176","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"era":"clan-inv","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":11,"armorAllocation":{"front":14,"left":8,"right":8,"rear":8,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":[["er-small-laser","front","#0"],["er-small-laser","front","#1"],["er-small-laser","front","#2"],["er-small-laser","front","#3"],["er-small-laser","front","#4"],["ecm-suite","front","#5"],["beagle-active-probe","front","#6"]]}},"500":{"tro":{"production":"~172","components":{"armor":"~173"},"text":{"overview":"~174","capabilities":"~175","deployment":"Introduced in 3073 during the Jihad, the ECCM variant strips all weaponry and the standard sensor suite in favor of a dedicated electronic warfare configuration. Mounting a second Guardian ECM Suite alongside a C3 slave computer and mast mount, this Mantis serves as an advanced scout and battlefield coordination platform. An extralight fusion engine replaces the standard micro-fusion powerplant, increasing both speed and available tonnage for additional armor protection. The variant found immediate use in counter-Blakist operations, where its electronic warfare capabilities helped disrupt Word of Blake command and control networks.","battleHistory":"The Mantis traces its origins to Michaelson Heavy Industries' successful contract with Imperator Automatic Weaponry of Atreus. The ease with which Michaelson obtained Free Worlds League permission to import light Gauss rifles for the Hawk Moth gunship encouraged the company to pursue additional cross-border technology deals. This led to a partnership with Diverse Optics, who agreed to supply their extended-range small lasers with the stipulation that the first production run would ship to the Free Worlds League at a considerably reduced price.\\n\\nThe Mantis was still undergoing field testing at Michaelson's live-fire range on Ruchbah in 3060, entering full production the following year. It joined an impressive stable of Michaelson VTOL designs including the Sprint Scout helicopter, Yellow Jacket gunship, Cavalry Attack helicopter, and Hawk Moth gunship. The steady stream of successful designs from Michaelson's production lines generated immediate interest in the Mantis even during its prototype phase.\\n\\nDuring the Jihad, Ruchbah fell under Word of Blake blockade, severely impacting Michaelson's operations. The company faced near-bankruptcy during the occupation, eventually selling technology to Brooks Incorporated to remain solvent. Despite these hardships, Mantis production continued in reduced numbers, and the design proved valuable in the guerrilla warfare waged against the Blakist presence. Local cavalry units supplemented by Michaelson's swift VTOLs maintained a persistent low-level resistance throughout the conflict.\\n\\nFollowing the Jihad, Ruchbah came under Republic of the Sphere administration, and Michaelson's factories were rebuilt. The planet later became contested territory during the Dark Age, with both the Spirit Cats and Steel Wolves fighting for control of Michaelson's manufacturing facilities in 3133. Despite repeated ownership changes, Mantis production persisted through each transition, a reflection of the design's enduring utility and Michaelson's ability to continue operations regardless of political upheaval."}},"name":"~176","model":"(ECCM)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":16,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":14,"left":10,"right":10,"rear":8,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":["~177",["ecm-suite","#1",null],["ecm-suite","#2",null],["vtol-mast-mount","#3","rotor"]]}},"501":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Grumman Industries|Grumman Industries|GrummanCRI","primaryFactory":"New Earth|Terra|Melissia","communications":"GRD 300","targeting":"GRD059"},"components":{"engine":"Magna 260","armor":"Grumman CRT"},"text":{"overview":"~178","capabilities":"~179","deployment":"The base Marksman served Lyran Commonwealth artillery formations throughout the Succession Wars, typically assigned to battalion-level fire support batteries operating from concealed positions behind the forward line. The vehicle's limited speed made it unsuitable for mobile artillery roles, and commanders standardized on using it from prepared firing positions where it could conduct sustained indirect fire with minimal exposure to direct counter-fire. Surviving examples migrated to second-line and planetary militia service as attrition removed them from front-line inventories, and the design remained a fixture of Lyran militia artillery through the Civil War period.","battleHistory":"~180"}},"name":"~181","tonnage":65,"era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":49,"left":19,"right":19,"rear":19,"turret":19,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~182",["large-laser","turret","#1",null],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#2","sniper-artillery"],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#3","sniper-artillery"]]}},"502":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"GRD 300","targeting":"GRD059"},"components":{"engine":"Magna 260"},"text":{"overview":"~178","capabilities":"~179","deployment":"The (AC) variant arose as a field expedient on the Pentagon Worlds before Operation Klondike, where isolated communities modified available Marksman hulls to produce a more straightforward direct-combat vehicle. The conversion stripped the artillery tube and protective plating to free tonnage for a hull-mounted heavy autocannon with substantial ammunition reserves and a battery of rocket launchers. The resulting vehicle retained the original's fusion power plant and proved capable in the close-range engagements that characterized the Pentagon Worlds conflicts. Few examples survived Operation Klondike itself, and the variant effectively ceased to exist as a distinct configuration after the Clans consolidated control of the region.","battleHistory":"~180"}},"name":"~181","model":"(AC)","tonnage":65,"era":"early-sw","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":54,"left":20,"right":20,"rear":20,"turret":22,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["autocannon-standard-d","front","#0",null],["~183","front","#1",null],["~183","front","#2",null],["~183","front","#3",null],["~184","front","#4",null],["~184","front","#5",null],["large-laser","turret","#6",null],"~185","~186",["ammo-ac-20-standard",null,"#9","autocannon-standard-d"]]}},"503":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"GrummanCRI","primaryFactory":"Melissia","communications":"GRD 300","targeting":"GRD059"},"components":{"engine":"Magna 260 Light","armor":"Grumman CRT (Light FF)"},"text":{"overview":"~178","capabilities":"~179","deployment":"The (Light PPC) variant entered service with Lyran Alliance forces in 3068, initially deployed to formations operating against Clan Jade Falcon in the Melissia Theater. The redesigned vehicle proved more adaptable than its predecessor, capable of contributing direct fire with its PPC turret when mission requirements demanded it, while retaining the indirect fire capability that defined the original design. GrummanCRI continued producing the variant at Melissia through the Jihad and into the Dark Age, with the facility's ownership eventually transferring to Clan Jade Falcon during the late Dark Age and subsequently to the Vesper Marches in the ilClan era. Whether production continued under subsequent owners remains uncertain.","battleHistory":"~180"}},"name":"~181","model":"(Light PPC)","tonnage":65,"era":"jihad","engineType":"cell","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":50,"left":19,"right":19,"rear":19,"turret":20,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~182",["machine-gun-heavy","front","#1",null],["machine-gun-heavy","front","#2",null],["light-ppc","turret","#3",null],["machine-gun-heavy","turret","#4",null],["machine-gun-heavy","turret","#5",null],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#6","sniper-artillery"],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#7","sniper-artillery"],["~187",null,"#8","machine-gun-heavy"],["~187",null,"#9","machine-gun-heavy"]]}},"504":{"tro":{"production":"~188","components":{"engine":"285","armor":"Protec 12"},"text":{"overview":"~189","capabilities":"~190","deployment":"The M1 targets planetary militias and independent operators priced out of the M1A. It retains the Marksman's core Gauss Rifle capability and robust chassis at a lower acquisition cost, trading the M1A's energy backup weapons and multi-missile flexibility for a short-range missile package oriented toward close engagements. The absence of CASE reflects a design trade-off accepted by cost-conscious buyers. The M1 performs best in defensive positions and urban environments, where its Gauss Rifle can be used at favorable ranges and the short-range missile systems can be brought to bear against closing targets.","battleHistory":"~191"}},"name":"Marksman MBT","model":"M1","tonnage":95,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":62,"left":41,"right":41,"rear":28,"turret":34,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~56","$v":["~192",["mrm-10","turret","#1",null,null],["mrm-10","turret","#2",null,null],"~193","~194","~195","~196","~197","~198","~199","~200",["~143",null,"#11","standard-gauss-rifle",null],["~143",null,"#12","standard-gauss-rifle",null],["ammo-is-mrm-standard",null,"#13","mrm-10",null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#14","srm-6",null],["~89",null,"#15","streak-srm-4",null],["~14",null,"#16","machine-gun",true]]}},"505":{"tro":{"production":"~188","components":{"engine":"Edasich Motors 285 Light Fusion","armor":"AmberStar Weave Heavy Ferro"},"text":{"overview":"~189","capabilities":"~190","deployment":"The M1A serves as the primary main battle tank for Republic of the Sphere Protectorate forces, planned for distribution across Protectorate worlds to anchor combined-arms formations. Its long-range Gauss Rifle and ferro-fibrous protection make it well suited to defensive engagements and attritional fighting, where it can hold position and engage approaching enemy armor and BattleMechs at range. The multi-missile launchers and laser battery provide fallback options when the engagement closes or ammunition reserves run low. Cost limits its penetration into militia and private markets, keeping the M1A predominantly in Protectorate and well-funded planetary defense roles.","battleHistory":"~191"}},"name":"Marksman MBT","model":"M1A","tonnage":95,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"cell","armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":73,"left":62,"right":62,"rear":50,"turret":60,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["standard-gauss-rifle","#1","turret",null],["mml-9","#2","turret",null],["mml-9","#3","turret",null],"~201",["medium-laser","#5","turret",null],["medium-laser","#6","turret",null],"~25","~26",["machine-gun","#9","turret",null],["machine-gun","#10","turret",null],["machine-gun","#11","turret",null],"~202","~203",["ammo-lrm-standard","#14",null,"mml-9"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#15",null,"mml-9"],["ammo-srm-standard","#16",null,"mml-9"],["ammo-srm-standard","#17",null,"mml-9"],"~204"]}},"506":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Refit - Jake's Discount Equipment Warehouse","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"COMTEC 400E","targeting":"GroundTracker EE-4"},"components":{"engine":"Unknown XL","armor":"Protec 12"},"text":{"overview":"~189","capabilities":"~190","deployment":"The M1J is a third-party refit produced by Jake's Discount Equipment Warehouse for operators seeking greater area-fire capability from the Marksman chassis. The engine swap reduces the tank's performance ceiling in exchange for tonnage redirected to larger MRM banks, shifting the vehicle toward indirect and mid-range fire support rather than the precision long-range role of the base Marksman. JDEW's independent distribution network gives the M1J a presence across markets outside the main Protectorate supply chain, including peripheral and independent buyer communities.","battleHistory":"~191"}},"name":"Marksman MBT","model":"M1J","tonnage":95,"era":"late-rep","engineType":"xl","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":65,"left":50,"right":50,"rear":35,"turret":41,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~128","$v":["~192",["mrm-20","turret","#1",null,null],["mrm-20","turret","#2",null,null],"~193","~194","~195","~196","~197","~198","~199","~200",["~14",null,"#11",true,"machine-gun"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#12",null,"srm-6"],["~89",null,"#13",null,"streak-srm-4"],["ammo-is-mrm-standard",null,"#14",null,"mrm-20"],["ammo-is-mrm-standard",null,"#15",null,"mrm-20"],["~143",null,"#16",null,"standard-gauss-rifle"],["~143",null,"#17",null,"standard-gauss-rifle"]]}},"507":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Various","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Q2 Block 9","targeting":"~205"},"components":"~206","text":{"overview":"~207","capabilities":"~208","deployment":"The Mars serves as the primary heavy assault vehicle in Clan garrison clusters and second-line toumans. Its long-range weapons array and ECM protection make it a natural anchor for defensive positions. Clan Hell's Horses deploys the Mars alongside BattleMechs in combined-arms stars, using its firepower to suppress enemy formations while faster units maneuver for advantage. The slow speed restricts its utility in pursuit or exploitation roles, so Mars tanks are typically assigned to hold terrain rather than advance. After 3072, the type appeared in Horses garrisons across their Inner Sphere occupation zone, and commercial sales made it available to buyers throughout the periphery of Clan space.","battleHistory":"~209"}},"name":"Mars Assault Vehicle","tonnage":100,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":2,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~210","equipment":{"$k":"~10","$v":["~211",["clan-autocannon-lbx-10","#1","front",null,null],["clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv","#2","front",null,null],["clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv","#3","front",null,null],["clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv","#4","front",null,null],["clan-streak-srm-6","#5","right",null,null],["clan-streak-srm-6","#6","left",null,null],["clan-er-large-laser","#7","turret",null,null],["clan-gauss-rifle","#8","turret",null,null],["clan-machine-gun","#9","turret",null,null],["clan-machine-gun","#10","turret",null,null],["~14","#11",null,true,"clan-machine-gun"],["~212","#12",null,null,"clan-gauss-rifle"],["~212","#13",null,null,"clan-gauss-rifle"],["~54","#14",null,null,"clan-streak-srm-6"],["~54","#15",null,null,"clan-streak-srm-6"],["~213","#16",null,null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~213","#17",null,null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~213","#18",null,null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~214","#19",null,null,"clan-autocannon-lbx-10"],["~215","#20",null,null,"clan-autocannon-lbx-10"]]}},"508":{"tro":{"production":"~216","components":"~206","text":{"overview":"~207","capabilities":"~208","deployment":"The ATM variant supplements the base model's long-range profile with the advanced targeting missile system's flexibility to select ammunition type by engagement range. This adaptability suits defensive positions where distances are unpredictable. The replacement of the Extended Range Large Laser with a Heavy Large Laser trades reach for raw damage output at closer ranges, allowing the variant to transition more smoothly between fire support and direct engagement roles. Clan Hell's Horses deployed this configuration where mixed-range engagements were anticipated, and Swedenborg production at Kirchbach made it accessible through commercial channels during the Dark Age.","battleHistory":"~209"}},"name":"Mars Assault Vehicle","model":"(ATM)","tonnage":100,"tech":"clan","era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":2,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~210","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~217",["atm-9","#1","front",null],["atm-9","#2","front",null],["atm-9","#3","front",null],["clan-streak-srm-4","#4","right",null],["clan-streak-srm-4","#5","left",null],["large-heavy-laser","#6","turret",null],["clan-gauss-rifle","#7","turret",null],["clan-machine-gun","#8","turret",null],["clan-machine-gun","#9","turret",null],["~14","#10",null,"clan-machine-gun"],["~212","#11",null,"clan-gauss-rifle"],["~212","#12",null,"clan-gauss-rifle"],["~54","#13",null,"clan-streak-srm-4"],["~54","#14",null,"clan-streak-srm-4"],["ammo-clan-atm-er","#15",null,"atm-9"],["ammo-clan-atm-standard","#16",null,"atm-9"],["ammo-clan-atm-he","#17",null,"atm-9"],["ammo-clan-atm-er","#18",null,"atm-9"],["ammo-clan-atm-standard","#19",null,"atm-9"],["ammo-clan-atm-he","#20",null,"atm-9"],["ammo-clan-atm-er","#21",null,"atm-9"],["ammo-clan-atm-standard","#22",null,"atm-9"],["ammo-clan-atm-he","#23",null,"atm-9"]]}},"509":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Odin Manufacturing","primaryFactory":"Orestes","communications":"Q2 Block 9","targeting":"~205"},"components":"~218","text":{"overview":"~207","capabilities":"~208","deployment":"The HAG variant entered service during the Jihad as the Rasalhague Dominion sought to stiffen its defenses following the Ice Hellion raids. Produced exclusively at Orestes by Odin Manufacturing, it combines the XL engine's speed improvement with the heaviest armor of any Mars configuration and a Class 30 Hyper-Assault Gauss Rifle capable of inflicting devastating structural damage at range. The Ghost Bears did not share the design freely. Trial or salvage remained the primary means of acquisition outside the Dominion. The HAG remained a Rasalhague Dominion frontline asset through the Dark Age and into the ilClan era, valued for its combination of firepower and survivability.","battleHistory":"~209"}},"name":"Mars Assault Vehicle","model":"(HAG)","tonnage":100,"tech":"clan","era":"jihad","engineType":"clan_xl","cruiseMP":2,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":105,"left":70,"right":70,"rear":50,"turret":60,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~217",["hyper-assault-gauss-30","#1","front",null],"~219","~220","~221",["clan-er-large-laser","#5","turret",null],["clan-gauss-rifle","#6","turret",null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#7","turret",null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#8","turret",null],["~213","#9",null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~213","#10",null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~213","#11",null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~213","#12",null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],"~222",["~212","#14",null,"clan-gauss-rifle"],["~212","#15",null,"clan-gauss-rifle"],["~223","#16",null,"hyper-assault-gauss-30"],["~223","#17",null,"hyper-assault-gauss-30"],["~223","#18",null,"hyper-assault-gauss-30"],["~40","#19",null,"ap-gauss-rifle"]]}},"510":{"tro":{"production":"~216","components":"~218","text":{"overview":"~207","capabilities":"~208","deployment":"The XL variant trades half a ton of armor and the machine guns for an extralight fusion engine, raising top speed to 54 km/h. This improvement gives the vehicle enough tactical mobility to reposition under fire and keep pace with faster supporting elements, addressing the base model's most persistent operational criticism. Clan Hell's Horses developed the XL specifically for their combined-arms formations, where the ability to shift the heavy fire-support line mattered more than anti-infantry capability. Production at both Niles and, after 3074, Kirchbach ensured sufficient numbers for frontline Hell's Horses service.","battleHistory":"~209"}},"name":"Mars Assault Vehicle","model":"(XL)","tonnage":100,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-inv","engineType":"clan_xl","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":48,"left":42,"right":42,"rear":34,"turret":45,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~217",["clan-autocannon-lbx-10","#1","front",null],"~219","~220","~221",["clan-streak-srm-6","#5","right",null],["clan-streak-srm-6","#6","left",null],["clan-er-large-laser","#7","turret",null],["clan-gauss-rifle","#8","turret",null],["~212","#9",null,"clan-gauss-rifle"],["~212","#10",null,"clan-gauss-rifle"],["~54","#11",null,"clan-streak-srm-6"],["~54","#12",null,"clan-streak-srm-6"],"~222",["~213","#14",null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~213","#15",null,"clan-lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~214","#16",null,"clan-autocannon-lbx-10"],["~215","#17",null,"clan-autocannon-lbx-10"]]}},"511":{"tro":{"production":"~224","components":"~225","text":{"overview":"~226","capabilities":"~227","deployment":"The standard Marten fills scout and special forces insertion roles across militia and mercenary formations throughout the Inner Sphere and Periphery. Its speed allows it to penetrate contested airspace and withdraw before opposition concentrates, and its SRM launcher provides limited self-defense against light infantry and unarmored vehicles. Commanders typically assign it to reconnaissance missions and single-pass insertion operations rather than sustained combat tasks. Its mechanical simplicity also makes it attractive to budget-constrained forces lacking the logistics to support more complex VTOL platforms.","battleHistory":"~228"}},"quirks":"~9","name":"Marten Scout VTOL","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":1,"era":"late-sw-rn","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":13,"armorAllocation":"~6","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~229",["~13","rear","#1",null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#2","srm-2"]]}},"512":{"tro":{"production":"~224","components":"~225","text":{"overview":"~226","capabilities":"~227","deployment":"The infantry transport variant replaces the SRM 2 launcher and its ammunition with expanded passenger capacity, allowing it to carry a larger infantry payload at the cost of essentially all offensive capability. The variant requires dedicated escort or suppression support for any contested insertion, as it carries no meaningful self-defense armament. It saw limited distribution but found use with formations that prioritized troop delivery and judged the standard model's firepower an acceptable trade for additional lift capacity.","battleHistory":"~228"}},"quirks":"~9","name":"Marten Scout VTOL","model":"(Infantry)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":3,"era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":13,"armorAllocation":"~6","equipment":[{"tag":"~13","location":"rear","uuid":"#0"}]},"513":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Taurus Territorial Industries|Marian Arms Incorporated","primaryFactory":"Sterope|Alphard (MH)","communications":"Neil 6000-x","targeting":"TracTex Alpha-1"},"components":"~230","text":{"overview":"~231","capabilities":"~232","deployment":"The standard Maultier represents the full-specification production model with ferro-fibrous armor and Streak missile technology. It entered production at Taurus Territorial Industries on Sterope once Pinard Protectorates Limited successfully replicated the Streak SRM system in 3054. Marian Arms on Alphard also produces this configuration, making it the most widely available version. Infantry commanders across the Trinity Alliance and the Marian Hegemony favor it for rapid platoon insertion operations where the Streak launcher provides a measure of defensive capability during the vulnerable deployment phase.","battleHistory":"~233"}},"name":"Maultier Hover APC","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":3,"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":9,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":14,"left":10,"right":10,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~234"},"514":{"tro":{"production":"~235","components":"~230","text":{"overview":"~231","capabilities":"~232","deployment":"Introduced in 3070, the BA variant reflects Taurian efforts to adapt the Maultier for battle armor transport operations. The conversion required difficult trade-offs between armament, protection, and carrying capacity on a 15-ton chassis. Designers explored three unpalatable options: stripping out the Streak launcher, reducing the already minimal armor, or replacing the internal combustion engine with a far more expensive fusion plant. The resulting compromise remains somewhat controversial among TDF logisticians who debate whether the small platform can meaningfully support battle armor operations.","battleHistory":"~233"}},"name":"Maultier Hover APC","model":"(BA)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":4,"era":"jihad","cruiseMP":9,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":9,"left":6,"right":6,"rear":5,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~234"},"515":{"tro":{"production":"~235","components":{"engine":"Magna 50 ICE","armor":"Standard"},"text":{"overview":"~231","capabilities":"~232","deployment":"The Basic was the first Maultier produced by the Taurian Concordat after rediscovering the design in 3019. Limited by the Concordat's inability to manufacture ferro-fibrous armor or Streak missile launchers, TTI substituted standard armor and a conventional SRM-2. The weight savings from the lighter standard launcher were redirected to additional armor plating, giving the Basic comparable overall protection to the original design despite using less advanced materials. No longer in active production, surviving examples remain in service with Taurian militia formations and a handful of Federated Suns units that captured them over the years.","battleHistory":"~233"}},"name":"Maultier Hover APC","model":"(Basic)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":3,"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":9,"armorAllocation":{"front":16,"left":11,"right":11,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~16"},"516":{"tro":{"production":"~235","components":{"engine":"50 Fusion","armor":"~236"},"text":{"overview":"~231","capabilities":"~232","deployment":"Introduced in 3070, the Fusion variant replaces the standard internal combustion engine with a fusion power plant, eliminating the vehicle's dependence on conventional fuel supplies. This configuration also swaps the Streak SRM-2 for an extended-range medium laser, removing all ammunition dependencies and creating a logistics-friendly transport. The significantly higher cost of the fusion engine limits production numbers, making this the most expensive Maultier variant by a considerable margin.","battleHistory":"~233"}},"name":"Maultier Hover APC","model":"(Fusion)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":4,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":10,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~237","equipment":[{"tag":"er-medium-laser","location":"front","uuid":"#0"}]},"517":{"tro":{"production":"~235","components":"~230","text":{"overview":"~231","capabilities":"~232","deployment":"The Intermediate variant represents the midpoint in the Concordat's efforts to restore the Maultier to full Star League specifications. Introduced in 3043 after Taurus Territorial Industries mastered ferro-fibrous armor production, it upgrades the Basic's standard armor to Durallex Light Ferro-Fibrous while retaining the conventional SRM-2 launcher. This configuration served as the TDF's primary Maultier model for roughly a decade until the Streak system became available in 3054.","battleHistory":"~233"}},"name":"Maultier Hover APC","model":"(Intermediate)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":3,"era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":9,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~237","equipment":"~16"},"518":{"tro":{"production":"~235","components":"~230","text":{"overview":"~231","capabilities":"~232","deployment":"The MG variant prioritizes infantry carrying capacity over defensive firepower. Based on the Intermediate configuration, it replaces the SRM launcher and its ammunition with an expanded four-ton infantry bay and a single machine gun with half a ton of ammunition. Introduced in 3070, this variant is favored by commanders who need maximum troop capacity and view the Maultier's speed as its primary defense rather than any mounted weapon system.","battleHistory":"~233"}},"name":"Maultier Hover APC","model":"(MG)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":4,"era":"jihad","cruiseMP":9,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~237","equipment":{"$k":"~128","$v":["~238",["~14",null,"#1",true,"machine-gun"]]}},"519":{"tro":{"production":"~239","components":"~240","text":{"overview":"~241","capabilities":"~242","deployment":"The standard Mauna Kea appears in almost every command position in wet navies across the Inner Sphere and near Periphery. Licensed clones are produced under various local names by manufacturers on dozens of worlds. The Draconis Combine's Nav Hull facility on New Samarkand has maintained the longest continuous production run, while the Lyran world of Skye produces occasional hulls through Skye Pleasure Craft Ltd. The vessel's strategic importance lies in its ubiquity rather than any single capability.","battleHistory":"~243"}},"name":"~244","tonnage":85,"motiveType":"naval-surface","bays":"~245","armorAllocation":"~246","equipment":{"$k":"~131","$v":["~11","~247","~248","~249","~250","~251",["autocannon-standard-c","#6","turret",null,null],["lrm-15","#7","turret",null,null],["ammo-lrm-standard","#8",null,"lrm-15",null],["ammo-lrm-standard","#9",null,"lrm-15",null],["~14","#10",null,"machine-gun",true],["ammo-ac-10-standard","#11",null,"autocannon-standard-c",null],["ammo-ac-10-standard","#12",null,"autocannon-standard-c",null]]}},"520":{"tro":{"production":"~239","components":"~240","text":{"overview":"~241","capabilities":"~242","deployment":"The LB-X variant is the Jihad-era modernization of the Mauna Kea, replacing the classic Mydron autocannon with an LB 10-X and adding Artemis IV fire control to the missile launcher. Earthwerks developed this specification at their Calloway VI facility, and production has been active since the Dark Age era. The upgraded fire control and cluster munition capability significantly improve the vessel's ability to engage small, fast craft that might threaten a naval task force.","battleHistory":"~243"}},"name":"~244","model":"(LB-X)","tonnage":85,"motiveType":"naval-surface","bays":"~245","era":"jihad","armorAllocation":"~246","equipment":{"$k":"~10","$v":["~11","~247","~248","~249","~250","~251",["lrm-15-artemis-iv","#6","turret",null,null],["autocannon-lbx-10","#7","turret",null,null],["~14","#8",null,true,"machine-gun"],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv","#9",null,null,"lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv","#10",null,null,"lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["~146","#11",null,null,"autocannon-lbx-10"],["~146","#12",null,null,"autocannon-lbx-10"]]}},"521":{"tro":{"production":"~252","components":"~253","text":{"overview":"~254","capabilities":"~255","deployment":"The standard Maxim (I) is produced exclusively at Scarborough Manufacturers' Al Na'ir facility. It serves primarily with DCMS formations along the Federated Suns border, where battle armor operations are a regular feature of cross-border skirmishing. The design replaced the earlier upgraded Maxim on the Al Na'ir production line after demand shifted decisively toward dedicated battle armor carriers. Scarborough suggests removal of the TAG system to make room for a full battle armor squad, though crews often strip the machine guns instead to preserve indirect fire coordination ability.","battleHistory":"The Maxim (I) owes its existence to one of the most embarrassing episodes in Scarborough Manufacturers' history. When the Draconis Combine Military Services actively sought contracts for a transport specifically designed to carry a battlesuit-equipped squad, Scarborough won the bid with a proposal to upgrade the proven Maxim chassis. The company invested heavily in the project, publicly showing wider doors, specially designed handholds, and seats modified for armored troopers. The DCMS purchased the upgraded Maxim and began distributing it throughout the military.\\n\\nThe design flaw became apparent when a Smoke Jaguar Nova raided McAlister within a week of general distribution. The upgraded Maxim lacked the capability to carry an entire squad of battle armor without losing speed, a critical failing that the original marketing materials had obscured. Contrary to the company's claims, the transport could only carry three battle suits inside its bay during the actual demonstration while maintaining operational speed. Scarborough initially denied the problem and attempted a public display of the Maxim's capabilities, but the demonstration only confirmed the shortcoming. The company quietly returned to the drawing board, and the DCMS began modifying already-purchased transports in the field.\\n\\nThe Blackwell Corporation, manufacturer of the competing Bandit hover tank, seized the opportunity to embarrass Scarborough further. Blackwell launched an advertising campaign built around the slogan \\"Able to carry what we say it can,\\" directly targeting the Maxim's failure to meet its specifications. Sales of the upgraded Maxim declined steadily throughout the late 3050s as units lost confidence in Scarborough's claims. The public relations damage extended beyond the single product, threatening Scarborough's credibility as a military contractor.\\n\\nScarborough's engineers eventually produced a properly redesigned transport that delivered on the original promise. The Maxim (I), released in 3060, featured a genuinely expanded twelve-ton infantry bay capable of carrying battle armor at full operational speed. The redesign stripped most of the heavy missile armament, retaining only a Streak SRM-6, a pair of extended-range medium lasers, and a TAG unit. This weapons reduction was a practical necessity, as the expanded bay consumed mass that had previously been allocated to ordnance.\\n\\nThe Maxim (I) found wide circulation with DCMS units stationed along the Federated Suns border, where the increase of battle armor troops in infantry regiments created constant demand for dedicated carriers. The anti-personnel variant of the earlier upgraded Maxim was forced out of production as demand shifted decisively toward battle armor transport. Scarborough retooled the Al Na'ir production line accordingly, with the infantry transport version replacing the combat-oriented upgraded Maxim. The fire-support variant continued in parallel production, mounting a single LRM-15 rack alongside the original pair of LRM-5s to provide dedicated long-range bombardment capability.\\n\\nField modifications of the Maxim (I) emerged during the late Civil War and Jihad eras, taking advantage of vehicle-compatible C3 field modification kits. Some units replaced the machine guns for a C3 slave computer, while others went further and stripped out the cargo space and TAG to install a C3 master. The most extreme field variant paired C3 master computers with other similarly modified hover tanks and VTOLs, creating ad hoc networked reconnaissance and transport lances. The Company Command variant formalized this concept with twin C3 masters and a Guardian ECM suite, serving as a mobile command node for combined-arms formations."}},"name":"~256","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":12,"era":"clan-inv","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~257","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["streak-srm-6","turret","#0",null],"~258",["er-medium-laser","turret","#2",null],"~259",["~89",null,"#4","streak-srm-6"]]}},"522":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Scarborough Talky 2","targeting":"Scarborough Tracky 1"},"components":"~253","text":{"overview":"~254","capabilities":"~255","deployment":"The Company Command variant formalizes field modifications that emerged during the late Civil War era. Twin C3 master computers and a Guardian ECM suite transform the Maxim (I) into a mobile command platform for combined-arms formations. The armor and weaponry remain identical to the standard infantry variant, preserving the vehicle's ability to transport battle armor troops while simultaneously providing electronic warfare support and C3 network coordination for an entire company-level formation.","battleHistory":"The Maxim (I) owes its existence to one of the most embarrassing episodes in Scarborough Manufacturers' history. When the Draconis Combine Military Services actively sought contracts for a transport specifically designed to carry a battlesuit-equipped squad, Scarborough won the bid with a proposal to upgrade the proven Maxim chassis. The company invested heavily in the project, publicly demonstrating wider doors, specially designed handholds, and seats modified for armored troopers. The DCMS purchased the upgraded Maxim and began distributing it throughout the military.\\n\\nThe design flaw became apparent when a Smoke Jaguar Nova raided McAlister within a week of general distribution. The upgraded Maxim lacked the capability to carry an entire squad of battle armor without losing speed, a critical failing that the original marketing materials had obscured. Contrary to the company's claims, the transport could only carry three battle suits inside its bay during the actual demonstration while maintaining operational speed. Scarborough initially denied the problem and attempted a public display of the Maxim's capabilities, but the demonstration only confirmed the shortcoming. The company quietly returned to the drawing board, and the DCMS began modifying already-purchased transports in the field.\\n\\nThe Blackwell Corporation, manufacturer of the competing Bandit hover tank, seized the opportunity to embarrass Scarborough further. Blackwell launched an advertising campaign built around the slogan \\"Able to carry what we say it can,\\" directly targeting the Maxim's failure to meet its specifications. Sales of the upgraded Maxim declined steadily throughout the late 3050s as units lost confidence in Scarborough's claims. The public relations damage extended beyond the single product, threatening Scarborough's credibility as a military contractor.\\n\\nScarborough's engineers eventually produced a properly redesigned transport that delivered on the original promise. The Maxim (I), released in 3060, featured a genuinely expanded twelve-ton infantry bay capable of carrying battle armor at full operational speed. The redesign stripped most of the heavy missile armament, retaining only a Streak SRM-6, a pair of extended-range medium lasers, and a TAG unit. This weapons reduction was a practical necessity, as the expanded bay consumed mass that had previously been allocated to ordnance.\\n\\nThe Maxim (I) found wide circulation with DCMS units stationed along the Federated Suns border, where the increase of battle armor troops in infantry regiments created constant demand for dedicated carriers. The anti-personnel variant of the earlier upgraded Maxim was forced out of production as demand shifted decisively toward battle armor transport. Scarborough retooled the Al Na'ir production line accordingly, with the infantry transport version replacing the combat-oriented upgraded Maxim. The fire-support variant continued in parallel production, mounting a single LRM-15 rack alongside the original pair of LRM-5s to provide dedicated long-range bombardment capability.\\n\\nField modifications of the Maxim (I) emerged during the late Civil War and Jihad eras, taking advantage of vehicle-compatible C3 field modification kits. Some units replaced the machine guns for a C3 slave computer, while others went further and stripped out the cargo space and TAG to install a C3 master. The most extreme field variant paired C3 master computers with other similarly modified hover tanks and VTOLs, creating ad hoc networked reconnaissance and transport lances. The Company Command variant formalized this concept with twin C3 masters and a Guardian ECM suite, serving as a mobile command node for combined-arms formations."}},"name":"~256","model":"(Company Command)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":27,"left":25,"right":25,"rear":16,"turret":23,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~159","~260","~261",["streak-srm-6","#3","turret",null],["er-medium-laser","#4","turret",null],"~262","~263","~170"]}},"523":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Cyclops Incorporated","primaryFactory":"Skye","communications":"Maxim New Standard I/O","targeting":"~264"},"components":{"engine":"PowerTech 165 Highlift"},"text":{"overview":"The Maxim Flanker is an experimental prototype developed at Cyclops Incorporated's facility on Skye in 3074. Designed by technician Buford Leopold, the Flanker attempted to reimagine the Maxim's role during troop deployment by mounting sponson turrets and adopting a deliberately asymmetric armor layout. The concept never progressed beyond the prototype stage, and only a single vehicle was constructed.\\n\\nIntended to present a heavily armored broadside to the enemy while deploying infantry from the protected opposite side, the Flanker represented an unconventional and ultimately unsuccessful approach to hover transport tactics. The design attracted brief attention within Cyclops for its creative ambition, though its fundamental tactical assumptions proved flawed under testing conditions.","capabilities":"The Flanker replaces the standard Maxim's turret with a pair of sponson turrets mounted on either side of the hull, each carrying a mix of missile and ballistic weapons. The right sponson mounts an LRM-15 and an SRM-4, while the left sponson carries an SRM-2 and three machine guns. A rear-mounted machine gun provides minimal protection against pursuing infantry. A fusion engine replaces the standard ICE powerplant, increasing the flank speed to 129 kilometers per hour while freeing internal volume.\\n\\nThe deliberate asymmetry of the armor layout places nearly all protection on the left side, with the right side carrying minimal plating. This was intended to allow the vehicle to orbit an enemy position while presenting its armored flank, then deploy infantry from the protected side. In practice, the lack of a top-mounted turret eliminated hull-down engagement options, and the unbalanced armor distribution was not properly compensated for in the vehicle's guidance system. The hover skirt proved particularly vulnerable to flanking fire, and the overall concept failed to account for the basic reality that hovercraft are especially susceptible to side hits that damage their mobility.","deployment":"The sole Maxim Flanker prototype was constructed at Cyclops Incorporated's Skye facility in 3074 and never progressed beyond initial testing. The prototype's poor performance during live-fire exercises led Cyclops to cancel the project. No production variants were authorized, and the design exists only as an experimental curiosity in the company's archives.","battleHistory":"The Maxim Flanker emerged from the personal initiative of Buford Leopold, a technician at Cyclops Incorporated on Skye who envisioned a new approach to troop deployment from hover transports. Leopold proposed that a Maxim could be modified to present a single heavily armored side to the enemy while using sponson-mounted weapons to suppress opposition during infantry deployment. The concept attracted enough internal interest at Cyclops to secure prototype funding, with construction beginning in 3074 during the height of the Jihad.\\n\\nLeopold's design was apparently developed without significant input from combat veterans, and the prototype's first live-fire exercise highlighted numerous fundamental problems. The asymmetric armor made the vehicle dangerously vulnerable to flanking fire from the right side, a weakness that any competent opponent could exploit within moments of engagement. The sponson turrets lacked the firing arcs of a conventional top turret, and the unbalanced weight distribution was never properly addressed in the flight control software. The exercise ended when the Flanker's air skirt collapsed during a low-speed maneuver, leaving the prototype stationary and exposed.\\n\\nCyclops Incorporated shelved the project after the failed trials. The Flanker's design files were retained in the company archives, and the single prototype was reportedly scrapped for parts. The project is occasionally referenced within Cyclops as a cautionary example of the gap between theoretical innovation and battlefield practicality."}},"name":"Maxim Flanker","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"sponsonTurrets":true,"sponsonMounts":true,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":4,"doors":1}],"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":{"front":20,"left":10,"right":46,"rear":12,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","location","uuid","sponson","feedsWeaponTag","halfTon"],"$v":[["lrm-15","right","#0",true,null,null],["srm-4","right","#1",true,null,null],["srm-2","left","#2",true,null,null],["machine-gun","left","#3",true,null,null],["machine-gun","left","#4",true,null,null],["machine-gun","left","#5",true,null,null],["machine-gun","rear","#6",null,null,null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#7",null,"lrm-15",null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#8",null,"lrm-15",null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#9",null,"srm-4",null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#10",null,"srm-2",null],["~14",null,"#11",null,"machine-gun",true]]}},"524":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Scarborough Manufacturers|Cyclops Incorporated|Hellespont Industrials","primaryFactory":"Al Na'ir|Skye|Sian","communications":"Maxim New Standard I/O","targeting":"~264"},"components":"~265","text":{"overview":"~266","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"The standard Maxim remains the most widely fielded variant, produced continuously since 2689 across multiple factories and three successor manufacturers. Every major Inner Sphere military fields the base model in significant numbers, and it is a common sight in mercenary and planetary militia formations as well. The design's longevity owes as much to its reliability and parts availability as to its combat effectiveness.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":3,"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~270","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~271","~272",["srm-2","right","#2",null],["srm-2","left","#3",null],["lrm-5","rear","#4",null],"~273","~274","~275",["srm-6","turret","#8",null],["~14",null,"#9","machine-gun"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#10","srm-6"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#11","srm-2"],"~276"]}},"525":{"tro":{"production":"~252","components":"~253","text":{"overview":"~277","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"The first major factory upgrade to the Maxim, produced by Scarborough Manufacturers on Al Na'ir beginning in 3052. The upgrade integrates Streak SRM-2 launchers, a TAG unit, and ProtecTech 6 ferro-fibrous armor onto the proven chassis while retaining the core weapons layout. Originally intended for the DCMS, the upgraded Maxim saw distribution across multiple states before production shifted to newer variants.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(3052 Upgrade)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":3,"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~257","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~271","~272","~278","~279","~67","~273","~274",["is-tag","turret","#7",null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#8","srm-6"],["~89",null,"#9","streak-srm-2"],"~280",["~14",null,"#11","machine-gun"]]}},"526":{"tro":{"production":"~252","components":"~253","text":{"overview":"~266","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"Designed to counter heavy concentrations of conventional infantry, this Scarborough variant replaces the Streak SRM-2 launchers with additional machine guns. The configuration proved effective against unarmored infantry formations but became increasingly obsolete as battle armor proliferated across the Inner Sphere. Production was relatively brief, ceasing as demand shifted toward battle armor transport variants.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(Anti-Infantry)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":3,"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":{"front":25,"left":23,"right":23,"rear":16,"turret":17,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~271","~272",["machine-gun","front","#2",null],["machine-gun","right","#3",null],["machine-gun","right","#4",null],["machine-gun","left","#5",null],["machine-gun","left","#6",null],"~281",["machine-gun","turret","#8",null],["machine-gun","turret","#9",null],["is-tag","turret","#10",null],"~282","~276",["~14",null,"#13","machine-gun"],["~14",null,"#14","machine-gun"]]}},"527":{"tro":{"production":"~252","components":"~253","text":{"overview":"~283","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"Designed by engineers at Scarborough's manufacturing plants, this factory modification expands the Maxim's infantry bay to accommodate a squad of battle armor. The TAG system is removed to free the necessary internal volume. This variant represents Scarborough's official answer to the growing demand for battle armor carriers, distinct from the field modifications that troops developed independently.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(BA Factory Upgrade)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":4,"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~257","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~271","~272","~278","~279","~67","~273","~274","~70",["~89",null,"#8","streak-srm-2"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#9","lrm-5"],["~14",null,"#10","machine-gun"]]}},"528":{"tro":{"production":"~284","components":"~265","text":{"overview":"~277","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"Developed by troops in the field rather than factory engineers, this variant also transports a squad of battle armor but achieves the necessary bay expansion by removing the machine guns and ammunition instead of the TAG system. The freed weight also allows for additional armor plating. This field modification appeared independently across multiple commands, reflecting the universal need for battle armor transport capacity.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(BA Field Upgrade)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":4,"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":28,"left":28,"right":28,"rear":17,"turret":24,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~271","~272","~278","~279","~67",["is-tag","turret","#5",null],"~69","~161",["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#8","lrm-5"]]}},"529":{"tro":{"production":"~284","components":"~265","text":{"overview":"~266","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"This variant trades the infantry bay entirely for a C3 master computer, converting the Maxim from a transport into a mobile command and networking node. Because the C3 master can function as a TAG system, the turret-mounted TAG is also removed. Additional armor replaces the machine guns and ammunition. The C3M variant operates as the hub of a C3 lance, coordinating fire for networked vehicles and BattleMechs.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(C3M)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","era":"jihad","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":31,"left":26,"right":26,"rear":18,"turret":24,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~159","~285","~286","~287","~288","~289",["ammo-srm-standard","#6",null,"srm-6"],["~89","#7",null,"streak-srm-2"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#8",null,"lrm-5"]]}},"530":{"tro":{"production":"~284","components":"~265","text":{"overview":"~290","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"Used in conjunction with the C3 master variant, this modification removes the machine guns and ammunition to install a C3 slave computer while expanding the infantry bay to four tons for a battle armor squad. The TAG system is retained, allowing the C3S variant to designate targets for semi-guided munitions while feeding targeting data through the C3 network. This combination of transport capability and networked fire support makes it a versatile element in C3-equipped formations.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(C3S)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":4,"era":"jihad","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~257","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~100","~285","~286","~287","~288","~289","~263",["ammo-srm-standard","#7",null,"srm-6"],"~291",["ammo-lrm-standard","#9",null,"lrm-5"]]}},"531":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"WC Site 4","primaryFactory":"Arc-Royal","communications":"Unknown (Clan)","targeting":"Unknown (Clan)"},"components":{"engine":"Unknown (Clan Fusion)","armor":"Unknown (Clan Standard)"},"text":{"overview":"~283","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"Retrofitted with Clan technology at WC Site 4 on Arc-Royal, the Clan variant replaces the ICE powerplant with a Clan fusion engine and installs lighter missile systems to free weight for an expanded weapons array and a six-ton infantry bay. An ECM suite and CASE provide electronic protection and ammunition safety. Production began in 3059 by refitting Skye-built chassis, with all production transitioning to Arc-Royal by 3063. Sales initially extended to interested Clans including the Diamond Sharks and Nova Cats, though salvage has become the primary means of spread as the other Clans have established their own production capabilities.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(Clan)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":6,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":25,"left":22,"right":22,"rear":12,"turret":24,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~10","$v":["~211",["clan-lrm-15","#1","front",null,null],["clan-srm-2","#2","right",null,null],["clan-srm-2","#3","left",null,null],["clan-machine-gun","#4","turret",null,null],["clan-machine-gun","#5","turret",null,null],["clan-streak-srm-6","#6","turret",null,null],["~14","#7",null,true,"clan-machine-gun"],["ammo-srm-standard","#8",null,null,"clan-srm-2"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#9",null,null,"clan-lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#10",null,null,"clan-lrm-15"],["~54","#11",null,null,"clan-streak-srm-6"]]}},"532":{"tro":{"production":"~284","components":{"engine":"PowerTech Unknown (Fuel Cell)","armor":"ArcShield V"},"text":{"overview":"~277","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"The Escort variant is a standardized combat refit that eliminates the infantry bay entirely in favor of an improved weapons array. A fuel cell engine frees sufficient mass to upgrade the turret-mounted SRM-6 to a Streak version, while the machine guns are reorganized into a turret-mounted array. A pair of MML-5 launchers replace the standard LRM racks, with a single rear-mounted launcher for withdrawal coverage. Improved one-shot SRM-2 packs on each side provide emergency close-range firepower. The Escort serves as a dedicated combat escort for transport variants, providing organic fire support without risking embarked infantry.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(Escort)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","era":"late-rep","engineType":"compact","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~257","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["mml-5","front","#0",null],["mml-5","front","#1",null],["srm-2-ios","right","#2",null],["srm-2-ios","left","#3",null],["mml-5","rear","#4",null],"~273","~274","~275",["is-machine-gun-array","turret","#8",null],["streak-srm-6","turret","#9",null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#10","mml-5"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#11","mml-5"],["~14",null,"#12","machine-gun"],["~89",null,"#13","streak-srm-6"]]}},"533":{"tro":{"production":"~252","components":"~253","text":{"overview":"~290","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"The fire-support variant strips the short-range missile systems and TAG in favor of a turret-mounted FarFire LRM-15, converting the Maxim into a dedicated indirect fire platform. This variant is often deployed in pairs or lances alongside standard Maxims, providing sustained long-range bombardment while the transports deliver their infantry. Production at Scarborough's Al Na'ir plant continued through the Jihad era.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(Fire Support)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":3,"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~257","equipment":{"$k":"~56","$v":["~292","~293","~294","~295","~296","~297","~298",["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#7","lrm-5",null],"~299"]}},"534":{"tro":{"production":"~284","components":"~265","text":{"overview":"~290","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"A simple field modification that moves the side-mounted SRM-2 launchers into the turret, giving them a wider field of fire. This is one of the oldest and most common Maxim modifications, performed by unit technicians with minimal tooling. The rearrangement improves the vehicle's ability to engage targets of opportunity without changing the overall weapons loadout.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(SRM2)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":3,"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~270","equipment":{"$k":"~56","$v":["~292","~293",["lrm-5","rear","#2",null,null],"~295","~296",["machine-gun","turret","#5",null,null],["srm-2","turret","#6",null,null],["srm-2","turret","#7",null,null],["srm-6","turret","#8",null,null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#9","srm-6",null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#10","srm-2",null],["~14",null,"#11","machine-gun",true],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#12","lrm-5",null]]}},"535":{"tro":{"production":"~284","components":"~265","text":{"overview":"~283","capabilities":"~267","deployment":"Another common field modification, this variant replaces the LRM-5 racks with SRM-4 launchers, sacrificing long-range fire support in favor of heavier close-range firepower. One SRM-4 is traded for additional ammunition storage, ensuring the remaining launchers can sustain fire through extended engagements. This configuration is particularly popular in urban environments where engagement ranges rarely exceed a few hundred meters.","battleHistory":"~268"}},"name":"~269","model":"(SRM4)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":3,"era":"early-sw","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~270","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["srm-4","front","#0",null],["srm-2","right","#1",null],["srm-2","left","#2",null],["srm-4","rear","#3",null],["machine-gun","turret","#4",null],"~273","~274","~281",["~14",null,"#8","machine-gun"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#9","srm-4"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#10","srm-4"],"~282",["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#12","srm-6"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#13","srm-2"]]}},"536":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Cyclops Industries, Inc.|Scarborough Manufacturers","primaryFactory":"Skye|Al Na'ir","communications":"Cyclops Surelink","targeting":"Scarborough Tracky 3"},"components":"~300","text":{"overview":"The Maxim Mk II Transport is a joint venture between Cyclops Incorporated and Scarborough Manufacturers, developed in the aftermath of the Jihad for the newly formed Republic of the Sphere's Armed Forces. Introduced in 3083, the Mk II was designed from the ground up as a dedicated battle armor carrier, prioritizing troop capacity and survivability over the heavy weaponry that defined its predecessor.\\n\\nThe design philosophy behind the Mk II serves as a deliberate departure from the original Maxim's role as a heavily armed combat transport. By minimizing the weapons suite, the Mk II encourages crews to withdraw from dangerous situations rather than engage, extending the vehicle's operational lifespan and reducing replacement costs for the cash-strapped Republic military.","capabilities":"~301","deployment":"The standard Mk II is produced at both the Cyclops facility on Skye and the Scarborough plant on Al Na'ir. Originally built exclusively for the Republic Armed Forces, the design has since entered service with successor states including the Draconis Combine, the Lyran Commonwealth, and Clan occupation forces. Approximately ninety percent of Mk II production is the standard configuration, reflecting the RAF's emphasis on maximizing battle armor transport capacity across its formations.","battleHistory":"~302"}},"name":"Maxim Mk II Transport","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":16,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~303","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["mml-3","#1","turret",null],["mml-3","#2","turret",null],["ammo-lrm-standard","#3",null,"mml-3"],["ammo-srm-standard","#4",null,"mml-3"]]}},"537":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Cyclops Surelink","targeting":"Scarborough Tracky 3"},"components":"~300","text":{"overview":"The Maxim Mk II Transport is a joint venture between Cyclops Incorporated and Scarborough Manufacturers, developed in the aftermath of the Jihad for the newly formed Republic of the Sphere's Armed Forces. Introduced in 3083, the Mk II was designed from the ground up as a dedicated battle armor carrier, prioritizing troop capacity and survivability over the heavy weaponry that defined its predecessor.\\n\\nThe design philosophy behind the Mk II is a deliberate departure from the original Maxim's role as a heavily armed combat transport. By minimizing the weapons suite, the Mk II encourages crews to withdraw from dangerous situations rather than engage, extending the vehicle's operational lifespan and reducing replacement costs for the cash-strapped Republic military.","capabilities":"~301","deployment":"A field refit variant of the Mk II, the ECM configuration downgrades the turret weaponry to a single Streak SRM-2 while installing a Guardian ECM suite. The full sixteen-ton infantry bay is retained, making this variant an effective electronic warfare platform that can still fulfill its primary transport role. The ECM variant is particularly valued in formations operating against opponents with sophisticated targeting networks or semi-guided munitions.","battleHistory":"~302"}},"name":"Maxim Mk II Transport","model":"(ECM)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":16,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~303","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~91",["streak-srm-2","#1","turret",null],["~89","#2",null,"streak-srm-2"],["~89","#3",null,"streak-srm-2"]]}},"538":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Earthwerks Ltd.","primaryFactory":"Tiber (FWL)"},"components":{"chassis":"Earthwerks","engine":"Unknown (300 Light Fusion)"},"text":{"overview":"~304","capabilities":"~305","deployment":"The Mk. IX is the modern revival of the Merkava line, produced by Earthwerks Incorporated at a new facility on Tiber in the Principality of Regulus. Introduced in 3073, it replaces the autocannon with a light Gauss rifle and upgrades the short-range missile system with Streak technology. A light fusion engine maintains the same speed as the Mk. VIII while freeing tonnage for the heavier primary weapon. The Mk. IX was deployed primarily to the Regulan Hussars, who received the tank in large numbers during the latter half of the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~306"}},"name":"Merkava Heavy Tank","model":"Mk IX","tonnage":75,"era":"jihad","engineType":"cell","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~307","equipment":{"$k":"~56","$v":["~238","~308","~294",["streak-srm-4","turret","#3",null,null],["gauss-rifle-light","turret","#4",null,null],"~297","~298",["~89",null,"#7","streak-srm-4",null],"~299","~309",["~127",null,"#10","gauss-rifle-light",null],["~127",null,"#11","gauss-rifle-light",null]]}},"539":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Leopard Armor","primaryFactory":"Terra","communications":"Teledon 11","targeting":"Baltex K400"},"components":{"chassis":"Leopard Armor","engine":"Vlar 300","armor":"PanzerSlab"},"text":{"overview":"~304","capabilities":"~305","deployment":"The Mk. VIII represented the definitive Merkava, benefiting from a fully refined fusion engine that pushed its top speed to 64 kph. Its upgraded weapons suite and ten tons of standard armor made it the most capable variant in the series. The Mk. VIII served as a frontline asset in the Hegemony Armed Forces from 2465 through the Reunification War, after which Leopard Armor discontinued the production line in 2601. Retired vehicles were distributed to Star League member states and Hegemony militias. Few survived the Succession Wars intact, as most were stripped for their valuable fusion engines.","battleHistory":"~306"}},"name":"Merkava Heavy Tank","model":"Mk VIII","tonnage":75,"engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~307","equipment":{"$k":"~56","$v":["~238","~308","~294",["srm-4","turret","#3",null,null],["autocannon-standard-b","turret","#4",null,null],"~297","~298",["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#7","srm-4",null],"~299","~309",["ammo-ac-5-standard",null,"#10","autocannon-standard-b",null]]}},"540":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Michealson Heavy Industries|Michealson Heavy Industries|Michealson Heavy Industries","primaryFactory":"June|Ruchbah|Terra","communications":"Garret Garret Supremesound","targeting":"Garret Garret GRNDTRK 9"},"components":{"engine":"Michaelson Heavy Industries Michaelson 155 Fusion","armor":"~18"},"text":{"overview":"The MHI Amphibious APC is a thirty-five-ton wheeled personnel carrier developed by Michaelson Heavy Industries and introduced in 3101. The design emerged from a post-Jihad commercial reality: the gradual drawdown of BattleMech formations across the Inner Sphere had reoriented military procurement toward affordable support vehicles for infantry-heavy commands. Michaelson targeted a specific market gap, producing a wheeled carrier capable of transporting a full infantry complement across water obstacles that most conventional wheeled vehicles could not negotiate. Built at facilities on June, Ruchbah, and Terra, the APC sold steadily to Republic of the Sphere garrisons, Federated Suns and Lyran Commonwealth militia commands, and eventually to Capellan Confederation border units, establishing Michaelson as a significant participant in Dark Age-era wheeled vehicle production.","capabilities":"The Amphibious APC's defining characteristic is its full amphibious capability, allowing the vehicle to enter and exit water obstacles while carrying a complete infantry complement. A Michaelson 155 Fusion power plant gives the vehicle a cruising speed of 54 kph and a flank speed of 86 kph, fast enough to avoid most engagements rather than fight through them. A single Diverse Optics Extended Range Medium Laser provides modest self-defense, sufficient to discourage light pursuit without drawing the vehicle into a sustained firefight. StarSlab/1 armor delivers protection appropriate to moving through contested zones rather than holding ground under sustained fire. The Garret Supremesound communications system and Garret GRNDTRK 9 targeting array provide baseline electronics reliable enough for dispersed combined-arms operations.\\n\\nThe vehicle's eight tons of infantry capacity is its primary combat contribution. The APC is not a frontline combatant, and its speed makes rapid repositioning the preferred response to unexpected contact rather than a fighting withdrawal. Commanders who task it as a fighting platform rather than a transport risk both the vehicle and the troops aboard. Its greatest tactical value lies in its ability to deliver infantry to terrain that wheeled and tracked vehicles without amphibious fittings cannot access, giving combined-arms formations options that dismounted movement alone could never match.","deployment":"The Amphibious APC found its primary market among militia forces, planetary guard commands, and second-line formations where water crossing capability addressed tactical gaps in non-mech orders of battle. June-produced vehicles entered Federated Suns militia inventories, while Ruchbah output served local garrisons and spread to neighboring Capellan Confederation border commands through resale channels. The Republic of the Sphere adopted the vehicle widely for combined-arms garrison formations, favoring its amphibious mobility for defending territories with significant water terrain features.\\n\\nCommanders consistently pair the APC with conventional armor or faster screening elements, positioning it to exploit water obstacles rather than sustain engagement on open ground, where its light armament makes it unsuitable for direct fire missions. The vehicle is most effective when operators understand its role as an enabler rather than a combatant, using speed and amphibious access to deliver infantry to positions that heavier elements cannot reach.","battleHistory":"Michaelson Heavy Industries began development of the Amphibious APC in the post-Jihad years, when the systematic decommissioning of BattleMech formations across the Inner Sphere opened commercial space for capable conventional military vehicles. The June facility led engineering, with production tooling simultaneously prepared at Ruchbah and the Terra facility that Michaelson shared with other contractors. The vehicle entered service in 3101, coinciding with Michaelson's broader Dark Age expansion phase and timed to meet anticipated Republic garrison demand.\\n\\nInitial customers were primarily Republic of the Sphere planetary commands, whose combined-arms orientation made amphibious transport operationally essential on worlds with significant water terrain. As Republic procurement relationships deepened, the APC spread to Federated Suns and Lyran Commonwealth militia inventories. Ruchbah-built vehicles reached Capellan Confederation border units through resale channels, reflecting the design's appeal to formations needing transport flexibility without the logistics burden of a dedicated military program.\\n\\nThe APC's most significant operational test came in December 3130 on Finsterwalde, when a Tamarind-Abbey force struck the lightly garrisoned Lyran Commonwealth world at Tarkington Point. When the defending militia's heavier units were forced back, an infantry commander loaded reserve troops into available APCs and crossed the Bay of Balmoral in storm conditions, using the vehicles' amphibious capability to reach the waterfront engagement and contribute to the defense of the position. The action became a minor illustration of exactly the scenario Michaelson had built the vehicle to address. Production continued at all three facilities through the late Dark Age, though the Ruchbah line passed through independence and Capellan Confederation control as the Inner Sphere's political borders shifted."}},"name":"MHI Amphibious APC","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"wheeled","troopSpace":8,"chassisModifications":["fully-amphibious"],"era":"late-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":49,"left":22,"right":22,"rear":15,"turret":20,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":[{"tag":"er-medium-laser","location":"turret","uuid":"#0"}]},"541":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Michaelson Heavy Industries|Michaelson Heavy Industries|Michaelson Heavy Industries","primaryFactory":"June|Ruchbah|Terra","communications":"Garret T12-S","targeting":"Garret D2j"},"components":{"chassis":"~310","engine":"GM 240 XL","armor":"Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/7 Heavy Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"The MHI Defense AA Tank entered service in 3106 as Michaelson Heavy Industries' purpose-built mobile anti-aircraft platform for the Republic Armed Forces. Designed to protect ground formations from airborne threats while providing supplemental fire support, the sixty-ton tracked vehicle represented Michaelson's ambitious expansion beyond the VTOL designs that had defined the company for decades. Produced at three facilities across the Inner Sphere, the Defense AA became a mainstay of Republic and Federated Suns armor formations. Its combination of versatile autocannon armament, respectable armor protection, and limited amphibious capability made it a flexible asset for combined-arms commanders operating across diverse terrain.","capabilities":"The Defense AA Tank's primary armament consists of twin turret-mounted Armstrong Class-B LB 10-X Autocannons, a versatile pairing that gives the vehicle exceptional flexibility against both aerial and ground targets. Crews can load cluster munitions for devastating anti-aircraft barrages or solid slug rounds for engaging armored vehicles, with four tons of CASE-protected ammunition providing sustained endurance across extended operations. Three hull-mounted SperryBrowning Light Machine Guns offer close defense against infantry, backed by a half ton of CASE-protected machine gun ammunition.\\n\\nA GM 240 XL fusion engine drives the tracked chassis to a maximum road speed of 64 kilometers per hour, keeping the tank competitive with the mechanized formations it supports. The extralight powerplant saves considerable weight but introduces vulnerability to side hits, a calculated trade-off for a vehicle expected to operate behind the forward line. Eight tons of StarSlab/7 Heavy Ferro-Fibrous armor provide solid durability for its weight class, though the protection falls short of what dedicated assault tanks carry. Limited amphibious equipment allows the Defense AA to ford shallow waterways, extending its operational reach across terrain that would stop purely land-bound vehicles. Mobile anti-air platforms are often the first targets enemy scouts designate during surprise attacks, and the ability to reposition across light water obstacles helps the Defense AA survive initial contact.","deployment":"Defense AA Tanks are found across the Inner Sphere, serving in the armies of the Republic of the Sphere, the Federated Suns, and various other states whose worlds host Michaelson factories. The vehicle is a popular choice for irregular units the RAF deployed secretly beyond the Fortress for reconnaissance and harassment duties. Its versatile LB-X autocannons and amphibious capability make it attractive to formations operating on water-rich worlds where conventional anti-air assets struggle to maintain area coverage. Federated Suns commands have integrated the Defense AA into combined-arms air defense networks, pairing it with mobile sensor platforms and fighter screen elements. The large ammunition capacity allows crews to reserve a portion of their stowage for standard high-explosive shells in case ground units close to direct engagement range.","battleHistory":"Michaelson Heavy Industries developed the Defense AA Tank during a period of significant corporate transformation. Originally a smaller Federated Suns contractor specializing in VTOLs, the company nearly went bankrupt after the Jihad when the Word of Blake blockade of Ruchbah crippled its home manufacturing operations. Forced to sell intellectual property to remain solvent, Michaelson watched competitors flood the market with copies of its own designs. Recovery came through Republic of the Sphere contracts in the early 3080s, including the Trireme battle armor transport VTOL and the highly successful Bishop Transport VTOL introduced in 3093. These programs funded expansion to a new plant on Terra and a production line on June.\\n\\nThe Defense AA Tank filled a recognized capability gap in Republic formations that had grown reliant on air superiority but lacked sufficient ground-based air defense. Republican forces secretly deployed the vehicle among units operating beyond the Fortress Republic's walls, screening formations conducting reconnaissance and harassment in neighboring states. During the clash on Dyev in 3141, RAF resistance cells suffering from Combine close-air support arranged a bold operation against a concealed airbase. Defense AA Tanks smuggled to the world aboard JES I Tactical Missile Carriers ambushed returning fighters and then broke through the perimeter to destroy grounded aircraft on the tarmac, costing the Combine squadron more than half its strength.\\n\\nDefense AA crews of the Com Guards claimed a fearsome toll during the organization's last stand on Epsilon Eridani. Though the final combat drop of the Fourteenth Hastati sealed the Com Guards' fate, several Defense AA tanks destroyed a number of the falling Hastati before ground forces could overrun their positions."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"quirk-anti-aircraft-targeting"}],"name":"MHI Defense AA Tank","tonnage":60,"chassisModifications":["limited-amphibious"],"era":"late-rep","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":44,"left":31,"right":31,"rear":37,"turret":25,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~10","$v":["~119",["machine-gun-light","#1","front",null,null],["machine-gun-light","#2","front",null,null],["machine-gun-light","#3","front",null,null],["autocannon-lbx-10","#4","turret",null,null],["autocannon-lbx-10","#5","turret",null,null],["~129","#6",null,true,"machine-gun-light"],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster","#7",null,null,"autocannon-lbx-10"],"~147",["~146","#9",null,null,"autocannon-lbx-10"],["~146","#10",null,null,"autocannon-lbx-10"]]}},"542":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~311","primaryFactory":"Kathil|Tikonov|Warlock","communications":"Overlook J-9","targeting":"Brightstar 17 Mk. II"},"components":"~312","text":{"overview":"~313","capabilities":"~314","deployment":"The base Minion was produced at General Motors' Kathil annex and both Ceres Metals facilities on Tikonov and Warlock, giving the design a presence in both Federated Suns and Capellan Confederation orders of battle. During the FedCom Civil War, the vehicle appeared on both sides of the conflict as factories changed hands. Its C³ slave integration makes it a natural fit for mixed combined-arms lances on both sides of the Capellan March.","battleHistory":"~315"}},"name":"~316","motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":9,"armorAllocation":"~317","equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":["~177","~318","~319"]}},"543":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Ceres Metals Industries|Ceres Metals Industries","primaryFactory":"Tikonov|Warlock","communications":"Overlook J-9","targeting":"Brightstar 17 Mk. II"},"components":"~312","text":{"overview":"~313","capabilities":"~314","deployment":"The TAG-equipped variant serves as an artillery spotter, coordinating semi-guided and Arrow IV artillery strikes for units operating within its C³ network. Produced at the Tikonov and Warlock facilities under Capellan management, this variant was a natural fit for Capellan artillery-heavy formations. Its speed allows it to keep pace with mobile artillery assets while the network connection ensures targeting data reaches supporting batteries with minimal delay.","battleHistory":"~315"}},"name":"~316","model":"(TAG)","motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":9,"armorAllocation":"~317","equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":["~78","~79",["is-tag","front","#2"]]}},"544":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"General Motors","primaryFactory":"Kathil","communications":"Overlook J-9","targeting":"Brightstar 17 Mk. II"},"components":"~312","text":{"overview":"~313","capabilities":"~314","deployment":"This Kathil-exclusive redesign, developed for the Federated Suns market, replaces the C³ slave with a targeting computer, transforming the Minion from a network node into an independent precision combat platform. The targeting computer greatly enhances the accuracy of its pulse lasers at the cost of network interoperability, making this variant a close-quarters threat in its own right. It is found primarily in Federated Suns formations operating outside the reach of an established C³ net.","battleHistory":"~315"}},"name":"~316","model":"(Targeting Computer)","motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":9,"armorAllocation":"~317","equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":[["targeting-computer","#0",null],"~318","~319"]}},"545":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Ghost Bear Facility Gamma-1|Hector MotorWorks Facility Gamma","primaryFactory":"Bearclaw|Hector","communications":"Consolidated BMR 6c","targeting":"TRTTS Mark II CWS"},"components":"~320","text":{"overview":"~321","capabilities":"~322","deployment":"The standard Mithras serves across the light armor formations of nearly all Clans, deployed in small clusters to exploit its speed advantage over heavier vehicles. The design performs best in flanking assignments, where its combination of long-range autocannon and laser backup can harass enemy formations without exposing the tank to sustained return fire. It works particularly well alongside Elemental battle armor, which can screen the vehicle's vulnerable rear arc while the Mithras uses its weapons to suppress or fix opposing forces. The wide distribution of the design across Clan toumans makes it one of the most commonly encountered combat vehicles in both the homeworlds and the Clan occupation zones.","battleHistory":"~323"}},"name":"Mithras Light Tank","tonnage":25,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":6,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~324","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["er-medium-laser-clan","front","#0",null],["er-medium-laser-clan","turret","#1",null],["clan-autocannon-uac-2","turret","#2",null],["ammo-clan-ultra-ac-2-standard",null,"#3","clan-autocannon-uac-2"]]}},"546":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Hector MotorWorks Facility Gamma","primaryFactory":"Hector","communications":"Consolidated BMR 6c","targeting":"TRTTS Mark II CWS"},"components":"~320","text":{"overview":"~321","capabilities":"~322","deployment":"The ERLL variant is fielded primarily by forces operating from the Hector facility during the Jihad and early Dark Age periods. Its Clan Extended Range Large Laser and AP Gauss Rifle combination, guided by a Targeting Computer, makes the variant a capable long-range fire support platform that can engage targets well beyond the reach of the base model. The ERLL trades the autocannon's sustained-fire flexibility for concentrated precision, making it most effective in defensive positions or when attached to larger formations where it can specialize in picking off light vehicles and battle armor at extended range.","battleHistory":"~323"}},"name":"Mithras Light Tank","model":"(ERLL)","tonnage":25,"tech":"clan","era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":6,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~324","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["clan-targeting-computer","#0",null,null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#1","front",null],["clan-er-large-laser","#2","turret",null],["~40","#3",null,"ap-gauss-rifle"]]}},"547":{"tro":{"production":"~325","components":"~326","text":{"overview":"~327","capabilities":"~328","deployment":"The standard fusion-powered Mobile HQ with its original medium laser armament is the rarest and most valuable configuration. Those still in service are typically assigned to prestigious units such as the Sword of Light regiments in the Combine or the Lyran Guard in the Commonwealth. Wolf's Dragoons maintained five fully operational examples, considered among the finest command assets outside of a DropShip. Restoration efforts following the technological renaissance have slowly increased operational numbers, though the vehicle remains uncommon.","battleHistory":"~329"}},"name":"Mobile Headquarters","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","bays":"~5","era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~330","equipment":"~331"},"548":{"tro":{"production":"~325","components":"~332","text":{"overview":"~327","capabilities":"~328","deployment":"This variant combines the ICE engine conversion with the large laser turret swap, creating a vehicle that has lost its fusion engine and gained a more powerful weapon. The power amplifier requirements of the large laser further constrain the vehicle's already reduced electronics suite. These doubly-modified vehicles are found primarily among Combine and Confederation forces that performed both modifications sequentially as their Mobile HQs degraded over the centuries.","battleHistory":"~329"}},"name":"Mobile Headquarters","model":"(ICE - LL)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"era":"early-sw","armorAllocation":{"front":10,"left":11,"right":11,"rear":8,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":["~333",["large-laser","#1","front"]]}},"549":{"tro":{"production":"~325","components":"~332","text":{"overview":"~334","capabilities":"~328","deployment":"The ICE-LRM variant pairs the internal combustion engine conversion with the LRM-10 weapon swap, producing a vehicle that has been stripped of its fusion engine and most of its original communications capability. Of all the Mobile HQ variants, this configuration has strayed furthest from the vehicle's original design intent, functioning more as a lightly armored fire support platform with vestigial command capability than a true mobile headquarters.","battleHistory":"~329"}},"name":"Mobile Headquarters","model":"(ICE - LRM)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","bays":"~5","era":"early-sw","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~330","equipment":"~335"},"550":{"tro":{"production":"~325","components":"~332","text":{"overview":"The Mobile Headquarters is the original dedicated command vehicle of the Star League Defense Force, designed and manufactured by Star League Defense Industries on Mars beginning in 2620. For over a century, the Mobile HQ served as the nerve center of large-scale planetary operations, gathering information from ground forces, aerospace assets, and orbiting spacecraft to present a comprehensive battlefield picture to the commanding officer. The vehicle changed little during its production run, proof of the soundness of its original design. Its destruction during the Amaris Coup and the subsequent loss of replacement technology made surviving examples among the most prized military assets in the Inner Sphere.","capabilities":"~328","deployment":"The ICE conversion represents the most radical field modification of the Mobile HQ. During the worst years of the Succession Wars, fusion engines were stripped from these vehicles and transplanted into light BattleMechs, with internal combustion engines fitted as replacements. Power amplifiers and generators were added to operate the laser and remaining communications equipment. This salvage job was common among mercenary commands, peripheral states, and any force that valued a functioning BattleMech over a partially operational command vehicle.","battleHistory":"~329"}},"name":"Mobile Headquarters","model":"(ICE)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","era":"early-sw","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~330","equipment":"~331"},"551":{"tro":{"production":"~325","components":"~326","text":{"overview":"~334","capabilities":"~328","deployment":"The large laser variant was a common Succession Wars-era modification favored by the Draconis Combine and Capellan Confederation. Replacing the medium laser with a large laser gave the command vehicle a more serious defensive punch at the cost of increased heat generation. These modifications were often made to vehicles that were already partially disabled, with technicians reasoning that a more powerful weapon compensated somewhat for degraded communications capability.","battleHistory":"~329"}},"name":"Mobile Headquarters","model":"(LL)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~330","equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":["~333",["large-laser","#1","turret"]]}},"552":{"tro":{"production":"~325","components":"~326","text":{"overview":"The Mobile Headquarters is the original dedicated command vehicle of the Star League Defense Force, designed and manufactured by Star League Defense Industries on Mars beginning in 2620. For over a century, the Mobile HQ served as the nerve center of large-scale planetary operations, gathering information from ground forces, aerospace assets, and orbiting spacecraft to present a comprehensive battlefield picture to the commanding officer. The vehicle changed little during its production run, a sign of the soundness of its original design. Its destruction during the Amaris Coup and the subsequent loss of replacement technology made surviving examples among the most prized military assets in the Inner Sphere.","capabilities":"~328","deployment":"The LRM variant replaced the standard medium laser with an LRM-10 launcher and a ton of ammunition, sacrificing direct-fire capability for indirect support. This modification reduced the communications equipment to two tons, significantly degrading the vehicle's primary function. Draconis Combine and Capellan Confederation forces favored this configuration, preferring the ability to contribute fire support over preserving coordination capability that their technicians could no longer fully maintain.","battleHistory":"~329"}},"name":"Mobile Headquarters","model":"(LRM)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~330","equipment":"~335"},"553":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~336","primaryFactory":"Mars","communications":"ROMTECH 100","targeting":"O/P 10-40 LTR"},"components":{"chassis":"~336","engine":"Robel 110 ICE","armor":"~18"},"text":{"overview":"~337","capabilities":"~338","deployment":"The Mobile Long Tom is typically deployed in batteries attached to regimental-level formations, operating as the fire support spine of any combined-arms group. Road-bound and requiring significant logistical support, batteries rarely operate independently and are almost always paired with dedicated escort elements for anti-air and anti-armor protection. Inner Sphere militaries routinely station one or two batteries near major garrisons and population centers as deterrents against enemy landings. Operators use concealment among urban terrain and reverse slopes to deny aerospace assets a targeting solution. The constant need for refueling and ammunition resupply makes extended operations in isolated areas impractical without dedicated support elements.","battleHistory":"~339"}},"name":"~340","model":"LT-MOB-25","tonnage":75,"hasTurret":false,"era":"star-league","armorAllocation":{"front":16,"left":16,"right":16,"rear":16,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~10","$v":["~11",["long-tom-artillery","#1","front",null,null],"~248",["machine-gun","#3","right",null,null],"~250",["machine-gun","#5","left",null,null],["trailer-hitch","#6","rear",null,null],["~14","#7",null,true,"machine-gun"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#8",null,null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#9",null,null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#10",null,null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#11",null,null,"long-tom-artillery"]]}},"554":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Bithinian Ballistics|Lycomb-Davion IntroTech|Austen-Armstrong Industries|Taurus Territorial Industries","primaryFactory":"Bithinia|New Avalon|Bainsville|Sterope","communications":"ROMTECH 100","targeting":"O/P 10-40 LTR"},"components":{"chassis":"Bithinian Ballistics, Lycomb-Davion IntroTech, Austen-Armstrong Industries, Taurus Territorial Industries","engine":"Robel 195 ICE","armor":"Brooks, Inc. StarSlab/1 Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"~337","capabilities":"~338","deployment":"The LT-MOB-25F offers improved road speed compared to its predecessor, allowing batteries to reposition more quickly after fire missions and reducing vulnerability to counterbattery response. CASE protection reduces the risk of catastrophic ammunition explosion. Like the LT-MOB-25, the system requires a full complement of trailers to conduct meaningful fire support missions, and the gun carriage carries limited ammunition reserves on its own. Post-Blackout production at Bithinian Ballistics, Lycomb-Davion IntroTech, Austen-Armstrong Industries, and Taurus Territorial Industries made the LT-MOB-25F the most widely distributed variant during the Dark Age, appearing across Free Worlds League successor states, Capellan forces, the Federated Suns, and Periphery formations.","battleHistory":"~339"}},"name":"~340","model":"LT-MOB-25F","tonnage":65,"hasTurret":false,"era":"star-league","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":15,"left":16,"right":16,"rear":15,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["long-tom-artillery","#1","front",null],["machine-gun","#2","right",null],"~341","~342",["machine-gun","#5","left",null],["trailer-hitch","#6","rear",null],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#7",null,"long-tom-artillery"],"~343","~344"]}},"555":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~345","primaryFactory":"Kirklin"},"components":{"chassis":"~345"},"text":{"overview":"~337","capabilities":"~338","deployment":"The LT-MOB-95 eliminates the convoy requirement entirely, integrating the Long Tom's weapons, ammunition, and control systems into a single tracked chassis. Introduced by the Federated Suns and produced by Kallon Weapon Industries on Kirklin, this variant is substantially more mobile and easier to maintain than legacy convoy systems. The elimination of trailer dependencies removes the road restriction and logistical burden that constrained its predecessors, allowing LT-MOB-95 batteries to operate in terrain and conditions that would have been impractical for the wagon-train design. Production remains within the Federated Suns sphere of influence.","battleHistory":"~339"}},"name":"~340","model":"LT-MOB-95","tonnage":95,"era":"early-rep","cruiseMP":2,"armorType":"hardened","armorAllocation":{"front":24,"left":24,"right":24,"rear":24,"turret":24,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["~346","#1",null,null],"~261",["long-tom-artillery","#3","front",null],["machine-gun","#4","turret",null],["machine-gun","#5","turret",null],["machine-gun","#6","turret",null],["machine-gun","#7","turret",null],"~343",["ammo-long-tom-standard","#9",null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#10",null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#11",null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#12",null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#13",null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#14",null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#15",null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#16",null,"long-tom-artillery"],["ammo-long-tom-standard","#17",null,"long-tom-artillery"],"~204"]}},"556":{"tro":{"production":"~347","components":"~348","text":{"overview":"~349","capabilities":"~350","deployment":"The M1 entered service as the Duchy of Andurien's primary frontline armor, distributed among militia formations and Free Worlds League units garrisoning Duchy territory. Its long-range fire combination proved well suited for engaging at maximum distance, while the integrated TAG allowed it to call in artillery strikes on fortified positions. M1 units were among the first Molkes exported to Duchy-aligned states following the Jihad, and they remain the most widely distributed variant in former League space.","battleHistory":"~351"}},"name":"Moltke MBT","model":"M1","tonnage":75,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~352","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~353","~354",["er-large-laser","turret","#2",null],["~127",null,"#3","gauss-rifle-light"],["~127",null,"#4","gauss-rifle-light"],"~355"]}},"557":{"tro":{"production":"~347","components":"~348","text":{"overview":"~349","capabilities":"~350","deployment":"The M2 was developed to complement M1 formations in combined-arms engagements, providing close-range saturation firepower that the M1 lacked. Its five multi-missile launchers give formation commanders flexibility in ammunition type across a single engagement, and the Snub-Nose PPC delivers reliable direct-fire at brawling ranges without heat concerns that affect energy weapon-heavy designs. The M2 saw broad export distribution alongside the M1, becoming a common pairing in Duchy-affiliated garrison formations where the two variants' range profiles complemented each other.","battleHistory":"~351"}},"name":"Moltke MBT","model":"M2","tonnage":75,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~352","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["mml-7","front","#0",null],["snub-nose-ppc","turret","#1",null],["mml-7","turret","#2",null],["mml-7","turret","#3",null],["mml-7","turret","#4",null],["mml-7","turret","#5",null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#6","mml-7"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#7","mml-7"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#8","mml-7"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#9","mml-7"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#10","mml-7"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#11","mml-7"]]}},"558":{"tro":{"production":"~347","components":"~348","text":{"overview":"~349","capabilities":"~350","deployment":"The M3 entered service with Andurien forces during the later Jihad and became the preferred command vehicle in combined-arms formations, with its TAG system and plasma rifle making it effective against infantry-heavy threats and enabling fire direction for artillery assets. Its LB 10-X autocannons offer flexibility between solid slugs for armor engagements and cluster munitions for vehicle or infantry suppression, though managing two separate ammunition types and the plasma rifle's reserve demands careful logistical planning. Sales of the M3 have been more restricted than earlier variants, with Andurien authorities limiting availability to trusted political partners within the former League sphere.","battleHistory":"~351"}},"name":"Moltke MBT","model":"M3","tonnage":75,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":4,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~352","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["autocannon-lbx-10","turret","#0",null],"~166",["plasma-rifle","turret","#2",null],"~259","~167",["~146",null,"#5","autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster",null,"#6","autocannon-lbx-10"],["~130",null,"#7","plasma-rifle"],["~130",null,"#8","plasma-rifle"]]}},"559":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Nav Hull","primaryFactory":"New Samarkand","communications":"Various Various","targeting":"Various Various"},"components":{"chassis":"Nav Hull","engine":"Various 195 ICE","armor":"ArcShield Maxi II"},"text":{"overview":"The Monitor Naval Vessel was developed by Nav Hull, a small company founded by entrepreneur Robert Habeas in the mid-twenty-ninth century. Habeas had traveled to numerous water-rich worlds and observed how planetary defense forces relied on improvised patrol craft, armed wooden barges fitted with recoilless rifles and small SRM tubes, to police their rivers and coastal regions. Dedicated military riverine vessels existed for wealthier states, but light-industrial worlds had no practical options. Nav Hull addressed this gap not by building a conventional factory, but by designing the Monitor as an unassembled kit. Customers, typically planetary governments and militia forces, sourced the hull, power system, and combustion engine locally from available suppliers. Nav Hull supplied the weapons package and turret assembly as the primary value-added components, keeping overhead low and spreading production risk across the supply chain.","capabilities":"The Monitor's armament is built around two 185mm ChemJet Gun autocannons mounted in a turret assembly derived from the Demolisher heavy tank. The turret itself comes from Demolisher production, with Nav Hull adapting the mounting hardware for aquatic operations. Three Harvester 2k SRM launchers are positioned along the sides and rear of the vessel, providing close-range coverage against ambushing infantry in dense riverside terrain. Commanders frequently load inferno rounds for engagements along riverbanks, as the flammable gel floats and burns on the water surface long enough to deny the shoreline to assaulting forces for a brief but critical window.\\n\\nThe Monitor carries eight tons of ArcShield Maxi II armor, a practical but modest protection level. Some of this plating comes directly from the Demolisher turret, while the remainder consists of flat panels, a cost-reduction measure Nav Hull adopted to keep kits affordable. The armor is sufficient against small arms fire and light weapons typical of riverine piracy and guerrilla actions, though it provides limited protection against dedicated anti-armor systems. The vessel's most operationally significant characteristic is its half-meter draft, which allows it to operate in the shallowest river channels and wetland systems that deny access to heavier naval assets. The tradeoff is that the Monitor relies on its shallow-water mobility rather than armor to survive encounters with better-armed opponents.","deployment":"The Monitor is deployed primarily by planetary defense forces and militia units throughout the Draconis Combine, with the heaviest concentrations on worlds featuring extensive river systems, delta regions, or shallow coastal waters. Because the kit model allows customers to substitute locally available components, individual examples vary considerably in their electronics suites and armor configurations, depending on what parts the purchasing government could supply. Most end up in low-priority garrison assignments, patrolling waterways that conventional armored units cannot easily access. The Galedon Military District has historically been the Monitor's strongest market, given the extensive river delta systems found on several district worlds. A small number of examples have appeared in the hands of pirate bands operating along the Combine's spinward frontier, likely captured from militia units or obtained through gray-market kit sales.","battleHistory":"Robert Habeas established Nav Hull after observing the improvised naval forces maintained across water-rich worlds throughout the Inner Sphere. These forces typically lacked purpose-built military vessels, relying instead on converted fishing boats and barges. Habeas lacked the capital to build a conventional manufacturing facility or ship finished vehicles across interstellar distances, but recognized that the kit-assembly model shifted the most difficult logistical problems to the customer. By requiring buyers to source their own hulls and engines locally, Nav Hull kept its own overhead minimal and avoided the warehousing and transport costs that made small-run vehicle production uneconomical for minor manufacturers.\\n\\nNav Hull began delivering Monitor kits in 2910, operating from New Samarkand under Draconis Combine jurisdiction. The kits found a ready market among Combine worlds with significant inland waterways and coastal regions, where conventional armored transports could not reach fortified positions deep in river systems. The design's reliance on locally available parts also appealed to garrison commanders who struggled to maintain supply lines for more sophisticated equipment.\\n\\nThe Fourth Succession War severely disrupted Nav Hull's operations. Commerce disruptions made it impossible to gather complete kits for assembly, and back orders accumulated as the company struggled to source consistent materials. Following the war's conclusion, Nav Hull negotiated a supply agreement with New Samarkand Metals for discounted raw materials, stabilizing production and allowing the company to work through its order backlog.\\n\\nThe Monitor saw meaningful combat during the War of 3039, when Draconis Combine forces employed several vessels in defense of Athenry's Larsen Island Chain. Operating in shallow coastal waters, Monitor crews used their autocannons to strike at Federated Suns beach emplacements while using their shallow draft to maneuver through terrain that heavier vessels could not access. Several crews deliberately beached their vessels at tactical positions, using the hull as cover for infantry units caught in the open. The performance validated the Monitor's core design concept, demonstrating that an affordable, kit-assembled vehicle could still contribute meaningfully to planetary defense operations."}},"name":"Monitor Naval Vessel","tonnage":75,"motiveType":"naval-surface","troopSpace":1.5,"era":"late-sw-lt","armorAllocation":{"front":22,"left":22,"right":22,"rear":22,"turret":40,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["srm-2","right","#0",null],["srm-2","left","#1",null],["srm-2","rear","#2",null],["autocannon-standard-d","turret","#3",null],["autocannon-standard-d","turret","#4",null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#5","srm-2"],["ammo-ac-20-standard",null,"#6","autocannon-standard-d"],"~185","~186"]}},"560":{"tro":{"production":"~356","components":"~357","text":{"overview":"~358","capabilities":"~359","deployment":"The base Morningstar, armed with the Mydron Excel Autocannon 10 and three Slingshot Machine Guns, is the original production configuration. Produced at Kathil, Tikonov, and Warlock, it serves broadly in both Federated Suns and Capellan Confederation formations. The Company Command variant has largely superseded it in dedicated command roles where full C³ master computer integration is required, but the base Morningstar remains in service wherever that level of network infrastructure is unavailable or operationally unnecessary.","battleHistory":"~360"}},"name":"~361","tonnage":60,"motiveType":"wheeled","troopSpace":4,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~362","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~163","~260","~118","~341","~342",["autocannon-standard-c","#5","turret",null],["ammo-ac-10-standard","#6",null,"autocannon-standard-c"],["ammo-ac-10-standard","#7",null,"autocannon-standard-c"],["~14","#8",null,"machine-gun"]]}},"561":{"tro":{"production":"~356","components":"~357","text":{"overview":"~358","capabilities":"~359","deployment":"The Company Command variant is the most widely produced Morningstar configuration, carrying the C³ master computer that defines the vehicle's command role. Deployed primarily in urban defense formations across the Federated Suns and Capellan Confederation, it coordinates mixed lances of BattleMechs and supporting vehicles. Production at Kathil, Tikonov, and Warlock ensures wide distribution, and the Company Command variant remains the standard Morningstar configuration through the ilClan era.","battleHistory":"~360"}},"name":"~361","model":"(Company Command)","tonnage":60,"motiveType":"wheeled","troopSpace":4,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~362","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~163",["ecm-suite","#1",null,null],["c3-computer-master","#2",null,null],["c3-computer-master","#3",null,null],"~24",["machine-gun","#5","right",null],["machine-gun","#6","left",null],["autocannon-standard-b","#7","turret",null],["ammo-ac-5-standard","#8",null,"autocannon-standard-b"],"~344"]}},"562":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Ceres Metals Industries","primaryFactory":"Tikonov","communications":"Overlook J11","targeting":"Brightstar 17 Mk. II"},"components":"~357","text":{"overview":"~358","capabilities":"~359","deployment":"The HPG variant is a specialized Word of Blake adaptation of the Morningstar platform, converting the command vehicle into a mobile communications and coordination hub during the Jihad. Produced in limited numbers at the Tikonov facility, it extended Word of Blake battlefield communications rather than serving a direct combat command role. Its purpose was operationally distinct from the standard Morningstar configurations, making it a rare and purpose-specific variant. The MUPL shows no active production eras for this variant, consistent with its limited, Jihad-era production run under Word of Blake direction.","battleHistory":"~360"}},"name":"~361","model":"(HPG)","tonnage":60,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":6,"era":"jihad","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":43,"left":29,"right":29,"rear":19,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["ground-mobile-hpg","#0",null,null],["gauss-rifle-magshot","#1","front",null],["gauss-rifle-magshot","#2","right",null],["gauss-rifle-magshot","#3","left",null],["~111","#4",null,"gauss-rifle-magshot"],["~111","#5",null,"gauss-rifle-magshot"]]}},"563":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"General Motors|Ceres Metals Industries","primaryFactory":"Kathil|Tikonov","communications":"Overlook J11","targeting":"Brightstar 17 Mk. II"},"components":"~357","text":{"overview":"~358","capabilities":"~359","deployment":"The Laser variant replaces the Mydron Excel Autocannon 10 with a large laser, improving sustained fire capability at the cost of ballistic punch. This configuration appeals to formations prioritizing energy independence over raw damage output, particularly in theaters where autocannon ammunition resupply is unreliable. Production from both Kathil and Tikonov was limited, making this variant less common in the field than the base configuration. It was produced only during the Civil War era, with both lines ceasing output before or during the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~360"}},"name":"~361","model":"(Laser)","tonnage":60,"motiveType":"wheeled","troopSpace":4,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":43,"left":31,"right":31,"rear":20,"turret":25,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~163",["large-laser","#1","front",null],["autocannon-standard-c","#2","turret",null],["ammo-ac-10-standard","#3",null,"autocannon-standard-c"],["ammo-ac-10-standard","#4",null,"autocannon-standard-c"]]}},"564":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~363","primaryFactory":"York|Trondheim","communications":"~364","targeting":"~365"},"components":"~366","text":{"overview":"The Morrígú is an 80-ton tracked fire support vehicle introduced in 3064 by Clan Blood Spirit. Khan Karianna Schmitt commissioned the design in the aftermath of the Absorption War, intending it to stand alongside new developments in the Blood Spirit OmniMech and ProtoMech programs as a demonstration of the Clan's continuing martial capability. The design reflects a century of Blood Spirit engineering philosophy: practical weapons without extravagant technologies, paired with more armor protection than is typically found on Clan combat vehicles of comparable size.\\n\\nKhan Schmitt chose the vehicle's name personally, drawing on the Welsh ancestry revered within Blood Spirit culture to invoke a Celtic goddess of war, emphasizing the Clan's intention to remain a dangerous force. The Morrígú delivers concentrated long-range firepower from a well-protected hull, establishing a clear combat identity as a dedicated fire support platform.","capabilities":"~367","deployment":"The Morrígú entered Blood Spirit service in 3064 and was rapidly distributed to every Galaxy in the Touman, including the newly formed Iota Galaxy: Retribution. Its fire support role suited the Clan's combined-arms approach, providing sustained ranged fire in engagements across the Homeworlds. The vehicle also saw use outside strictly Blood Spirit formations during the Civil War era, reflecting the close ties the Spirits maintained with certain Kindraa factions of Clan Fire Mandrill. Following the transfer of production to Clan Diamond Shark at Trondheim, the Morrígú became commercially available, appearing in mercenary inventories, Periphery forces, and smaller Inner Sphere militaries seeking capable fire support at accessible prices.","battleHistory":"~368"}},"name":"~369","tonnage":80,"tech":"clan","era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","armorAllocation":"~370","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~217","~371","~372",["clan-er-large-laser","#3","turret",null],["clan-er-large-laser","#4","turret",null],"~373",["clan-lrm-15","#6","turret",null],["ammo-lrm-standard","#7",null,"clan-lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#8",null,"clan-lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#9",null,"clan-lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#10",null,"clan-lrm-15"],["ammo-srm-standard","#11",null,"clan-srm-2"]]}},"565":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"New York Vehicle Y2 Facility","primaryFactory":"Trondheim","communications":"~364","targeting":"~365"},"components":"~366","text":{"overview":"The Morrígú is an 80-ton tracked fire support vehicle introduced in 3064 by Clan Blood Spirit. Khan Karianna Schmitt commissioned the design in the aftermath of the Absorption War, intending it to stand alongside new developments in the Blood Spirit OmniMech and ProtoMech programs as a demonstration of the Clan's continuing martial capability. The design reflects a century of Blood Spirit engineering philosophy: practical weapons without extravagant technologies, paired with more armor protection than is typically found on Clan combat vehicles of comparable size.\\n\\nKhan Schmitt chose the vehicle's name personally, drawing on the Welsh ancestry revered within Blood Spirit culture to invoke a Celtic goddess of war, reinforcing the Clan's intention to remain a dangerous force. The Morrígú delivers concentrated long-range firepower from a well-protected hull, establishing a clear combat identity as a dedicated fire support platform.","capabilities":"~367","deployment":"The HAG variant was developed at the New York Vehicle Y2 Facility on Trondheim and introduced in 3083, reflecting the Diamond Sharks' willingness to evolve the design beyond its Blood Spirit origins. The two ER large lasers are replaced with a single HAG/40, retained in the turret alongside the LRM-15s and SRM-2 flankers of the base vehicle. The hyper-assault Gauss rifle delivers exceptional damage in the medium-to-long range bracket and is particularly effective against massed or concentrated targets. The cost is increased dependence on ammunition supply, as the HAG/40 consumes significant stores in sustained engagements. This variant appeals most to forces with guaranteed resupply access seeking maximum burst damage rather than sustained energy fire. Production is exclusive to Trondheim; the original York facility never produced this configuration.","battleHistory":"~368"}},"name":"~369","model":"(HAG)","tonnage":80,"tech":"clan","era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","armorAllocation":"~370","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~217","~371","~372",["hyper-assault-gauss-40","#3","turret",null],["clan-lrm-15","#4","turret",null],"~373",["ammo-srm-standard","#6",null,"clan-srm-2"],["~374","#7",null,"hyper-assault-gauss-40"],["~374","#8",null,"hyper-assault-gauss-40"],["~374","#9",null,"hyper-assault-gauss-40"],["~374","#10",null,"hyper-assault-gauss-40"],["~374","#11",null,"hyper-assault-gauss-40"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#12",null,"clan-lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#13",null,"clan-lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#14",null,"clan-lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#15",null,"clan-lrm-15"]]}},"566":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~363","primaryFactory":"York|Trondheim","communications":"Consolidated Type 2M","targeting":"~365"},"components":"~366","text":{"overview":"The Morrígú is an 80-ton tracked fire support vehicle introduced in 3064 by Clan Blood Spirit. Khan Karianna Schmitt commissioned the design in the aftermath of the Absorption War, intending it to stand alongside new developments in the Blood Spirit OmniMech and ProtoMech programs as a demonstration of the Clan's continuing martial capability. The design reflects a century of Blood Spirit engineering philosophy: practical weapons without extravagant technologies, paired with more armor protection than is typically found on Clan combat vehicles of comparable size.\\n\\nKhan Schmitt chose the vehicle's name personally, drawing on the Welsh ancestry revered within Blood Spirit culture to invoke a Celtic goddess of war, confirming the Clan's intention to remain a dangerous force. The Morrígú delivers concentrated long-range firepower from a well-protected hull, establishing a clear combat identity as a dedicated fire support platform.","capabilities":"~367","deployment":"Introduced in 3067, the Laser variant addresses logistical constraints Blood Spirit units faced during extended Homeworld operations by eliminating all ammunition-dependent systems. Replacing the LRM-15 racks, SRM-2 flankers, and ECM suite with a third Series 1 ER large laser produces a vehicle entirely dependent on heat management rather than resupply. Two tons of armor are sacrificed to accommodate the additional heat sinks, reducing survivability compared to the base model. This tradeoff favors extended raids or defensive actions where resupply is unavailable, but the loss of the ECM suite leaves crews more exposed to precision targeting by well-equipped opponents. Diamond Shark production at Trondheim made this variant available on the commercial market alongside the standard model.","battleHistory":"~368"}},"name":"~369","model":"(Laser)","tonnage":80,"tech":"clan","era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","armorAllocation":{"front":53,"left":35,"right":35,"rear":24,"turret":29,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~15","$v":[["clan-er-large-laser","turret","#0"],["clan-er-large-laser","turret","#1"],["clan-er-large-laser","turret","#2"]]}},"567":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium","primaryFactory":"Richvale|Kalidasa|Layover|Maxwell","communications":"Communicator","targeting":"~59"},"components":{"engine":"180 Fuel Cell","armor":"Aldis Heavy Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"The Mortar Carrier is a sixty-ton tracked combat vehicle mounting three Class 8 Mech Mortars on the standard carrier chassis. Developed by the Quikscell Company in 3083, the design is a Dark Age evolution of the weapons carrier concept, providing indirect fire capability through ballistic mortar bombardment rather than guided missiles. The fuel cell engine and heavy ferro-fibrous armor distinguish it from earlier carrier designs.","capabilities":"The Mortar Carrier mounts three forward-firing Class 8 Mech Mortars, providing indirect fire capability through ballistic bombardment. A 180-rated fuel cell engine replaces the traditional internal combustion powerplant, maintaining fifty-four kilometer per hour speed with cleaner operation. Aldis Heavy Ferro-Fibrous armor provides eighty-nine points of protection, nearly double the standard carrier family's coverage.\\n\\nA Communicator system and FireScan with IndirecTrack targeting provide fire direction capability. The mortar system fires in a high ballistic arc, allowing engagement of targets behind terrain features and buildings. The substantially improved armor protection and fuel cell engine represent a generational leap over earlier carrier designs, though at increased production cost.","deployment":"Quikscell plants on Richvale, Kalidasa, and Layover produce the Mortar Carrier alongside Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium on Maxwell. The design entered service in 3083, filling an indirect fire role in Dark Age-era combined-arms formations.","battleHistory":"The Mortar Carrier was developed by Quikscell in 3083 during the early Dark Age, applying the weapons carrier concept to Mech Mortar technology. The design replaced the SRM Carrier's missile launchers with three Class 8 Mech Mortars on the same basic chassis, though the fuel cell engine and heavy ferro-fibrous armor represent significant departures from earlier carrier designs.\\n\\nProduction was established at Quikscell plants on Richvale, Kalidasa, and Layover, supplemented by Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium on Maxwell. The mortar system's indirect fire capability and area-effect munitions filled a tactical gap between direct-fire carriers and dedicated artillery platforms."}},"name":"Mortar Carrier","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"era":"jihad","engineType":"compact","armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":23,"left":22,"right":22,"rear":22,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["mech-mortar-8","front","#0",null],["mech-mortar-8","front","#1",null],["mech-mortar-8","front","#2",null],["~375",null,"#3","mech-mortar-8"],["~375",null,"#4","mech-mortar-8"],["~375",null,"#5","mech-mortar-8"],["~375",null,"#6","mech-mortar-8"],["~375",null,"#7","mech-mortar-8"],["~375",null,"#8","mech-mortar-8"],["~375",null,"#9","mech-mortar-8"],["~375",null,"#10","mech-mortar-8"]]}},"568":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Quikscell Company","primaryFactory":"Oliver","communications":"Communicator with C3","targeting":"~59"},"components":{"engine":"InterComBust 180 ICE","armor":"~98"},"text":{"overview":"The MRM Carrier is a sixty-ton tracked combat vehicle mounting three MRM-30 launchers on the standard carrier chassis. Developed by the Quikscell Company as a variant of the SRM/LRM Carrier family, the MRM Carrier emerged during the FedCom Civil War era to exploit medium-range missile technology. A C3 slave unit enables networked targeting to compensate for the MRM system's inherent inaccuracy at extended range.","capabilities":"The MRM Carrier mounts three MRM-30 launchers as its primary armament, supplemented by a C3 slave unit for networked targeting data. An InterComBust 180 internal combustion engine maintains the standard fifty-four kilometer per hour top speed. Lexington Ltd. High Grade ferro-fibrous armor provides fifty-three points of protection, improved over the standard carrier's Simple Armor Plate.\\n\\nA Communicator system with C3 link and FireScan with IndirecTrack targeting enable coordination with C3 master units. Each MRM-30 launcher carries only eight rounds, giving the vehicle limited endurance before requiring resupply. The MRM system's inherent scatter pattern makes it most effective against clustered targets or in area-denial roles.","deployment":"Quikscell produced the MRM Carrier at its Oliver facility beginning around 3063. The design found modest adoption across the Inner Sphere, often paired with ECM-equipped units to protect the slow-moving carrier from electronic countermeasures during engagements.","battleHistory":"The MRM Carrier was introduced by Quikscell as a variant during the FedCom Civil War era, mounting three MRM-30 launchers with a C3 slave unit on the standard carrier chassis. TRO:3058 Upgrade describes it as 'almost an entirely new design,' noting that it is often paired with ECM-equipped units as it is too slow to keep up in swift advances. Each launcher carries only eight rounds, requiring frequent resupply.\\n\\nQuikscell produced the MRM Carrier at its Oliver facility for distribution across the Inner Sphere. The design found modest popularity as a middle-ground between the long-range LRM Carrier and the close-range SRM Carrier, though limited ammunition capacity remained a persistent complaint."}},"name":"MRM Carrier","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~99","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~100",["mrm-30","#1","front",null],["mrm-30","#2","front",null],["mrm-30","#3","front",null],["ammo-is-mrm-standard","#4",null,"mrm-30"],["ammo-is-mrm-standard","#5",null,"mrm-30"],["ammo-is-mrm-standard","#6",null,"mrm-30"]]}},"569":{"tro":{"production":"~376","components":"~377","text":{"overview":"~378","capabilities":"~379","deployment":"The standard Musketeer deploys throughout the AFFS in combined-arms battalions and independent screening formations. Its hover chassis provides the cross-terrain flexibility to keep pace with faster strike elements while the RAC/5 delivers sustained fire capable of stripping armor from enemy vehicles and suppressing BattleMechs. AFFS militia units along the Draconis Combine and Capellan borders received early priority deliveries, and the design saw active combat during the FedCom Civil War within its first years of service. It is most effective in open or semi-open terrain where the hover chassis can exploit its speed advantage.","battleHistory":"~380"}},"name":"Musketeer Hover Tank","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":7,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":36,"left":27,"right":27,"rear":18,"turret":26,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],"~381","~382","~383","~384","~168","~28","~29","~291"]}},"570":{"tro":{"production":"~376","components":"~377","text":{"overview":"~378","capabilities":"~379","deployment":"The (3080 Upgrade) incorporates system refinements drawn from Civil War and Jihad combat experience, improving reliability and reducing maintenance demands without substantially altering the vehicle's role or armament philosophy. It is primarily deployed with AFFS formations that accumulated field experience with the base design and requested updated equipment as part of post-Jihad reconstitution. The variant represents the mature production form of the Musketeer line and extended the design's service life through the Dark Age period.","battleHistory":"~380"}},"name":"Musketeer Hover Tank","model":"(3080 Upgrade)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":4,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":38,"left":32,"right":32,"rear":22,"turret":34,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],"~381","~382",["~30","#3",null,"rotary-ac-5"],["~30","#4",null,"rotary-ac-5"],"~168"]}},"571":{"tro":{"production":"~376","components":"~377","text":{"overview":"~378","capabilities":"~379","deployment":"The (Armor) variant replaces the standard model's RAC/5 with a lighter RAC/2 system and divides the recovered tonnage between additional ferro-fibrous armor protection and expanded ammunition storage. The extended range and improved ammunition efficiency of the RAC/2 make this configuration well-suited for extended operations away from established supply lines. Unit commanders who prioritize vehicle survivability and operational endurance over burst-fire output generally favor this variant, and it is frequently assigned to missions where resupply cannot be guaranteed.","battleHistory":"~380"}},"name":"Musketeer Hover Tank","model":"(Armor)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","era":"jihad","cruiseMP":7,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":51,"left":34,"right":34,"rear":23,"turret":28,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],"~381",["rotary-ac-2","#2","turret",null],"~383","~384",["~385","#5",null,"rotary-ac-2"],["~385","#6",null,"rotary-ac-2"],["~385","#7",null,"rotary-ac-2"],"~291"]}},"572":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"New Earth Trading Company|Jalastar Aerospace|Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium","primaryFactory":"New Earth|Panpour|Maxwell","communications":"O/P R Janxiir","targeting":"TargiTrack 717"},"components":{"engine":"Nissan 200","armor":"New Earth Trading Company ArcShield Maxi II"},"text":{"overview":"~386","capabilities":"~387","deployment":"The standard Myrmidon found its primary market among Lyran Alliance commands reinforcing the Clan border, and among independent and mercenary forces operating throughout the Chaos March. Jalastar's licensed production on Panpour supplied AFFS requirements through the Civil War and Jihad eras. Following the destruction of NETC's New Earth facility, Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium resumed manufacture on Maxwell under license from approximately 3088, sustaining supply through the Republic period and into the ilClan era under Capellan administration of the Maxwell system.","battleHistory":"~388"}},"name":"Myrmidon Medium Tank","tonnage":40,"era":"clan-inv","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~389","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["standard-ppc","turret","#0",null],"~65","~390"]}},"573":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Jalastar Aerospace","primaryFactory":"Panpour","communications":"O/P R Janxiir","targeting":"TargiTrack 717"},"components":{"engine":"Nissan 200","armor":"Jalastar Aerospace ArcShield Maxi II"},"text":{"overview":"~386","capabilities":"~387","deployment":"The Anti-Infantry variant emerged from Jalastar Aerospace's investigation of a pirate-modified Myrmidon encountered on Kolovraty during the Jihad. After securing a production license from NETC, Jalastar reconfigured the design to mount an extended-range large laser in the main turret and six light machine guns across paired sponson turrets, replacing the PPC to avoid undercutting Jalastar's own Manticore sales. The revised loadout delivers exceptional effectiveness against unarmored infantry at close range but sacrifices the long-range striking power of the original design.\\n\\nInitial AFFS adoption was slow, as procurement officers preferred direct-fire platforms. The variant found wider acceptance with Republic Armed Forces units patrolling the Confederation border, and mercenary commands attracted by the low acquisition cost became consistent buyers. Jalastar reinvested profits from Anti-Infantry production back into the Manticore line, using the revenue to reduce Manticore prices to AFFS for the following decade.","battleHistory":"~388"}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","equipmentUuid"],"$v":[["quirk-trailer-hitch",null],["quirk-poor-workmanship",null],["quirk-accurate-weapon","#0"]]},"name":"Myrmidon Medium Tank","model":"(Anti-Infantry)","tonnage":40,"sponsonTurrets":true,"sponsonMounts":true,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~389","equipment":{"$k":"~391","$v":["~392","~393",["machine-gun-light","right","#3",true,null],"~394","~395","~396",["er-large-laser","turret","#0",null,null],["~129",null,"#7",null,"machine-gun-light"]]}},"574":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"O/P R Janxiir","targeting":"TargiTrack 717"},"components":{"engine":"Nissan 200","armor":"Light FF"},"text":{"overview":"~386","capabilities":"~387","deployment":"This experimental pirate modification, first documented during raids attributed to the mercenary band Breed's Brawlers in 3075, swaps the standard particle projection cannon for a snub-nose PPC and mounts twin sponson machine gun arrays for close-range anti-infantry work. Light ferro-fibrous armor provides marginally improved protection at the cost of manufacturing complexity, an unusual investment for a field refit. The vehicle was implicated in deliberate attacks on civilian infrastructure on Kolovraty, drawing substantial bounties from both the Lyran Alliance Armed Forces and ComStar. The configuration has never been formally replicated and remains a singular example of pirate-modified Myrmidon technology.","battleHistory":"~388"}},"name":"Myrmidon Medium Tank","model":"P (Tate)","tonnage":40,"sponsonTurrets":true,"sponsonMounts":true,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":5,"armorType":"light-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~389","equipment":{"$k":"~391","$v":[["machine-gun-light","right","#0",true,null],"~392","~393",["~397","right","#3",true,null],"~394","~395","~396",["~397","left","#7",true,null],["snub-nose-ppc","turret","#8",null,null],["~129",null,"#9",null,"machine-gun-light"]]}},"575":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Trellshire Heavy Industries","primaryFactory":"Sudeten","communications":"Basix 200","targeting":"OptiSight 12"},"components":{"engine":"Starfire 180 XL","armor":"Compound Alpha"},"text":{"overview":"The Nacon Armored Scout was developed by Trellshire Heavy Industries on Sudeten in anticipation of Clan Jade Falcon's eventual desant into the Republic of the Sphere. Introduced in 3096, the design addresses a practical gap in Jade Falcon combined-arms doctrine, providing a fast and expendable reconnaissance platform capable of keeping pace with OmniMechs without consuming the logistical resources required to field additional BattleMechs. Named for a Mayan war god, the designation strikes most Inner Sphere observers as somewhat grandiose for an armored scout car, and even Clan warriors tend to view the official name as a stretch given the vehicle's limited offensive profile. Despite this skepticism, the Nacon reliably fulfills its intended role, delivering performance that has repeatedly exceeded the modest expectations accompanying its introduction to Jade Falcon touman formations.","capabilities":"The Nacon is built around speed and resilience for its weight class. The Starfire 180 XL fusion engine drives the wheeled chassis to a maximum speed of 162 kilometers per hour, allowing it to outpace most opposition and disengage from engagements it cannot win. A System 4 ATM-6 launcher forms the primary armament, offering tactical flexibility through standard, extended-range, or high-explosive ammunition loads. This adaptability makes the Nacon useful against a range of target types, from armored vehicles to dispersed infantry concentrations. Two Series 34NC Light Machine Guns supplement the ATM launcher, providing effective close-in defense against conventional infantry attempting to close with the vehicle.\\n\\nThe Nacon carries substantially more armor than most vehicles in its weight class, with Compound Alpha ferro-fibrous protection allowing it to absorb punishment that would disable lighter scouts. This survivability is critical to its bait-and-switch role, where the vehicle must attract fire and then withdraw in reasonable condition. The design's most significant weakness lies in its rear armor plating, which is noticeably thin. When the Nacon performs its intended function of drawing opponents into the weapons envelope of accompanying BattleMechs, it exposes this vulnerable aspect to any enemy that declines to pursue. Commanders consistently rate the Nacon highest in infantry screening and bait-and-switch operations, and lowest when required to hold a position without heavier fire support nearby.","deployment":"The Nacon Armored Scout remains largely in service with Clan Jade Falcon and Clan Sea Fox, though a meaningful number have entered mercenary hands through Diamond Shark trade networks. Within Jade Falcon formations, the vehicle operates in combined-arms elements alongside OmniMechs, handling forward screening, route reconnaissance, and infantry suppression ahead of advancing BattleMech stars. Sea Fox operators deploy the Nacon in comparable roles, frequently as pickets for garrison detachments or advance security for trade convoys. Mercenary operators typically assign the design to scouting and screening duties where its speed and armor resilience offset the relatively limited range of its ATM launcher.","battleHistory":"Trellshire Heavy Industries completed the Nacon's development on Sudeten in 3096, responding to Jade Falcon operational planning that anticipated large-scale conventional forces would be essential to support the Clan's push into the Republic of the Sphere. The design entered Jade Falcon service with little ceremony, viewed by many warriors as a concession to practical necessity rather than an expression of Clan combat doctrine.\\n\\nThe Nacon saw its first significant combat during the Falcons' desant, deploying across Republic Prefectures VIII and IX. On Chaffee, armored scout elements operated primarily against planetary militia formations that lacked the weapons to threaten the swift vehicle, validating the design's value in asymmetric engagements. The battle for Skye in mid-3134 proved more demanding. Nacon elements conducted continuous reconnaissance and screening operations at the vanguard of Jade Falcon advances, identifying ambush positions and drawing out Republic defenders ahead of the main force. The fighting demonstrated that the vehicle's survivability margins were genuinely sufficient for contested-space reconnaissance rather than merely low-threat scouting.\\n\\nAt Weston Heights, Nacons were committed in force to reconnaissance sweeps attempting to locate Republic troop concentrations. Units that successfully baited heavier Republic elements found them pounced upon by Jade Falcon BattleMechs before reinforcements could arrive. The majority of scout cars deployed to these operations were lost, but the intelligence they generated was considered operationally decisive by Falcon commanders.\\n\\nBy 3135, the Nacon had also begun attracting outside interest. A Third Falcon Swoop Cluster operation on Zoetermeer pitted a Binary of light BattleMechs and Nacons against Wolf garrison forces from the Twentieth Wolf Regulars. The Wolf commanders focused fire on the Falcon OmniMechs, treating the scout vehicles as secondary targets. The Nacon drivers exploited this misstep, pressing Wolf infantry and light armor while the Falcon BattleMechs maneuvered. The Falcons secured their objectives, and the engagement drew attention from Clan Sea Fox, who subsequently acquired a considerable number of Nacons for resale through mercenary networks."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"quirk-easy-to-maintain"}],"name":"Nacon Armored Scout","motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"tech":"clan","era":"early-rep","engineType":"clan_xl","cruiseMP":10,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":44,"left":21,"right":21,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~128","$v":[["atm-6","front","#0",null,null],["clan-light-machine-gun","front","#1",null,null],["clan-light-machine-gun","front","#2",null,null],["ammo-clan-light-machine-gun-standard",null,"#3",true,"clan-light-machine-gun"],["ammo-clan-atm-standard",null,"#4",null,"atm-6"],["ammo-clan-atm-standard",null,"#5",null,"atm-6"]]}},"576":{"tro":{"production":"~398","components":"~399","text":{"overview":"~400","capabilities":"The Narukami's primary armament consists of a Grizzard Model 200 Gauss Rifle paired with two Lord's Light Particle Projection Cannons, a combination designed to engage targets well beyond the effective range of most opposing armor. Two Chosatsugai no Kunai Anti-Missile Systems provide reliable point defense, protecting the crew and ammunition stores from incoming missile fire. The New Samarkand Teppeki Hardened armor, installed with integral CASE to contain potential ammunition explosions, provides substantial protection against weapons fire, with the hardened construction offering additional resistance that standard armor cannot match. A GM 270 XL fusion engine gives the 90-ton vehicle adequate ground speed for defensive operations and siege work, though the design's critics have noted it leaves the Narukami slower than many opponents would prefer.\\n\\nThe design carries notable trade-offs. The XL engine reduces the vehicle's mass but increases vulnerability to internal destruction if the engine bays are penetrated. The Gauss rifle's reliance on discrete ammunition rounds creates logistical demands that must be planned for in extended campaigns. The PPC pair generates heat that, while manageable for a combat vehicle, stresses cooling systems during sustained engagements. These factors combine to make the Narukami a capable but demanding platform that rewards careful crew training and robust supply chain management.","deployment":"The NK-1C serves as a standard fire support unit across frontline and second-line DCMS armor regiments. Attrition over decades of conflict has concentrated surviving examples in the New Samarkand Regulars and the Legions of Vega, where proximity to the manufacturing facilities facilitates resupply and maintenance. The variant is deployed most effectively in defensive lines and contested corridor operations, where its long-range weapons suite can be used to full advantage. Field commanders favor the NK-1C for siege operations and prepared defensive positions, where its modest ground speed is a lesser concern.","battleHistory":"~401"}},"quirks":"~402","name":"Narukami Heavy Tank","model":"NK-1C","tonnage":90,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"xl","armorType":"hardened","armorAllocation":"~403","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["is-ams","#1","front",null],"~160",["standard-ppc","#3","turret",null],"~404",["is-ams","#5","turret",null],"~165",["ammo-is-ams-standard","#7",null,"is-ams"],["~143","#8",null,"standard-gauss-rifle"],["~143","#9",null,"standard-gauss-rifle"]]}},"577":{"tro":{"production":"~398","components":"~399","text":{"overview":"~400","capabilities":"The BC3 chassis mounts paired Grizzard Gauss rifles in place of the single rifle of the base model, offering substantially greater firepower at long range. Two Lord's Light Particle Projection Cannons are retained, and the advanced C3 support system allows the variant to coordinate targeting data across a fire network, making each shot more precise than the individual crew could achieve alone. The Omicron IX targeting suite is supplemented with C3 slave equipment, and the vehicle's defensive suite and armor remain identical to the NK-1C.\\n\\nThe trade-offs are significant. The second Gauss rifle consumes ammunition at twice the rate of the base model, placing considerable strain on logistics trains during extended operations. The C3 system is also dependent on a functional command network, meaning the variant loses much of its tactical advantage when operating independently or when its supporting command units are destroyed. The vehicle performs best when supported by a coherent combined-arms force with reliable communications, and its value diminishes sharply when that network is disrupted.","deployment":"The NK-BC3 is deployed exclusively in units equipped with C3 fire control networks, where it functions as a frontline sniper coordinating fire with lighter, faster elements. The variant's paired Gauss rifle configuration delivers greater raw firepower than the base model, but the associated ammunition requirements have repeatedly complicated extended operations far from supply depots. The DCMS has concentrated BC3 examples in units operating along the Federated Suns border, where command networks can make full use of the variant's targeting coordination capabilities.","battleHistory":"~401"}},"quirks":"~402","name":"Narukami Heavy Tank","model":"NK-BC3","tonnage":90,"era":"late-rep","engineType":"xl","armorType":"hardened","armorAllocation":"~403","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["c3-boosted-slave","#0",null,null],["case","#1",null,null],["is-ams","#2","front",null],"~164","~404",["standard-gauss-rifle","#5","turret",null],["light-ppc","#6","turret",null],["srm-2-ios","#7","turret",null],["ammo-is-ams-standard","#8",null,"is-ams"],["ammo-is-ams-standard","#9",null,"is-ams"],["~143","#10",null,"standard-gauss-rifle"],["~143","#11",null,"standard-gauss-rifle"],"~202","~203"]}},"578":{"tro":{"production":"~405","components":"~406","text":{"overview":"~407","capabilities":"~408","deployment":"The standard Neptune entered service with the AFFS in 2950, deployed primarily to worlds along the Draconis and Capellan Marches with significant ocean coverage and established underwater command infrastructure. Early deployments concentrated on Draconis March frontier worlds, where the threat of Combine naval or amphibious incursion was most acute. The Neptune performed its designed role with competence, patrolling assigned sectors and escorting underwater command facilities. Its limited torpedo reloads required coordinating Neptune deployments in pairs or small groups to maintain continuous coverage without gaps in patrol cycles.","battleHistory":"~409"}},"name":"Neptune Submarine","tonnage":100,"motiveType":"naval-sub","hasTurret":false,"era":"late-sw-lt","armorAllocation":{"front":78,"left":58,"right":58,"rear":38,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~410","~411","~412",["large-laser","front","#3",null],["ammo-srt-standard",null,"#4","srt-6"],"~413"]}},"579":{"tro":{"production":"~405","components":"~406","text":{"overview":"~407","capabilities":"The Neptune displaces one hundred tons and is driven by a Doorman 270 Naval HyPerOx propulsion system capable of pushing the vessel to a maximum speed of 54 kilometers per hour whether submerged or running on the surface. The engine can operate in open-cycle diesel mode on the surface or switch to hydrogen peroxide combustion for deep-water operation, providing tactical flexibility across varying depth requirements. A conventional double-hull construction separates the pressure hull from the outer hull, providing structural redundancy and a rated dive depth exceeding one kilometer. Six crew members occupy the interior, and their working conditions are especially cramped given the volume consumed by machinery and ammunition storage.\\n\\nThe primary offensive system is the Sea Devastator 20-rack torpedo battery, a marinized version of long-range missile technology with excellent range and firepower against surface and subsurface targets. Two Sea Harvester six-rack torpedo launchers provide close-range punch, ensuring the Neptune can engage across the tactical envelope. A Sutel XII large laser serves as a secondary weapon for surface engagements and situations where torpedo expenditure must be conserved. The Neptune's chief tactical weakness is limited torpedo reloads, which constrains its ability to sustain prolonged combat operations without resupply support.","deployment":"Introduced by Galtor Naval Yards in 3024, the Hunter-Killer is a purpose-built anti-submarine warfare variant designed to locate and destroy opposing submarine forces rather than defend fixed underwater installations. Its refined sensor package and weapons configuration optimize performance in direct submarine-versus-submarine engagements, filling a tactical niche that the standard Neptune could only partially address. The variant saw its first operational deployments during the Fourth Succession War, defending key Federated Suns coastal systems against Combine submarine interdiction. Following the formation of the Federated Commonwealth, GNY supplied the Hunter-Killer to both AFFS and LAAF commands with active undersea interdiction requirements, with production maintained at both the Exeter and Galtor facilities.","battleHistory":"~409"}},"name":"Neptune Submarine","model":"(Hunter-Killer)","tonnage":100,"motiveType":"naval-sub","hasTurret":false,"era":"late-sw-rn","armorAllocation":{"front":66,"left":50,"right":50,"rear":42,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["lrt-15","front","#0",null],"~411",["srt-6","front","#2",null],["srt-6","front","#3",null],["srt-6","front","#4",null],["srt-4","rear","#5",null],["lrt-5","rear","#6",null],["ammo-lrt-standard",null,"#7","lrt-15"],["ammo-lrt-standard",null,"#8","lrt-15"],["ammo-lrt-standard",null,"#9","lrt-5"],["ammo-srt-standard",null,"#10","srt-4"],["ammo-srt-standard",null,"#11","srt-6"],["ammo-srt-standard",null,"#12","srt-6"],["ammo-srt-standard",null,"#13","srt-6"]]}},"580":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Lynx Shur","targeting":"Sonar Sync Tracker"},"components":"~406","text":{"overview":"~407","capabilities":"~408","deployment":"The LRT configuration replaces the Sutel XII large laser with an additional Sea Devastator 20-rack torpedo installation and expanded ammunition capacity. Where the standard Neptune relies on the laser as a secondary weapon, the LRT variant doubles down on long-range torpedo saturation. Commands deploying Neptunes as convoy escorts or operating against well-defended deep targets favored this arrangement, as the added torpedo capacity allowed more sustained engagements before the submarine required resupply. The LRT variant is common in areas where torpedo logistics were reliable enough to support its greater consumption rate, particularly along heavily defended frontier worlds.","battleHistory":"~409"}},"name":"Neptune Submarine","model":"(LRT)","tonnage":100,"motiveType":"naval-sub","hasTurret":false,"era":"late-sw-lt","armorAllocation":{"front":80,"left":60,"right":60,"rear":40,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~410","~411","~412",["lrt-20","front","#3",null],["ammo-lrt-standard",null,"#4","lrt-20"],"~413",["ammo-lrt-standard",null,"#6","lrt-20"],["ammo-lrt-standard",null,"#7","lrt-20"],["ammo-srt-standard",null,"#8","srt-6"]]}},"581":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Yelm Weapons|Yelm Weapons","primaryFactory":"New Earth|Fletcher (TH)"},"components":"~414","text":{"overview":"~415","capabilities":"~416","deployment":"The original Nightshade served as the SLDF's primary airborne electronic warfare asset, deployed in support of combined-arms formations. Small teams of Nightshades operated ahead of advancing lances and companies, disrupting enemy communications before withdrawing behind friendly lines. The design was most effective when paired with heavier units that could exploit the confusion the Guardian ECM created. The light armor and single medium laser meant pilots had no margin for error if caught in direct engagement, and operational doctrine emphasized rapid repositioning over sustained presence in contested airspace.","battleHistory":"~417"}},"name":"Nightshade ECM VTOL","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~5","era":"star-league","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":14,"armorAllocation":{"front":10,"left":6,"right":6,"rear":8,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":[["ecm-suite","#0",null],["medium-laser","#1","front"]]}},"582":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various"},"components":{"chassis":"Yelm Weapons","engine":"Vlar 160 XL"},"text":{"overview":"~415","capabilities":"~416","deployment":"The (Armor) field refit appeared during the Early Succession Wars as technicians worked to extend the operational life of surviving Nightshades. An XL fusion engine swap freed sufficient tonnage to mount a large pulse laser and add ferro-fibrous armor plating, directly addressing the original design's fragility. The conversion was performed at various facilities by units that retained both operational Nightshades and the technical skill to modify them. The rarity of surviving airframes and the difficulty of sourcing XL fusion engine components limited this refit to isolated examples scattered among the militias of the Succession States. No centralized production record exists.","battleHistory":"~417"}},"name":"Nightshade ECM VTOL","model":"(Armor)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":1,"era":"early-sw","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":14,"armorAllocation":{"front":14,"left":10,"right":10,"rear":12,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~7"},"583":{"tro":{"production":"~418","components":"~419","text":{"overview":"~415","capabilities":"~416","deployment":"Developed as a companion to the (Light PPC) model and entering production in 3070, the (LAC) variant replaced the particle cannon with a light autocannon and added target acquisition gear to support indirect fire missions. The combination proved effective when coordinating with artillery batteries, allowing the VTOL to provide rapid targeting solutions while its speed enabled it to avoid counterbattery fire. Like the (Light PPC) version, production was centered at Terra and distribution was concentrated in Word of Blake Protectorate formations. Unlike its C³i-equipped sibling, the simpler electronics made this variant more broadly useful, and production continued at Terra under the Republic of the Sphere through the Dark Age period.","battleHistory":"~417"}},"name":"Nightshade ECM VTOL","model":"(LAC)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":12,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":21,"left":15,"right":15,"rear":9,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["autocannon-light-2","front","#0",null],["is-tag","front","#1",null],["ammo-is-light-ac-2-standard",null,"#2","autocannon-light-2"]]}},"584":{"tro":{"production":"~418","components":"~419","text":{"overview":"~415","capabilities":"~416","deployment":"Produced exclusively at Terra by MSDC under Word of Blake sponsorship, the (Light PPC) variant was distributed throughout Protectorate Militia divisions and served as the Blake forces' standard airborne ECM and reconnaissance platform. Its C³i integration made it most effective coordinating with other Blakist networked units, and Blake doctrine paired small groups of Nightshades with larger combined-arms formations rather than deploying them independently. Following the Jihad, the Republic of the Sphere discontinued the variant due to the C³i requirement. Surviving examples appeared in mercenary service, typically drawn from former Protectorate militia inventories.","battleHistory":"~417"}},"name":"Nightshade ECM VTOL","model":"(Light PPC)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":12,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":13,"left":10,"right":10,"rear":9,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":[["ecm-suite","#0",null],["c3i-computer","#1",null],["light-ppc","#2","front"]]}},"585":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Yelm Weapons","primaryFactory":"Fletcher (TH)"},"components":"~414","text":{"overview":"~415","capabilities":"~416","deployment":"The Royal variant was issued to selected SLDF units as a dedicated electronic warfare platform, incorporating enhanced Star League electronics beyond the standard production specification. Deployment was concentrated in SLDF formations with the maintenance infrastructure required to support the advanced components. The Royal's production window was short, and the loss of Yelm's facilities during the Amaris Crisis meant surviving examples became irreplaceable within a generation of the Exodus. The variant's advanced systems made it a priority target for capture or salvage, and few are believed to have survived the Succession Wars in operable condition.","battleHistory":"~417"}},"name":"Nightshade ECM VTOL","model":"(Royal)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~5","era":"star-league","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":14,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":12,"left":10,"right":10,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~41","$v":[["ecm-suite","#0",null],["large-pulse-laser","#1","front"]]}},"586":{"tro":{"production":"~420","components":"~421","text":{"overview":"~422","capabilities":"~423","deployment":"The standard Nisos configuration mounts a Ceres Arms Crusher plasma rifle and a Harpoon-6 SRM launcher in a single turret, providing a balanced mix of energy suppression and missile striking power. This loadout makes the base variant well-suited for its intended role as an interdictor, capable of engaging both armored vehicles and infantry in the open. Two tons of plasma rifle ammunition and one ton of SRM reloads ensure adequate endurance for extended reconnaissance patrols. The Nisos serves primarily with CCAF scout and cavalry formations, where its speed allows it to screen advancing forces or pursue retreating enemies across varied terrain.","battleHistory":"~424"}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","equipmentUuid"],"$v":["~425","~426","~427"]},"name":"Nisos Attack WIGE","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"wige","era":"late-rep","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":9,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~428","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["plasma-rifle","turret","#0",null],"~65","~390",["~130",null,"#3","plasma-rifle"],["~130",null,"#4","plasma-rifle"]]}},"587":{"tro":{"production":"~420","components":"~421","text":{"overview":"~422","capabilities":"~423","deployment":"Introduced in 3123, the Support variant replaces the standard Nisos armament with a pair of ER medium lasers and a single Extended LRM 10 with eighteen rounds of ammunition. This configuration shifts the vehicle from a close-range interdictor to a fire-support platform capable of engaging targets at extended ranges. The ER medium lasers provide energy-based firepower that eliminates ammunition dependency for the primary weapons, while the Extended LRM gives the Support variant the ability to contribute indirect fire from well behind the front line. The trade-off is a significant reduction in short-range combat effectiveness, making the Support variant more reliant on other units for close defense.","battleHistory":"~424"}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","equipmentUuid"],"$v":["~425","~426","~427",["quirk-modular-weapons","#2"]]},"name":"Nisos Attack WIGE","model":"(Support)","tonnage":35,"motiveType":"wige","era":"late-rep","engineType":"xl","cruiseMP":9,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~428","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["er-medium-laser","turret","#0",null],"~258",["extended-lrm-10","turret","#2",null],["~429",null,"#3","extended-lrm-10"],["~429",null,"#4","extended-lrm-10"]]}},"588":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Various","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Consolidated BMR 6","targeting":"TRTTS Mark II"},"components":{"engine":"Fusion 140","armor":"~430"},"text":{"overview":"The Odin Scout Tank is a lightweight Clan reconnaissance vehicle introduced in 2844. Weighing only twenty tons, the wheeled tank was designed from the outset as a dedicated intelligence-gathering platform rather than a combat vehicle. Its sophisticated sensor suite and impressive top speed made it one of the premier scouting assets available to any Clan's touman. Production spread across nearly every Clan's homeworld industrial base, evidence of the design's universal appeal and straightforward manufacturing requirements.\\n\\nDespite the Clans' well-documented disdain for conventional vehicles, the Odin carved out a lasting niche by excelling in a role that most warriors considered beneath them. Few other vehicles achieved such widespread adoption among so many different Clans, and the design's longevity across centuries of service speaks to its fundamental soundness as a reconnaissance platform.","capabilities":"~431","deployment":"The base Odin serves as the standard reconnaissance variant across Clan military forces. Produced at numerous facilities throughout the Kerensky Cluster and later at Inner Sphere factories, it remains one of the most widely distributed scout vehicles in any Clan touman. Contrary to Clan tradition, the two-person crew often trains together and serves as a team throughout their military career, a strategy required by the high level of coordination needed to effectively operate the vehicle's complex sensor systems while maneuvering at high speed.","battleHistory":"~432"}},"name":"Odin Scout Tank","motiveType":"wheeled","tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~433","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~39",["er-small-laser-clan","#1","front",null],["~42","#2","turret",null],["~42","#3","turret",null],["clan-streak-srm-2","#4","turret",null],["~54","#5",null,"clan-streak-srm-2"]]}},"589":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Joint Equipment Systems","primaryFactory":"Alshain","communications":"Consolidated BMR 6","targeting":"TRTTS Mark II"},"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell 140","armor":"~430"},"text":{"overview":"The Odin Scout Tank is a lightweight Clan reconnaissance vehicle introduced in 2844. Weighing only twenty tons, the wheeled tank was designed from the outset as a dedicated intelligence-gathering platform rather than a combat vehicle. Its sophisticated sensor suite and impressive top speed made it one of the premier scouting assets available to any Clan's touman. Production spread across nearly every Clan's homeworld industrial base, a reflection of the design's universal appeal and straightforward manufacturing requirements.\\n\\nDespite the Clans' well-documented disdain for conventional vehicles, the Odin carved out a lasting niche by excelling in a role that most warriors considered beneath them. Few other vehicles achieved such widespread adoption among so many different Clans, and the design's longevity across centuries of service speaks to its fundamental soundness as a reconnaissance platform.","capabilities":"~431","deployment":"The Spotter variant stands as a significant reimagining of the Odin's role, shifting the vehicle from active reconnaissance to dedicated artillery support and target designation. Developed for the Kungsarme in 3071, this variant replaces the fusion engine with a fuel cell powerplant while maintaining the original top speed. The freed weight accommodates an ECM suite, target acquisition gear, and a Narc missile beacon, transforming the Odin into a potent force multiplier for indirect fire assets.\\n\\nAll original weaponry was stripped in favor of a pair of anti-personnel Gauss rifles, providing minimal self-defense capability. The Spotter's reliance on friendly fire support made it unappealing to Clan warriors, and no Clans expressed interest in the design despite its Inner Sphere origins at Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain.","battleHistory":"~432"}},"name":"Odin Scout Tank","model":"(Spotter)","motiveType":"wheeled","tech":"clan","era":"jihad","engineType":"compact","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~433","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":["~39",["clan-ecm-system","#1",null,null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#2","turret",null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#3","turret",null],["clan-tag","#4","turret",null],["clan-narc","#5","turret",null],["~40","#6",null,"ap-gauss-rifle"],["~434","#7",null,"clan-narc"],["~434","#8",null,"clan-narc"]]}},"590":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Grumman Amalgamated|Kallon Weapon Industries","primaryFactory":"Shiro III|Nanking","communications":"Easy Talk-2","targeting":"~435"},"components":"~436","text":{"overview":"~437","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"Though originally built in Capellan space, the Ontos was highly favored by House Marik after the League captured Shiro III during the Succession Wars. After the annexation of Nanking by the Federated Commonwealth, the vehicle formed the backbone of many Free Worlds League heavy tank regiments. The larger-than-average vehicle formations favored by the Marik military gave the heavy tanks plenty of support to offset their short range.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","tonnage":95,"era":"early-sw","armorAllocation":{"front":28,"left":24,"right":24,"rear":24,"turret":28,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["lrm-5","turret","#0",null],["lrm-5","turret","#1",null],"~440","~441","~442","~443","~444","~445","~446","~447","~280"]}},"591":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Grumman Amalgamated","primaryFactory":"Shiro III","communications":"Easy Talk","targeting":"GuideRite with Laser Coordination Link and Artemis FCS"},"components":"~448","text":{"overview":"~449","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"Produced exclusively at Grumman's Shiro III facility, the upgraded Ontos entered service during the Clan Invasion era. Deployed with Marik, Capellan, Steiner, and Davion units, it also found its way to mercenary commands and the Touman of Clan Nova Cat. Despite improvements, the older variants remained common alongside the upgrade across multiple factions.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","model":"(3053 Upgrade)","tonnage":95,"era":"clan-inv","engineType":"ice","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~450","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],"~451","~452","~453","~201",["medium-pulse-laser","#5","turret",null],["medium-pulse-laser","#6","turret",null],["medium-pulse-laser","#7","turret",null],["lrm-15-artemis-iv","#8","turret",null],["srm-4-artemis-iv","#9","turret",null],["srm-6-artemis-iv","#10","turret",null],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv","#11",null,"lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv","#12",null,"lrm-15-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-srm-artemis-iv","#13",null,"srm-6-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-srm-artemis-iv","#14",null,"srm-4-artemis-iv"]]}},"592":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Grumman Amalgamated|Grumman Amalgamated","primaryFactory":"Shiro III|Trellisane"},"components":{"engine":"285 XL Fusion","armor":"Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"~437","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"Produced at Grumman's facilities on Shiro III and a new plant on Trellisane within the Wolf Empire, the Chemical Laser variant represents the latest evolution of the Ontos. Forces expecting the traditional medium laser battery face a significant surprise from both the extended range and hitting power of the chemical laser armament.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","model":"(Chemical Laser)","tonnage":95,"tech":"mis","era":"dark-ages","engineType":"xl","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorTechBase":"is","armorAllocation":"~450","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["~454","#1","turret",null],["~454","#2","turret",null],["~454","#3","turret",null],["~454","#4","turret",null],["~454","#5","turret",null],["~454","#6","turret",null],["~454","#7","turret",null],["~454","#8","turret",null],["lrm-5","#9","turret",null],["lrm-5","#10","turret",null],["ammo-lrm-standard","#11",null,"lrm-5"],["~455","#12",null,"~454"],["~455","#13",null,"~454"],["~455","#14",null,"~454"],["~455","#15",null,"~454"],["~455","#16",null,"~454"],["~455","#17",null,"~454"],["~455","#18",null,"~454"],["~455","#19",null,"~454"]]}},"593":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Kallon Weapon Industries|Refit - Various","primaryFactory":"Nanking|Various","communications":"Easy Talk-2","targeting":"~435"},"components":{"engine":"285 Fusion","armor":"~456"},"text":{"overview":"~457","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"The fusion-powered Ontos saw limited initial production, as the rarity and expense of fusion engines during the Succession Wars restricted distribution. Individual units and well-supplied garrison commands sometimes attempted the conversion, with mixed results. Kallon's Nanking facility began dedicated production of the Fusion variant after upgrading its lines in 3059, expanding availability significantly.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","model":"(Fusion)","tonnage":95,"era":"early-sw","engineType":"ice","armorAllocation":{"front":40,"left":35,"right":35,"rear":34,"turret":40,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["medium-laser","turret","#0",null],["medium-laser","turret","#1",null],"~440","~441","~442","~443","~444","~445","~446","~447",["lrm-10","turret","#10",null],["lrm-10","turret","#11",null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#12","lrm-10"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#13","lrm-10"]]}},"594":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Grumman Amalgamated","primaryFactory":"Shiro III","communications":"Easy Talk","targeting":"~458"},"components":"~448","text":{"overview":"~437","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"Grumman initially restricted sales of the Light Gauss variant to the Capellan Confederation and the Free Worlds League. Commercial interest and the outbreak of war loosened these restrictions over time. The variant filled an armored sniper role, harassing and softening enemy formations before engagement, a significant departure from the original Ontos doctrine.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","model":"(Light Gauss)","tonnage":95,"era":"civil-war","engineType":"ice","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~450","equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":["~353","~354",["gauss-rifle-light","turret","#2",null],["er-medium-laser","turret","#3",null],["er-medium-laser","turret","#4",null],"~355",["~127",null,"#6","gauss-rifle-light"],["~127",null,"#7","gauss-rifle-light"],["~127",null,"#8","gauss-rifle-light"]]}},"595":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Easy Talk-2","targeting":"~435"},"components":"~436","text":{"overview":"~459","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"The LRM variant was a common field modification throughout the Succession Wars, replacing the energy weapon array with a more conventional missile loadout. Crews who preferred engaging at range or operating in combined-arms formations often favored this conversion, which traded the Ontos's signature sustained-fire capability for greater striking power at distance.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","model":"(LRM)","tonnage":95,"era":"early-sw","armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":26,"right":26,"rear":24,"turret":30,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["lrm-20","turret","#0",null],["lrm-20","turret","#1",null],["lrm-10","turret","#2",null],["lrm-10","turret","#3",null],"~95","~96","~97",["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#7","lrm-20"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#8","lrm-20"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#9","lrm-10"]]}},"596":{"tro":{"production":"~460","components":"~448","text":{"overview":"~457","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"Developed during the Jihad, the MML variant combined long-range and short-range missile capabilities alongside residual Gauss armament in a versatile package. Both factory-produced models from Grumman's Shiro III facility and field refit conversions of existing Light Gauss Ontos tanks saw deployment across multiple factions during the chaotic fighting of that era and beyond.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","model":"(MML)","tonnage":95,"era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~450","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],["gauss-rifle-light","#1","turret",null],["gauss-rifle-light","#2","turret",null],["mml-7","#3","turret",null],["mml-7","#4","turret",null],"~262","~461",["~127","#7",null,"gauss-rifle-light"],["~127","#8",null,"gauss-rifle-light"],["~127","#9",null,"gauss-rifle-light"],["~127","#10",null,"gauss-rifle-light"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#11",null,"mml-7"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#12",null,"mml-7"],["ammo-srm-standard","#13",null,"mml-7"]]}},"597":{"tro":{"production":"~460","components":"~448","text":{"overview":"~459","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"The sealed Ontos was designed for amphibious and hostile-environment operations during the Jihad. It saw service in littoral combat zones and on worlds where atmospheric conditions required environmental protection. Grumman produced the variant at Shiro III, while field conversion kits allowed existing Ontos tanks to be sealed for specific deployments.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","model":"(Sealed)","tonnage":95,"chassisModifications":"~462","era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~450","equipment":{"$k":"~22","$v":[["case","#0",null,null],"~451","~452","~453","~201","~262","~461",["mml-7-artemis-iv","#7","turret",null],["mml-7-artemis-iv","#8","turret",null],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv","#9",null,"mml-7-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-lrm-artemis-iv","#10",null,"mml-7-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-srm-artemis-iv","#11",null,"mml-7-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-srm-artemis-iv","#12",null,"mml-7-artemis-iv"],["ammo-is-srm-artemis-iv","#13",null,"mml-7-artemis-iv"]]}},"598":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Quikscell Company","primaryFactory":"Richvale","communications":"Irian E.A.R.","targeting":"Wasat Aggressor"},"components":{"engine":"Pitban 285 Fusion","armor":"Lexington Ltd. High Grade Ferro-Fibrous"},"text":{"overview":"~449","capabilities":"~438","deployment":"The Ontos HEAT is deployed in small numbers wherever local environmental conditions are a primary concern. Notable postings include worlds heavily damaged during the Jihad, such as Hesperus II and Tharkad. Quikscell has restricted most sales to the Lyran Commonwealth, though the Capellan Confederation and Free Worlds League have also acquired limited numbers.","battleHistory":"~439"}},"name":"Ontos Heavy Tank","model":"HEAT","tonnage":95,"chassisModifications":"~462","era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":40,"left":37,"right":37,"rear":35,"turret":30,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~53","$v":[["combat-vehicle-escape-pod","rear","#0",null],["er-large-laser","turret","#1",null],"~440","~441","~442",["enhanced-lrm-15","turret","#5",null],["~169",null,"#6","enhanced-lrm-15"],["~169",null,"#7","enhanced-lrm-15"]]}},"599":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Manufacturing Plant SFF-NG3","primaryFactory":"Ingersoll","communications":"Khan-series (Type 2)","targeting":"Mark 12 IHADS"},"components":{"engine":"Fusion 100 XL","armor":"Gamma Special Reflective"},"text":{"overview":"The Onuris Attack VTOL entered production in 3145 at Manufacturing Plant SFF-NG3 on Ingersoll, a Clan Sea Fox facility operating within Capellan Confederation space. Conceived during the post-Blackout rearmament, the design emerged from widespread recognition that combat vehicles had proven their worth in the conflicts sweeping the Inner Sphere. Sea Fox merchants and warriors saw an opportunity to create a more capable combat platform from the proven Anhur Transport chassis, converting its generous cargo bay into flexible pod space. The result was a versatile OmniVehicle capable of filling roles that previously required multiple dedicated airframes. The Onuris quickly drew interest from buyers across the Sphere, and its combination of speed, adaptability, and Clan-grade equipment made it a popular addition to combined-arms formations seeking reliable aerial fire support.","capabilities":"The Onuris retains the Anhur's Fusion 100 XL engine, providing a cruising speed of 86 kilometers per hour. All configurations mount a VTOL jet booster that pushes the maximum speed to 172 kilometers per hour, delivering the burst acceleration needed for hit-and-run tactics and rapid battlefield disengagement. This combination of sustained cruise performance and sprint capability makes the Onuris exceptionally difficult to pin down.\\n\\nThe armor package was upgraded from the Anhur's ferro-fibrous plating to Gamma Special Reflective armor, providing improved protection against energy weapons. Overall coverage was increased to better suit a vehicle expected to operate in contested airspace rather than behind the front lines. The dual rotor arrangement provides stable flight characteristics but remains the design's most significant vulnerability, as with all VTOLs.\\n\\nFourteen tons of pod space freed by removing the Anhur's cargo bay accommodate loadouts ranging from plasma cannons and missile racks to autocannons and laser arrays. Standard configurations cover roles from close-range striker to long-range sniper to dedicated missile platform, giving formation commanders considerable tactical flexibility from a single airframe. User-developed configurations that proved themselves in combat were eventually added to the standard production catalog. Despite this versatility, the Onuris is still a 30-ton VTOL and cannot absorb sustained fire from dedicated anti-air assets.","deployment":"Configuration A replaces the Prime's close-range armament with an ER PPC and four AP Gauss Rifles sharing a single ton of ammunition, supported by five additional heat sinks. This transforms the Onuris into a long-range sniper platform capable of engaging targets well outside return fire range. The AP Gauss Rifles provide effective point defense against infantry and battle armor that might threaten the VTOL during low-altitude operations.","battleHistory":"The Onuris saw its first combat deployment during Task Force Navarre's assault on the Capellan Confederation. Typical deployments organized a Star with two Points each of Prime and A configurations alongside a Point of B configuration, providing overlapping fields of fire that were difficult for defenders to counter. During strikes on Pleione, the Capellan Home Guard commander's decision to disregard the approaching Sea Fox force proved costly. The speed of the Onurises allowed them to bypass most Home Guard positions while Sea Fox transports dropped off Elementals to raid stockpiles and destroy key objectives. A Star of Onurises provided overwatch, easily dispatching scouts and quick response forces. When the time came to withdraw, the upgraded armor allowed the VTOLs to absorb covering fire while their speed ensured extraction before heavier Home Guard elements could respond.\\n\\nA Star of Onurises later acquired by Clan Wolf equipped an experimental Nova in the Ninth Wolf Cavalry. The Wolf Onurises played a key role in the Cluster that drove the battered Tenth Principes into the Consecrated Desert on Shiloh. Various configurations were employed, but two Points of E variants outperformed the rest of the Star. These Onurises used their jet boosters to run down Republic scouts, crippled them with pulse laser fire, and then destroyed the immobilized vehicles with ATM barrages. Their superior range allowed them to avoid return fire from slower Republic formations until the Republic forces regrouped in strength, compelling the Wolf VTOLs to fall back.\\n\\nDuring a Trial of Possession on Satalice, a Point of Onurises helped a Trinary of Clan Hell's Horses' Thirty-Ninth Mechanized Assault Cluster secure a Binary of freshly built Kodiaks. Operating as guardians alongside Anhurs carrying Elementals, the Onurises surprised security forces that had expected only lightly armed transports. The Horses' VTOLs and Elementals carved through smaller, slower opposing units, destroying triple their tonnage before better-armored reinforcements arrived. By that point the main body of the Trinary had already defeated its counterparts, and the remaining Horses made short work of the last defenders."}},"name":"Onuris Attack VTOL","model":"A","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"tech":"clan","era":"dark-ages","engineType":"clan_xl","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"laser-reflective","armorAllocation":{"front":31,"left":20,"right":20,"rear":15,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~144","$v":[["vtol-jet-booster","#0",null,null,null],["er-ppc-clan","#1","front",true,null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#2","front",true,null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#3","front",true,null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#4","front",true,null],["ap-gauss-rifle","#5","front",true,null],["~40","#6",null,null,"ap-gauss-rifle"]]}}}`)};export{e as default};